window.AMG_ASSESSMENTS={"track":"health","version":1,"extraQuestions":[{"lesson":"19","prompt":"An in-network lab bills $600. Its contract allows $400. Mina has $100 left on her deductible and owes 20% coinsurance after that. There is no copay, and she is below her out-of-pocket limit. What does Mina owe?","options":["$160","$80","$200","$180"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Start with the $400 allowed amount. Mina pays the remaining $100 deductible, then 20% of the remaining $300, or $60. Her share is $160. The lab’s higher billed price is not used for this in-network claim.","id":"HM-lab-share","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov — Glossary of Health Coverage and Medical Terms","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/SBC-GLOSSARY/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"19","prompt":"Marco has met his deductible and is $70 short of his plan’s annual in-network out-of-pocket limit. His coinsurance on a covered in-network scan would normally be $140. What happens to his bill for that scan?","options":["He pays nothing because he already met his deductible.","He pays $70 plus another $70 that counts toward next year.","He pays $140 because the limit only applies to deductibles.","He pays $70; the plan pays the rest of the allowed amount."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Only $70 remains before Marco reaches his limit. For this covered in-network service, the plan pays the rest of the allowed amount. Meeting his deductible alone would not have stopped coinsurance.","id":"HM-remaining-limit","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov — Glossary of Health Coverage and Medical Terms","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/SBC-GLOSSARY/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"18","prompt":"After a covered three-day hospital stay, Eva receives a $4,800 bill. Her supplemental policy pays $150 per covered day, with no other benefit for this stay. What should she expect from that policy?","options":["The amount left over after her medical deductible","$450, based on the number of covered days","$4,800, based on the hospital bill","$150, regardless of how long she stayed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The policy promises a daily dollar amount. Three covered days at $150 each pay $450. That payment does not turn the supplemental policy into coverage for the full hospital bill.","id":"HM-drill-4","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"CMS — Hospital indemnity coverage","url":"https://www.cms.gov/cciio/resources/fact-sheets-and-faqs/aca_implementation_faqs11"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"29","prompt":"Rae compares two disability policies. Both pay the same monthly benefit for up to two years. One has a 30-day elimination period and the other has a 90-day elimination period. Which difference matters if her savings cover only six weeks?","options":["She would need to cover more time without benefits under the 90-day policy.","Neither plan can begin until she has been disabled for two years.","The 30-day policy stops paying after the first month.","The 90-day policy provides three times the monthly benefit."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The elimination period is time Rae must get through before disability benefits can begin. A longer wait can leave her without income after her savings run out. It does not change the stated monthly amount or two-year benefit period.","id":"HM-drill-9","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Insurance Administration — Disability Insurance","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/disability-insurance.aspx"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"46","prompt":"A clinic has confirmed that an employer plan pays first and Medicare pays second for Len’s visit. The employer plan processes the claim and leaves a balance. What should the clinic tell Len?","options":["The clinic must reverse the claim and bill Medicare as the primary payer.","Len can choose which payer is primary after he sees the bill.","Medicare considers the remaining charges under its own rules; he may still owe part of the bill.","Medicare must pay every dollar left by the employer plan."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Coordination of benefits sets the order of payment. The second payer looks at what remains and applies its own coverage rules. A balance on the first payer’s statement is not a promise that Medicare will pay all of it.","id":"HM-drill-34","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare — How Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/coordination"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: how Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/who-pays-first"},{"title":"Medicare: skilled nursing facility care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care"},{"title":"Maryland Department of Health: Medicaid","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Pages/home.aspx"}]}],"domains":["Maryland regulation","General insurance","Health insurance basics","Individual health policy provisions","Disability income","Medical plans","Group health insurance","Dental insurance","Senior citizens and special needs","Federal tax considerations"],"blueprint":[24,7,9,6,7,11,4,1,7,4],"forms":[{"id":"A","title":"Health Mock Exam A","minutes":105,"questions":[{"lesson":"49","prompt":"A Baltimore agency hires a receptionist who has no producer license. Which assignment can she accept?","options":["Explain why the caller needs the higher benefit limit","Schedule a meeting with the licensed producer","Recommend replacing the caller’s current health policy","Discuss which policy exclusions the caller should accept"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scheduling is administrative. Recommending coverage or discussing specific policy terms with a prospective buyer crosses into licensed activity.","id":"HM-A-1-1","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–103, license required and exceptions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-103"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–104 and §10–109, qualifications and examination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-104"},{"title":"MIA Bulletin 24-19, repeal of prelicensing education","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Pages/Bulletins/24-19-Repealing-the-Prelicensing-Education-and-Experience-Requirements.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"05","prompt":"A health contract responds only if a covered expense occurs and required proof is provided. Which contract characteristic explains this?","options":["Aleatory","Adhesion","Unilateral","Conditional"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Conditions must be satisfied before a promised insurance benefit becomes payable. A signed contract alone does not make every expense payable.","id":"HM-A-2-4","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Article §12–102, rescission for material misrepresentation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=12-102"}]},{"lesson":"26","prompt":"A Marketplace policyholder has met the in-network out-of-pocket maximum for the plan year. What should the plan generally pay for the next covered in-network service?","options":["All of its covered in-network cost for the rest of the plan year","Only 80 percent until next month","All premiums for the remaining months","No benefit until another deductible is met"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Once applicable cost sharing reaches the maximum, the plan pays covered in-network costs for the rest of that plan year. Premiums remain separate.","id":"HM-A-6-6","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: essential health benefits","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/what-marketplace-plans-cover/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: grandfathered plans","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/health-care-law-protections/grandfathered-plans/"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"A producer invents a story that a competing insurer cannot pay claims and repeats it to customers. What is the most specific concern?","options":["A permissible comparison of filed rates","Disclosure of a policy exclusion","Defamation of the competing insurer","Adverse selection by the customer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"False statements calculated to injure an insurer’s reputation or business may be defamatory. A factual comparison of coverage is different.","id":"HM-A-1-24","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"04","prompt":"A carrier repeatedly lets its producer deliver policies in its name and gives customers reason to believe he has that authority. What can a customer’s reasonable belief establish?","options":["A transfer of policy ownership","Apparent authority","A new statutory license","An independent contract drafted by the customer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Apparent authority arises from the principal’s conduct toward third parties. An agent’s unsupported claim alone is insufficient.","id":"HM-A-2-8","domain":1,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–101 definitions of insurance producer","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-101"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"50","prompt":"A resident producer completed all required ethics hours but let her license expire. A client wants to enroll today. What should she do first?","options":["Rely on the old license during a premium grace period","Accept the application while the renewal is pending","Take the premium under the agency license alone","Confirm her license has been reinstated"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Continuing education is one renewal requirement. A producer needs an active license when conducting a transaction that requires it.","id":"HM-A-1-3","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–116, continuing education","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-116"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–115, renewal and reinstatement","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-115"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, reporting of criminal and administrative actions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"}]},{"lesson":"40","prompt":"A dentist treats the gums and supporting bone around an otherwise intact tooth. Which dental specialty most directly corresponds to that treatment?","options":["Orthodontics","Endodontics","Periodontics","Prosthodontics"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Periodontics deals with the structures supporting teeth. Endodontics deals with the interior of a tooth.","id":"HM-A-8-2","domain":7,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: dental coverage in the marketplace","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/dental-coverage/"}]},{"lesson":"43","prompt":"An eligible Medicare beneficiary joins a private plan to receive covered Part A and Part B services through that plan. What has she chosen?","options":["A standalone Part D plan only","Medicare Advantage","A Medigap-only arrangement","Medicaid spend-down"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Medicare Advantage is the private-plan route for receiving Medicare benefits. A standalone drug plan alone does not deliver Part A and Part B services.","id":"HM-A-9-2","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare Advantage plans","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: drug coverage (Part D)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: Part D late enrollment penalty","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties"}]},{"lesson":"33","prompt":"An architecture firm wants money to hire a temporary replacement if its top designer is disabled. The firm will own the coverage and receive the benefit. Which policy fits?","options":["Key person disability","Individual residual disability paid to the designer","Business overhead coverage solely for the designer’s family bills","Disability buy-sell funding"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Key person coverage protects the business from the financial effect of a key worker’s disability, including replacement needs.","id":"HM-A-5-5","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics: disability insurance in employee benefits","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ebs/"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: business disability coverage","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"32","prompt":"A carrier limits the new disability benefit after learning that a client already has substantial group coverage. Which risk is the limit addressing?","options":["The length of the new plan’s elimination period","A difference between the two policies’ occupation definitions","Whether the benefit period extends beyond normal retirement age","Overinsurance relative to earned income"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Participation limits consider existing benefits so disability income does not replace an excessive share of normal earnings.","id":"HM-A-5-6","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"A producer offers to refund part of a commission to one buyer if she signs that day. The refund is absent from the policy. What issue does this raise?","options":["Risk classification","Rebating","An extended grace period","Replacement notice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Giving an unlisted financial benefit to induce a purchase is a rebate. The source of the money does not make the practice permissible.","id":"HM-A-1-14","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"02","prompt":"A company pays routine employee medical claims from its own funds and buys protection only for unusually large claims. How does it handle the routine claims?","options":["Risk avoidance","Reinsurance of another insurer","Risk transfer to the employees","Risk retention"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The employer retains ordinary losses when it pays them itself. Its separate protection transfers only the portion described in the stop-loss contract.","id":"HM-A-2-2","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Society of Actuaries — Risk and Insurance","url":"https://www.soa.org/globalassets/assets/files/edu/P-21-05.pdf"},{"title":"NAIC: A Regulator’s Introduction to the Insurance Industry","url":"https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/prod_serv_marketreg_rii_zb.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"A claimant sends timely notice, but the insurer fails to furnish its claim forms within the required 15 days. What may the claimant ordinarily do?","options":["Wait for the insurer’s form even beyond the proof deadline","Send written particulars of the covered loss","Restart the claim by giving a second notice after the filing period ends","Treat the initial notice alone as complete proof in every case"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The claim-forms provision allows written particulars of the loss when the insurer fails to supply forms in time.","id":"HM-A-4-3","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"A producer plans to place an ordinary health policy with a carrier he has never used. What should he verify about the carrier first?","options":["The carrier’s authorization in its state of domicile alone","The carrier’s Maryland certificate of authority","The agency’s contract to distribute products elsewhere","The producer’s own current Maryland health license"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An individual producer license does not authorize an insurer to operate. The carrier must have the appropriate authority to write the business.","id":"HM-A-1-13","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"22","prompt":"A PPO member chooses a nonparticipating physician for a nonemergency visit. What should she check before the visit?","options":["Only the in-network office copayment","Whether the PPO automatically converts to an HMO","The out-of-network benefit, deductible, allowed amount and possible balance bill","Whether her annual premium counts toward the visit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PPOs often offer some out-of-network coverage on different terms. The patient may owe more than the in-network cost sharing.","id":"HM-A-6-3","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary: PPO and POS","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"TRICARE plans","url":"https://www.tricare.mil/Plans"}]},{"lesson":"46","prompt":"A Medicare beneficiary needs ongoing assistance with dressing and bathing but no skilled treatment. What distinction matters when evaluating payment?","options":["Custodial help alone generally is not covered by Medicare as long-term care","Every nursing home stay is covered for exactly 100 days","A Medigap policy ordinarily pays for unlimited custodial care","Part D covers all personal care after age 65"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Medicare’s limited skilled care benefit is different from ongoing custodial care. Medicaid or private long-term care coverage may apply if requirements are met.","id":"HM-A-9-7","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/who-pays-first"},{"title":"Medicare: skilled nursing facility care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care"},{"title":"Maryland Department of Health: Medicaid","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Pages/home.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"A business buys overhead expense disability coverage to reimburse eligible rent and payroll while its owner is disabled. What is the usual business tax treatment?","options":["Premiums are generally deductible and benefits received by the business are generally taxable","Premiums buy a personal after-tax policy for the owner","Premiums and benefits are both always excluded from the books","Every benefit is a nontaxable capital gain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Business overhead insurance pays business expenses. Its usual tax treatment differs from an owner’s personally funded disability income policy.","id":"HM-A-10-5","domain":9,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"10","prompt":"A buyer reads “guaranteed renewable” in a brochure and assumes the insurer can never change any premium. What should the producer explain?","options":["The buyer may freely choose the renewal premium","Applicable class premiums may change under a guaranteed-renewable contract","The phrase refers only to the policy’s first month","Every guaranteed-renewable policy is a group policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guaranteed renewal protects continuation under the contract. A noncancelable rate guarantee is a separate feature.","id":"HM-A-3-9","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202, misrepresentation and false advertising","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://www.mdlifega.org/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 9, Subtitle 4, Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=9-403"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"A qualifying self-employed person wants to claim the self-employed health insurance deduction for a month when she was eligible for subsidized coverage through her spouse’s employer. Which issue applies?","options":["Every self-employed premium is deductible without conditions","Declining the employer plan always preserves the deduction","The premium automatically becomes a business overhead disability benefit","Eligibility for that subsidized employer plan can disallow the deduction for that month"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The self-employed deduction has eligibility restrictions, including months in which subsidized employer coverage was available through the taxpayer or spouse.","id":"HM-A-10-4","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"47","prompt":"A tax-qualified long-term care policy uses the federal activities-of-daily-living trigger. Which pair consists of two recognized daily activities?","options":["Bathing and transferring","Driving and shopping","Banking and reading","Cleaning and cooking"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bathing and transferring are among the six activities. Ordinary household tasks can be important but are not on that statutory list.","id":"HM-A-9-5","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Long-Term Care Insurance Partnership Program","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/LongTermCarePartnership.aspx"},{"title":"IRS Publication 502, long-term care premiums and per diem limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"Maryland Medicaid: long-term services and supports","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/longtermcare/Pages/Home.aspx"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: long-term care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"A producer omits a waiting period from a comparison to persuade a customer to cancel her current policy. What practice does this illustrate?","options":["Subrogation","Coordination of benefits","Twisting","Reinsurance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An incomplete or misleading comparison used to induce a policy change is twisting. A complete, accurate comparison can support a lawful replacement.","id":"HM-A-1-7","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"23","prompt":"A patient covered by two medical plans assumes the secondary plan must pay every dollar the primary plan leaves. What determines the actual secondary payment?","options":["The secondary plan’s coordination provisions and covered allowable expense","The amount of premium paid to the primary plan","The member’s selection after both claims are processed","Whichever provider billed the higher charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Coordination determines payment order and limits. Secondary coverage does not guarantee payment of all deductibles, exclusions or billed amounts.","id":"HM-A-6-4","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (model 120)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–10A, utilization review and grievances","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-10A-01"}]},{"lesson":"18","prompt":"A policy charges a flat $35 for an eligible office visit. What form of cost sharing is this?","options":["A deductible","Coinsurance","A copayment","A balance bill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A copayment is a specified dollar charge for a covered service. Coinsurance is ordinarily expressed as a percentage.","id":"HM-A-6-7","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"Two violations are established. Under Maryland Insurance Article §10-126, what is the maximum total of the per-violation penalties if the maximum applies to both?","options":["$100,000","$10,000","$2,500","$5,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The statutory maximum is $5,000 per violation. Two violations at that maximum total $10,000 before any separate restitution.","id":"HM-A-1-21","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"08","prompt":"A critical illness policy pays a lump sum after a covered diagnosis. Which expense can the insured generally use the cash for?","options":["Only charges approved by a network dentist","Only a Medicare deductible","Rent during treatment","Only the hospital’s final itemized bill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A lump-sum benefit can generally be used as the insured chooses. Eligibility still depends on the conditions and diagnoses listed in the policy.","id":"HM-A-3-10","domain":2,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1301 et seq., short-term limited duration insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1301"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"42 U.S.C. §300gg–91(c), categories of excepted benefits","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t42/s300gg-91/"}]},{"lesson":"23","prompt":"A health plan reviews whether a planned admission is medically necessary before it occurs. What type of utilization review is this?","options":["Retrospective review","Concurrent review","Coordination of benefits","Prospective review"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Prospective review occurs before the service. Concurrent review takes place during care and retrospective review follows it.","id":"HM-A-6-11","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (model 120)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–10A, utilization review and grievances","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-10A-01"}]},{"lesson":"17","prompt":"The buyer wants both continued individual disability coverage and a premium that the insurer cannot increase during the stated guarantee period. Which provision most closely fits?","options":["Optionally renewable","Conditionally renewable","Noncancelable","Cancelable on notice"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A noncancelable provision protects renewal and the contractual premium during its stated period when required premiums are paid.","id":"HM-A-4-6","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, interest on late claim payments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"A worker pays the entire premium on an individual disability income policy with after-tax dollars. Under the usual federal rule, how are the resulting disability benefits treated?","options":["Taxable because they replace taxable wages","Generally tax free to the insured","Taxable up to the premiums the insured paid","Tax free only to the extent of incurred medical bills"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When the individual pays the premium with after-tax funds, disability income benefits are generally excluded from taxable income.","id":"HM-A-10-1","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"37","prompt":"A group plan offers new employees a defined period to enroll after becoming eligible. An employee who wants coverage misses that period and has no qualifying event. Which opportunity normally comes next?","options":["Immediate retroactive enrollment on the original eligibility date","An automatic new initial eligibility period each month","An individual conversion window for coverage never elected","The next available open enrollment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A plan can limit enrollment to specified windows. A qualifying special enrollment event would be a separate basis for enrollment.","id":"HM-A-7-3","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: special enrollment","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/portability"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependent coverage","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"}]},{"lesson":"29","prompt":"A contract has a 60-day elimination period and pays for qualifying days after it. A continuous disability lasts 75 days. How many days fall after the waiting period?","options":["15","60","75","135"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first 60 days satisfy the elimination period. The remaining 15 days can be payable if the other conditions are met.","id":"HM-A-5-2","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"44","prompt":"When does the usual one-time federal six-month Medigap open enrollment period begin for a person age 65 or older?","options":["The month after any Medigap policy expires","Every January regardless of age or enrollment","The first month a prescription is filled","The first month the person is both at least 65 and enrolled in Part B"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both age and Part B enrollment matter. The federal six-month Medigap period differs from Medicare’s annual plan election period.","id":"HM-A-9-3","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how to compare Medigap policies","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits"},{"title":"Choosing a Medigap Policy (CMS guide)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/publications/02110-medigap-guide-health-insurance.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"10","prompt":"Which sales statement improperly uses the Maryland guaranty corporation?","options":["“Buy this policy because the guaranty corporation stands behind it.”","“Here is the company’s outline of coverage.”","“The policy lists when benefits begin.”","“Please read the summary notice enclosed with your policy.”"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A producer may deliver a required notice. Using the guaranty corporation’s protection as an inducement to buy is prohibited.","id":"HM-A-1-10","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202, misrepresentation and false advertising","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://www.mdlifega.org/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 9, Subtitle 4, Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=9-403"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"A taxpayer uses HSA funds for an eligible medical expense incurred after the HSA was established and keeps the receipt. How is the distribution generally treated?","options":["Tax free for the qualified expense","Tax free only if the account is closed immediately","Taxable as ordinary wages plus a penalty","Subject to the same tax as a nonqualified vacation withdrawal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An HSA distribution used for a qualified, otherwise unreimbursed medical expense can be excluded from federal taxable income.","id":"HM-A-10-3","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"30","prompt":"A residual rider pays the same proportion of a $4,000 benefit as the percentage of earnings lost. Earnings fall from $5,000 to $3,000 during a qualifying disability. What is the benefit?","options":["$4,000","$1,600","$2,400","$2,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The $2,000 loss is 40 percent of prior earnings. Forty percent of the $4,000 full benefit is $1,600.","id":"HM-A-5-3","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Social Security Administration: disability benefits","url":"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"15","prompt":"The premium on an individual policy with a 10-day monthly grace period is six days late. When a covered illness begins, what is the status of the policy?","options":["It remains in force during the grace period","It automatically converts to disability income coverage","It covers accidents only until the late premium arrives","It terminated the moment the due date passed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A grace period preserves coverage for the specified time after the due date. The outstanding premium is still owed.","id":"HM-A-4-2","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"55","prompt":"A producer sends a client’s medical underwriting file to an unrelated marketer without permission. Which principle is most relevant?","options":["Authorization or a permitted disclosure exception","A general premium authorization used for billing purposes","The producer’s existing appointment with the insurer","The marketer’s assurance that the list will be kept private"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A marketing disclosure is different from using information to administer coverage or pay a claim. The producer must have the required authority to disclose it.","id":"HM-A-1-22","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"39","prompt":"An otherwise eligible Maryland small employer has one worker with expensive claims. What should a carrier applying small-group rules do?","options":["Delay the group’s effective date until the worker recovers","Apply the usual lawful group eligibility rules","Require the worker to waive group coverage as a condition of issue","Rate that worker on documented past medical expenses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small-group availability and rating rules limit health-based selection. A high-cost participant does not by itself justify rejecting an eligible group.","id":"HM-A-1-25","domain":0,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §31–101, small employer definition","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201, small employer market definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating and availability","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"}]},{"lesson":"24","prompt":"A newly eligible employee asks whether a comprehensive group medical plan may exclude his asthma for a year because it began before he joined. What protection applies?","options":["Federal law bars a pre-existing condition exclusion in that coverage","The exclusion automatically lasts until the next plan year","A specialist must determine the length of the exclusion","The exclusion is compulsory unless he has an HSA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Modern comprehensive group health coverage cannot apply a pre-existing condition exclusion. Enrollment and ordinary benefit rules still apply.","id":"HM-A-6-10","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: HIPAA portability","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/portability"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule summary","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/laws-regulations/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"29","prompt":"Two otherwise identical disability contracts have 30-day and 90-day elimination periods. Which burden does the insured assume with the longer period?","options":["A longer guaranteed payment period after recovery","An automatic waiver of all future premiums","A larger cash benefit for every eligible claim","More time to finance before benefits start"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The elimination period delays the first payable period. A longer wait leaves a larger initial income gap with the insured.","id":"HM-A-5-8","domain":4,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"08","prompt":"A hospital indemnity policy pays $180 for each covered day. A patient stays three covered days and incurs a $6,700 bill. What does the stated daily benefit pay?","options":["$540","$6,160","$6,700","$180"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Three covered days at $180 total $540. The stated benefit is determined by the schedule rather than the size of the hospital bill.","id":"HM-A-3-2","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1301 et seq., short-term limited duration insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1301"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"42 U.S.C. §300gg–91(c), categories of excepted benefits","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t42/s300gg-91/"}]},{"lesson":"06","prompt":"A broken arm creates a hospital bill and keeps a carpenter away from work. Which policy is designed primarily for the lost earnings?","options":["Hospital expense insurance","Disability income insurance","A surgical expense policy","Dental insurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disability income coverage replaces part of qualifying lost earnings. Medical expense coverage addresses treatment bills.","id":"HM-A-3-1","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 3.2 and 3.3","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"42","prompt":"A Medicare beneficiary receives an outpatient physician service covered under Original Medicare. Which part ordinarily applies to the physician’s service?","options":["Part D","Part B","A standalone long-term care policy","Part A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Part B covers physician and many outpatient services. Part A principally covers inpatient hospital and certain other institutional benefits.","id":"HM-A-9-1","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare costs","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"},{"title":"Medicare & You handbook","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/medicare-and-you"}]},{"lesson":"21","prompt":"Ravi chooses a plan in which a primary care physician generally coordinates referrals to network specialists. Which plan feature is he describing?","options":["A fee schedule for professional services","A gatekeeper model","Retrospective utilization review","Open access to specialists"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a gatekeeper arrangement the primary care clinician coordinates access to specialists under the plan’s referral rules.","id":"HM-A-6-2","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Health-General Article Title 19, Subtitle 7, health maintenance organizations","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-701"},{"title":"COMAR 31.12.07, HMO regulations","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-12/chapter-31-12-07/"}]},{"lesson":"02","prompt":"An actuary studies a large pool of similar, independently occurring losses. What improves as the number of exposures grows?","options":["The reliability of aggregate loss estimates","Certainty about which member will be injured","The probability that each loss will cost exactly the average","The ability to eliminate all claims"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The law of large numbers improves predictions for a group. It does not identify in advance the individual member who will have a claim.","id":"HM-A-2-6","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Society of Actuaries — Risk and Insurance","url":"https://www.soa.org/globalassets/assets/files/edu/P-21-05.pdf"},{"title":"NAIC: A Regulator’s Introduction to the Insurance Industry","url":"https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/prod_serv_marketreg_rii_zb.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"03","prompt":"A Virginia corporation holds a certificate allowing it to sell health insurance in Maryland. How is the insurer classified in Maryland?","options":["Domestic and admitted","Foreign and admitted","Alien and nonadmitted","Foreign and nonadmitted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Its state of incorporation makes it foreign to Maryland. Its certificate of authority makes it admitted there.","id":"HM-A-1-2","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101 definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"Health-General Article §19–705, HMO certificate of authority","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-705"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"A Maryland statute requires an insurer to offer an optional benefit to a group. The group declines it. What does the word “offer” require?","options":["The insurer must pay every claim for that benefit after refusal","The group must decline its entire medical plan","The insurer must make the benefit available for the group’s decision","The benefit becomes compulsory for each employee"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A mandated offer gives the policyholder a choice. A mandated benefit is included in contracts to which that requirement applies.","id":"HM-A-1-23","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"12","prompt":"An insurer wants detail about the diagnosis and treatment behind an applicant’s recent surgery. Which source best addresses that need with appropriate authorization?","options":["A coded medical information alert","The producer’s confidential field report","An attending physician’s statement","An investigative consumer report"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The treating clinician’s records can clarify the condition, treatment and prognosis. A general inspection report serves a different purpose.","id":"HM-A-3-5","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"MIB Group: consumer information","url":"https://www.mib.com/consumer_information.html"},{"title":"Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 USC 1681","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act"},{"title":"Health-General Article §18–336, HIV testing informed consent","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=18-336"}]},{"lesson":"43","prompt":"A beneficiary has no other qualifying drug coverage for 70 consecutive days after the Part D enrollment window and does not qualify for Extra Help. What issue should be assessed when she enrolls?","options":["Automatic cancellation of Part B","A new Part A hospital deductible for those days","An automatic lifetime ban on Part D","A possible Part D late enrollment penalty"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A gap of 63 days or more without creditable prescription coverage can trigger the Part D late penalty. The calculation uses full uncovered months.","id":"HM-A-9-6","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare Advantage plans","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: drug coverage (Part D)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: Part D late enrollment penalty","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties"}]},{"lesson":"51","prompt":"An agency has a commission agreement on file. What should a newly hired producer check before claiming to be the insurer’s appointed agent?","options":["The agency’s appointment with the insurer alone","A signed compensation agreement with the agency alone","A valid individual producer license alone","Her individual appointment in the insurer’s records"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A contract sets business terms. An individual acting as the insurer’s agent must also have the required appointment.","id":"HM-A-1-4","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–118, appointments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-118"},{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101, definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"}]},{"lesson":"15","prompt":"An individual health policy includes the signed application as an attachment. A dispute arises about what documents form the agreement. Which provision should be consulted?","options":["Relation of earnings to insurance","Payment of claims","Entire contract","Change of occupation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The entire-contract provision identifies the documents that make up the contract. It prevents an unattached document from silently becoming part of it.","id":"HM-A-4-1","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"39","prompt":"A small employer asks whether group eligibility can be based simply on the business owner’s preference for one high-earning worker. Why do eligibility classifications matter?","options":["Salary alone gives the owner discretion to pick any individual","Eligibility can be defined separately after each employee applies","Eligibility follows the plan’s legitimate classifications","Eligibility follows the size of each employee’s expected claim"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Group eligibility and participation rules support a bona fide group and limit selective enrollment. Terms and applicable law govern who may be included.","id":"HM-A-7-5","domain":6,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §31–101, small employer definition","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201, small employer market definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating and availability","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"A producer copies an applicant’s signature to replace a page the carrier lost. Which fact matters for producer discipline?","options":["Whether the applicant later accepted policy delivery","The unauthorized signature on the insurance document","Whether the insurer ultimately approved the risk","Whether the premium went up after the change"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forging another person’s signature is grounds for discipline even if the producer thinks the coverage itself would be unchanged.","id":"HM-A-1-5","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"28","prompt":"A violinist cannot perform the material duties of her own occupation after a hand injury but can teach music. Which disability definition most directly evaluates her inability to perform as a violinist?","options":["Any occupation for which reasonably suited","Own occupation","Presumptive disability regardless of occupation","Partial disability based solely on reduced hours"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An own-occupation definition looks to duties of the insured’s occupation. The exact policy wording determines how other work affects benefits.","id":"HM-A-5-1","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"13","prompt":"In disability underwriting, two applicants with otherwise similar histories work at a desk and on an offshore rig. Which distinction can be relevant?","options":["The job title alone, without considering duties","Duties and hazards of the two occupations","The benefit period the employer selected for other workers","The insured’s current medical plan deductible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Occupational hazards and duties influence the risk of being unable to work. Underwriters consider these factors within applicable law.","id":"HM-A-3-6","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–504, discrimination against victims of abuse","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-504"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–909, genetic information","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-909"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–208, unfair discrimination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-208"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"A medical child support order directs an employee to cover a child living with the other parent. Why can the carrier not reject the enrollment solely because of residence?","options":["The child’s primary residence always determines coverage","The order makes the child an individual policyholder","Federal law treats every relative as a tax dependent","A qualifying support order protects the enrollment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A qualifying medical child support order allows enrollment under the parent’s plan. Living outside the employee’s household is not a permitted reason for refusal.","id":"HM-A-1-19","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"25","prompt":"A worker changes employers with $1,200 in a health savings account. What normally happens to that balance?","options":["It remains in the worker’s HSA","It must be returned to the former employer","It must be spent by the last day at work","It becomes taxable solely because of the job change"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An HSA is individually owned and portable. A job change can affect eligibility for new contributions without forfeiting existing funds.","id":"HM-A-6-5","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-19, 2026 HSA and HDHP amounts","url":"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-19.pdf"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"}]},{"lesson":"07","prompt":"After a consumer report contributes to an unfavorable insurance decision, whom should the applicant contact to dispute an error in that report?","options":["The consumer reporting agency identified in the notice","The hospital’s billing contractor in every case","The guaranty corporation","The insurer’s reinsurance broker"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The adverse-action notice identifies the reporting agency. The consumer can dispute inaccurate information in its file, while the insurer remains responsible for its decision.","id":"HM-A-2-7","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 USC 1681 et seq.","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act"},{"title":"18 USC 1033 and 1034","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1033"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: COBRA continuation coverage","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: ERISA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/erisa"}]},{"lesson":"45","prompt":"A Maryland Medigap policyholder wants a different insurer’s plan with equal benefits during the state birthday window. Which protection applies?","options":["The switch may be made without health underwriting during that window","The new carrier may require a higher-benefit plan","The new plan must add drug coverage","The applicant must first cancel Medicare Part B"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Maryland birthday rule allows a move to equal or lesser benefits during the statutory window without medical underwriting.","id":"HM-A-1-16","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–906, Medicare supplement minimum standards","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-906"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–909, guaranteed issue and birthday window","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-909"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–910, 30-day right to return","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-910"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"An insurer requests an examination under the individual policy’s physical-examination provision while a claim is pending. Who generally pays for that insurer-requested examination?","options":["The claimant’s beneficiary","The claimant’s employer","The producer personally","The insurer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Under the standard provision the insurer may request reasonable examinations at its own expense while a claim is pending.","id":"HM-A-4-7","domain":3,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"An unlicensed referral source is paid only when the prospect buys a health policy. What makes the arrangement problematic?","options":["The licensed producer conducted the entire sales meeting","The referrer provided only a name and contact information","The referral was made outside the producer’s office","Payment depends on the prospect buying coverage"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A referral to a licensed producer differs from sharing sales compensation with an unlicensed person. Conditioning payment on a purchase crosses that line.","id":"HM-A-1-20","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"27","prompt":"A health plan denies a covered person's request for an MRI and gives written appeal instructions. Which step ordinarily gives the plan a chance to reconsider its decision?","options":["A new policy application","An internal appeal","An assignment of benefits","A conversion request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An internal appeal asks the plan to review its adverse decision. An external review may be available for eligible disputes under applicable procedures.","id":"HM-A-6-12","domain":5,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: plan categories","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/choose-a-plan/plans-categories/"},{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Maryland Insurance Administration: appeals and grievances","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/AppealsandGrievances.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"A client makes a premium check payable to the agency. Where does the money belong while the agency holds it for the insurer?","options":["The agent’s commission account until the next remittance","The operating account until the carrier accepts the risk","The agency’s fiduciary premium account","A general agency reserve for disputed claims"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An agency holding premiums is a fiduciary. It must safeguard money owed to others and keep it separate from operating or personal funds.","id":"HM-A-1-6","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"01","prompt":"A delivery driver slips on an icy step and fractures her wrist. In insurance terminology, which part of this example is the peril?","options":["The insurer’s reserve for unpaid claims","The possibility of missing work next month","The accident that caused the injury","The size of her deductible"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A peril is a cause of loss. The icy condition increases the chance of a loss, and the deductible describes how it is shared.","id":"HM-A-2-1","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 2.1 and 3.1","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"31","prompt":"A surgeon expects her income to rise but worries that a later health change will prevent her from buying more disability coverage. Which rider is designed for that concern?","options":["Cost-of-living adjustment during a claim","Return of premium","Future increase option","Accidental death rider"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A future increase option allows specified increases without new evidence of medical insurability, subject to financial and other contractual limits.","id":"HM-A-5-4","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance riders","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"35","prompt":"In an insured employer group plan, a worker receives a certificate describing coverage. Who generally holds the master policy?","options":["The producer who enrolls the employees","Each employee as a separate individual policyholder","The sponsoring group","The third-party claims administrator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Group insurance uses a master contract issued to the policyholder. Certificates describe individual members’ coverage under that contract.","id":"HM-A-7-1","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"09","prompt":"A producer is comparing a comprehensive individual medical plan with a disability income policy. Why might their treatment of an existing condition differ?","options":["Every health product must use the same six-month exclusion","Different product types are governed by different underwriting and coverage rules","An insurer may always exclude prior conditions from comprehensive coverage","Disability coverage is automatically part of every medical plan"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Federal protections bar pre-existing condition exclusions in comprehensive individual medical coverage. Disability income coverage can have different underwriting rules.","id":"HM-A-3-3","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–814, reconstructive breast surgery","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-814"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: pre-existing conditions","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/pre-existing-conditions/"}]},{"lesson":"14","prompt":"A family plans to replace its individual health policy. Which comparison is especially important before giving up the existing policy?","options":["Dates, exclusions and waiting periods","The first-year savings without comparing benefit triggers","The new premium without checking benefit differences","The new insurer’s rating without checking when coverage starts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Replacement can create gaps or different benefits even at a lower premium. Comparing dates and limitations helps the family assess that risk.","id":"HM-A-3-7","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–909, replacement of Medicare supplement policies","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-909"},{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, long-term care insurance replacement","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"}]},{"lesson":"11","prompt":"An applicant leaves an important medical question blank and asks the producer to estimate the answer. What should the producer do?","options":["Record “no” because no condition was disclosed on that line","Submit the application as complete and correct it after issue","Use the producer’s assessment in place of the applicant’s response","Ask the applicant to complete and verify the response"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The application should reflect the applicant’s own information. Guessing can lead to inaccurate underwriting and later disputes.","id":"HM-A-3-4","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, producer conduct","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"}]},{"lesson":"38","prompt":"An eligible worker elects federal COBRA after a qualifying event. What is being continued?","options":["The individual’s old life insurance without premium","Permanent eligibility for the former employer’s payroll subsidy","Coverage under the employer group health plan for the applicable continuation period","A new individual disability income contract"],"answer":2,"explanation":"COBRA provides temporary continuation of qualifying group health coverage. The beneficiary generally pays the required continuation premium.","id":"HM-A-7-4","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–409, continuation after loss of employment","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-409"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–407, continuation after death","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-407"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–408, continuation after divorce","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-408"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: COBRA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"}]},{"lesson":"36","prompt":"A group carrier requires a minimum percentage of eligible employees to enroll. Which concern does this requirement primarily address?","options":["A mandatory replacement of every individual policy","An insurer’s inability to offer dependent coverage","Adverse selection if mainly employees expecting claims enroll","Whether employees all choose the same physician"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Broader participation helps prevent a voluntary group from consisting mostly of high expected claimants.","id":"HM-A-7-2","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201 et seq., small employer market","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: group health plan basics","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"A Maryland plan with dependent coverage charges extra for a newborn. Which advice helps the parents continue coverage beyond the initial period?","options":["Wait until the infant has completed medical underwriting","Give notice and pay the added premium within 31 days","Pay the first extra premium only after the first claim","Apply at the next open enrollment, regardless of age at that point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coverage starts at birth. When an extra premium is required, notice and payment within the statutory window maintain it beyond the initial period.","id":"HM-A-1-15","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"34","prompt":"A warehouse employee is injured during covered work. What generally distinguishes workers’ compensation from an ordinary negligence lawsuit?","options":["It covers all injuries during every vacation","Only a private life insurer may pay the benefits","The worker must always show the supervisor acted intentionally","The worker generally need not prove the employer was negligent to obtain covered benefits"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Workers’ compensation generally provides statutory benefits for work-related injuries without requiring proof of employer negligence.","id":"HM-A-5-7","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Social Security Administration: disability benefits","url":"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"},{"title":"Maryland Workers’ Compensation Commission","url":"https://www.wcc.state.md.us/"}]},{"lesson":"03","prompt":"A policyholder wants voting rights associated with ownership of the insurer. Which organization is designed around policyholder ownership?","options":["A third-party administrator","An independent agency","A mutual insurer","A stock insurer owned by investors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Policyholders own a mutual insurer. Customers of a stock insurer are not owners solely because they bought a policy.","id":"HM-A-2-5","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101 definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"Health-General Article §19–705, HMO certificate of authority","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-705"}]},{"lesson":"41","prompt":"A Maryland consumer applies through the state’s health coverage marketplace and appears eligible for Medicaid. What is the appropriate next step?","options":["Use the application information to assess or route Medicaid eligibility","Require a second private policy before considering Medicaid","Treat a marketplace application as proof of ineligibility","Enroll the applicant automatically in Medicare Part B"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maryland Health Connection screens for programs using the application. Medicaid eligibility is assessed under that program’s rules.","id":"HM-A-1-18","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 31, Maryland Health Benefit Exchange","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"}]},{"lesson":"49","prompt":"A business entity obtains a producer license. Its owner now wants every employee to quote and recommend policies. Who may actually do that work?","options":["Employees who hold the appropriate individual authority","Anyone who discloses that a licensed principal supervises them","Any employee whose compensation is entirely salaried","All employees working under the entity’s appointment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An agency’s license does not extend individual selling authority to unlicensed staff. Each person’s duties determine the licensing requirement.","id":"HM-A-1-11","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–103, license required and exceptions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-103"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–104 and §10–109, qualifications and examination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-104"},{"title":"MIA Bulletin 24-19, repeal of prelicensing education","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Pages/Bulletins/24-19-Repealing-the-Prelicensing-Education-and-Experience-Requirements.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"50","prompt":"A producer changes her business address. Which action fulfills her regulatory obligation?","options":["Report it only on the next renewal, regardless of date","Notify the Commissioner within the reporting window","Notify the insurer and rely on its appointment register","Keep the old address on record until a new policy is issued"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Keeping the licensing authority informed is an obligation of the producer. A private address change does not update the State’s records.","id":"HM-A-1-12","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–116, continuing education","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-116"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–115, renewal and reinstatement","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-115"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, reporting of criminal and administrative actions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"An employer pays the entire group disability premium and does not include it in employees’ taxable wages. How are benefits generally treated when paid to an employee?","options":["Taxable only to the extent they exceed employer premiums","Tax free because the employer held the master contract","Tax free up to the employee’s previous monthly earnings","Generally taxable to the employee"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Employer-funded disability income benefits are generally taxable to the employee when the employer-paid premiums were excluded from wages.","id":"HM-A-10-2","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"Which statement accurately distinguishes notice of claim from proof of loss?","options":["Notice determines final benefits and proof only pays a premium","They are interchangeable names for policy delivery","Notice alerts the insurer that a loss occurred and proof provides supporting detail","Proof is due before the insured can have a loss"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Notice starts the claims process. Proof documents the facts needed for the insurer to assess its obligation.","id":"HM-A-4-4","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"19","prompt":"A member has $300 of deductible remaining on a covered $1,300 in-network bill. The plan then pays 80 percent of the balance. How much does the member owe if no other limit applies?","options":["$300","$260","$560","$500"],"answer":3,"explanation":"After the $300 deductible, $1,000 remains. The member pays 20 percent of that $1,000, so total cost sharing is $500.","id":"HM-A-6-1","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"48","prompt":"A producer is discussing long-term care with a buyer who can barely meet current expenses. What should happen before a recommendation?","options":["Recommend the highest daily benefit available","Review the buyer’s financial circumstances and the ability to keep paying premiums","Count possible Medicaid payments as guaranteed premium income","Skip suitability questions because future claims are uncertain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Suitability review looks at need, income, assets and affordability over time. Coverage that the buyer cannot maintain may fail its purpose.","id":"HM-A-1-17","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, Long-Term Care Insurance","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §18–104 and §18–105, applications and applicants 80 and older","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=18-104"},{"title":"Tax-General Article §10–718, long-term care insurance credit","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gtg&section=10-718"}]},{"lesson":"46","prompt":"A 68-year-old is actively working and covered by the group health plan of an employer with 100 employees. Under the usual working-aged rules, which plan pays first?","options":["Medicare because the individual has reached 65","The carrier with the lower remaining deductible","The active employer group plan","The plan selected as primary by the employee"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For an employee age 65 or older covered through current employment at an employer with at least 20 employees, the group plan generally pays first.","id":"HM-A-9-4","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/who-pays-first"},{"title":"Medicare: skilled nursing facility care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care"},{"title":"Maryland Department of Health: Medicaid","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Pages/home.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"27","prompt":"Two marketplace plans cover the same essential service categories but have different metal levels. What does the metal level primarily describe?","options":["The comparative clinical quality of each hospital","A promise of exactly the same percentage for each individual member","Whether premiums are tax deductible for every buyer","How covered costs are expected to be shared across a standard population"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Metal levels reflect actuarial value across a standard population. An individual’s spending depends on the services used and plan terms.","id":"HM-A-6-9","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: plan categories","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/choose-a-plan/plans-categories/"},{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Maryland Insurance Administration: appeals and grievances","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/AppealsandGrievances.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"04","prompt":"An insurer’s written agreement specifically permits a producer to collect initial premiums. What authority is the producer exercising?","options":["Express authority","Apparent authority only","Emergency authority","Judicial authority"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Express authority comes from powers the principal specifically gives its agent. The written collection provision supplies that authority here.","id":"HM-A-2-3","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–101 definitions of insurance producer","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-101"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"20","prompt":"An insurer promises a stated cash amount for each covered day in a hospital. Which fact distinguishes this arrangement from comprehensive major medical?","options":["It uses the hospital’s full billed charge as its benefit","It first coordinates all payments with Medicare","It pays all covered charges above a deductible","It pays a scheduled amount rather than calculating a share of the allowed expense"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Fixed indemnity is designed around a named event and a stated payment. The patient’s actual facility charge may be much higher.","id":"HM-A-6-8","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary: fixed indemnity","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"}]},{"lesson":"40","prompt":"A dental plan pays 80 percent of basic restorative services after the deductible. For a covered $250 filling with the deductible already met, what is the patient’s share?","options":["$50","$80","$200","$250"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The patient’s share is 20 percent of the allowed $250, or $50. The covered service category determines the applicable rate.","id":"HM-A-8-1","domain":7,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: dental coverage in the marketplace","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/dental-coverage/"}]},{"lesson":"55","prompt":"An applicant is turned down after an insurer uses inaccurate information about a hospital stay. What can the applicant request?","options":["An unconditional right to the insurer’s preferred classification","Immediate reversal of the decision upon an oral objection","The specific reasons and supporting information","Deletion of the entire file before the disputed entry is reviewed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adverse underwriting notice and access rights help the applicant identify the information relied on and seek correction of errors.","id":"HM-A-1-8","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"47","prompt":"A reimbursement long-term care policy has a $230 daily maximum. The insured incurs $180 of eligible charges for a qualifying day. What amount is payable under that design before other limits?","options":["$180","$230","$410","$50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reimbursement pays actual eligible charges up to the maximum. A fixed indemnity benefit works differently.","id":"HM-A-9-8","domain":8,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Long-Term Care Insurance Partnership Program","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/LongTermCarePartnership.aspx"},{"title":"IRS Publication 502, long-term care premiums and per diem limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"Maryland Medicaid: long-term services and supports","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/longtermcare/Pages/Home.aspx"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: long-term care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care"}]},{"lesson":"17","prompt":"The insurer discovers an incorrect age on a policy that contains a misstatement-of-age provision. How is the benefit generally determined?","options":["By applying the producer’s current commission rate","By switching the policy to group coverage","By doubling the original benefit","By the amount the actual premium would have bought at the correct age"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A misstatement-of-age provision adjusts the benefit to the coverage that the premium would have purchased at the true age.","id":"HM-A-4-5","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, interest on late claim payments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"06","prompt":"A policy pays a stated amount if an accident causes the permanent loss of a hand. Which coverage is illustrated?","options":["Dental indemnity","Comprehensive major medical","Accidental dismemberment","Business overhead expense"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dismemberment benefits are scheduled for specified accidental losses. They do not depend on the employee’s business overhead or a medical bill total.","id":"HM-A-3-8","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 3.2 and 3.3","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"The Commissioner wants a producer to repay a client who lost money through misconduct. Which remedy addresses the client’s loss?","options":["Nonrenewal of the producer’s license","Suspension of new appointments","A civil penalty payable to the State","Restitution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Restitution addresses the financial injury to the affected person. A fine payable to the State serves a different purpose.","id":"HM-A-1-9","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]}]},{"id":"B","title":"Health Mock Exam B","minutes":105,"questions":[{"lesson":"15","prompt":"A monthly premium becomes overdue, and the owner asks whether the grace period permits skipping the payment permanently. Which answer is correct?","options":["It waives all later premiums","It extends the time to pay while coverage continues for the specified days","It turns the contract into paid-up coverage","It forgives that premium after one renewal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The grace period is additional time to pay. It does not permanently remove the premium obligation.","id":"HM-B-4-4","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"19","prompt":"A policy has a family deductible described as aggregate. What should a household check before expecting benefits on one member’s claim?","options":["Whether every family member has the same deductible claim on the same day","Whether premiums paid count automatically toward the deductible","Whether the term aggregate means that no cost sharing applies","Whether the plan requires the combined family deductible to be met before paying those benefits, subject to applicable individual limits"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An aggregate design combines eligible family expenses. The contract and applicable individual cost-sharing protections determine when benefits begin.","id":"HM-B-6-10","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"05","prompt":"A buyer pays a modest premium and later receives benefits far exceeding it after a covered illness. Which characteristic explains the unequal exchange?","options":["Subrogation","Aleatory","Bilateral negotiation of every clause","Indefeasible renewal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The value exchanged depends on an uncertain event. Insurance does not require each policyholder to receive benefits equal to the premium paid.","id":"HM-B-2-8","domain":1,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Article §12–102, rescission for material misrepresentation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=12-102"}]},{"lesson":"34","prompt":"A worker is disabled for an expected six weeks and asks whether that duration alone satisfies Social Security’s adult disability standard. What is the relevant duration test?","options":["Duration never matters if the worker has paid payroll tax","Every condition lasting six weeks qualifies","The condition generally must be expected to last at least 12 months or result in death","The worker must qualify for exactly 24 months of treatment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adult Social Security disability uses a long-term duration test plus other requirements, including its work standard. A short recovery alone does not meet that test.","id":"HM-B-5-6","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Social Security Administration: disability benefits","url":"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"},{"title":"Maryland Workers’ Compensation Commission","url":"https://www.wcc.state.md.us/"}]},{"lesson":"02","prompt":"A restaurant stops offering an activity because it does not want the associated exposure to injury. Which risk treatment did it choose?","options":["Sharing through coinsurance","Avoidance","Pooling","Retention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Discontinuing the activity removes that exposure. Paying the losses or buying a policy would leave the activity in place.","id":"HM-B-2-2","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Society of Actuaries — Risk and Insurance","url":"https://www.soa.org/globalassets/assets/files/edu/P-21-05.pdf"},{"title":"NAIC: A Regulator’s Introduction to the Insurance Industry","url":"https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/prod_serv_marketreg_rii_zb.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"37","prompt":"A child has coverage under both married parents’ group plans. Assume the ordinary birthday rule applies and there is no court order. One parent was born February 20 and the other August 3. Which plan normally pays first?","options":["The plan of the parent born February 20","The plan of the parent with a lower annual deductible","The plan that received the claim last","The plan of the older parent regardless of birthday month"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The birthday rule compares month and day within the calendar year, not the parents’ ages. February occurs before August.","id":"HM-B-7-3","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: special enrollment","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/portability"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependent coverage","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"}]},{"lesson":"26","prompt":"A member pays $350 in monthly premiums and $80 in eligible in-network cost sharing. What generally counts toward a Marketplace plan’s out-of-pocket maximum from those payments?","options":["$80","$270","$350","$430"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Eligible cost sharing counts toward the maximum. Premiums paid to keep the plan in force are excluded.","id":"HM-B-6-6","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: essential health benefits","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/what-marketplace-plans-cover/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: grandfathered plans","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/health-care-law-protections/grandfathered-plans/"}]},{"lesson":"49","prompt":"A licensed property producer is asked to advise which individual disability income policy a client should buy. What must she establish before soliciting it?","options":["Health producer authority for that activity","Written permission from the prospect to discuss disability coverage","A referral agreement that pays her only if coverage is issued","An appointment under an existing property license alone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Authority is issued by line. A license for property insurance alone does not authorize solicitation of disability income coverage.","id":"HM-B-1-1","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–103, license required and exceptions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-103"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–104 and §10–109, qualifications and examination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-104"},{"title":"MIA Bulletin 24-19, repeal of prelicensing education","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Pages/Bulletins/24-19-Repealing-the-Prelicensing-Education-and-Experience-Requirements.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"04","prompt":"A producer's contract authorizes policy delivery but does not list every routine step needed to arrange it. Which authority covers acts reasonably necessary to carry out the assigned duty?","options":["Unlimited authority to change premiums","Implied authority","Authority arising solely from the client’s signature","Authority to amend state law"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Implied authority accompanies the express assignment and covers reasonable steps needed to perform it. It does not authorize unrelated contract changes.","id":"HM-B-2-5","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–101 definitions of insurance producer","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-101"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"An agency has $9,000 in its premium trust account. All $9,000 is owed to insurers or clients. How much can the owner withdraw for payroll today?","options":["$0","$4,500","$9,000","$900"],"answer":0,"explanation":"None of this balance belongs to the agency. A withdrawal for payroll would use fiduciary money owed to other parties.","id":"HM-B-1-3","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"32","prompt":"A self-employed disability applicant reports gross business receipts of $180,000 and substantial business expenses. Why does the underwriter ask for financial records?","options":["To set benefits equal to all gross business receipts","To guarantee that profits will rise during the policy term","To establish supportable earned income available for replacement","To determine the deductible on the applicant’s home"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Gross receipts can overstate personal earnings. Financial underwriting supports an appropriate benefit amount.","id":"HM-B-5-7","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"18","prompt":"An in-network provider bills $800 but has agreed to accept $500 for a covered service. With a met deductible and 20 percent member coinsurance, what is the member’s coinsurance?","options":["$100","$400","$160","$300"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Coinsurance is based on the $500 allowed amount. The contracted difference from the billed amount is not additional coinsurance.","id":"HM-B-6-4","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"35","prompt":"A trade association existed to provide professional education long before it bought group insurance. Which fact supports its status as a bona fide group?","options":["It enrolled only people expecting large claims","It formed only after the insurer identified a particular applicant","It has no activities besides collecting premiums","It has a purpose independent of obtaining insurance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An independent association purpose helps distinguish a genuine group from a collection assembled solely to obtain coverage.","id":"HM-B-7-1","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"An advertisement calls a privately issued health policy an official Maryland insurance program. Which correction matters most?","options":["Move the insurer’s name to a footnote","Keep the claim but send it only to existing clients","Add the producer’s license number and keep the headline","Identify it as private coverage and remove the implied government endorsement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Advertising must accurately identify the insurer and product. A misleading implication of state sponsorship is not cured by a small disclosure.","id":"HM-B-1-5","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"47","prompt":"A tax-qualified long-term care policy permits a benefit trigger for severe cognitive impairment. Which fact most directly supports that trigger?","options":["The insured prefers meals prepared by someone else","The insured has stopped driving for convenience","The insured has reached age 70 without any impairment","A licensed practitioner certifies that substantial supervision is needed for safety"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Severe cognitive impairment requiring substantial supervision is an alternative to the functional daily-activity trigger under the applicable standard.","id":"HM-B-9-5","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Long-Term Care Insurance Partnership Program","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/LongTermCarePartnership.aspx"},{"title":"IRS Publication 502, long-term care premiums and per diem limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"Maryland Medicaid: long-term services and supports","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/longtermcare/Pages/Home.aspx"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: long-term care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care"}]},{"lesson":"31","prompt":"After an injury, a policy pays for approved retraining that helps the insured return to productive work. Which feature is being used?","options":["Rehabilitation benefit","Accidental death benefit","Refund of unused deductible","Waiver of subrogation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A rehabilitation benefit supports an approved return-to-work or retraining plan. It addresses recovery of work capacity.","id":"HM-B-5-8","domain":4,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance riders","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"11","prompt":"At delivery, the issued disability policy has a higher premium than the applicant requested. What should the producer do?","options":["Alter the printed premium before delivery","Cancel the applicant’s other coverage without discussing it","Represent it as the original offer","Explain the changed terms and obtain the applicant’s acceptance as required"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An issued policy with modified terms is a counteroffer. The applicant should understand what changed before accepting.","id":"HM-B-3-10","domain":2,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, producer conduct","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"}]},{"lesson":"20","prompt":"A nonprofit health service plan arranges services for subscribers through participating providers. What is the member’s contract generally called in that arrangement?","options":["A reinsurance treaty","A disability buy-sell agreement","A subscriber contract or certificate","A producer appointment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Health service plans provide or arrange covered services under subscriber contracts. A producer appointment governs the carrier-agent relationship.","id":"HM-B-6-11","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary: fixed indemnity","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"}]},{"lesson":"03","prompt":"A company is incorporated in Maryland and authorized to issue health policies there. Which term describes its domicile from Maryland’s perspective?","options":["Alien","Domestic","Nonadmitted","Foreign"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Domicile refers to where the insurer was organized. Admission describes its authority to transact business and is a separate classification.","id":"HM-B-1-2","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101 definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"Health-General Article §19–705, HMO certificate of authority","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-705"}]},{"lesson":"40","prompt":"A dental plan has a $1,500 annual benefit maximum and has paid $1,200 this year. It would otherwise pay $450 on the next claim. How much can it pay before the maximum is reached?","options":["$750","$300","$450","$1,200"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only $300 remains in the annual benefit limit. This is a cap on the plan’s payment, unlike a medical out-of-pocket maximum on the member’s costs.","id":"HM-B-8-1","domain":7,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: dental coverage in the marketplace","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/dental-coverage/"}]},{"lesson":"47","prompt":"A family caregiver needs temporary relief while the insured continues receiving covered long-term care. Which benefit name most closely fits?","options":["Adult day care specifically for outpatient clinical treatment","Convalescent rehabilitation following a hospital stay","Homemaker services for routine chores regardless of caregiver needs","Respite care"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Respite care temporarily relieves a caregiver, subject to policy terms. It addresses the care arrangement rather than the caregiver’s wages.","id":"HM-B-9-8","domain":8,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Long-Term Care Insurance Partnership Program","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/LongTermCarePartnership.aspx"},{"title":"IRS Publication 502, long-term care premiums and per diem limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"Maryland Medicaid: long-term services and supports","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/longtermcare/Pages/Home.aspx"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: long-term care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care"}]},{"lesson":"28","prompt":"A policy’s presumptive disability clause lists permanent loss of sight in both eyes. A covered insured meets that definition but continues some consulting. Which provision should be examined first?","options":["The recurrent disability provision","The policy’s presumptive loss clause","The regular total disability occupation test","The residual earnings-loss calculation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A specified presumptive loss can qualify under the policy’s own terms without the ordinary occupational showing.","id":"HM-B-5-1","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"06","prompt":"A business buys coverage that pays continuing office expenses when its owner becomes disabled. What is the insured need?","options":["Insuring the owner’s home against fire","Providing employees with retirement income","Keeping operating expenses paid during a covered disability","Replacing the owner’s full personal salary"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Business overhead expense insurance reimburses eligible operating costs. Personal income replacement is a separate coverage objective.","id":"HM-B-3-8","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 3.2 and 3.3","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"48","prompt":"A long-term care seller knowingly misstates the old contract’s benefits to induce replacement. Which response is appropriate?","options":["Reject the misleading comparison and make a complete, accurate one","Have the customer sign a blank comparison page","Cancel the old policy before describing the new benefits","Describe the transaction as a conversion to avoid disclosure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Replacement requires an informed comparison. Misstating existing benefits can constitute twisting and defeats the purpose of replacement disclosures.","id":"HM-B-1-21","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, Long-Term Care Insurance","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §18–104 and §18–105, applications and applicants 80 and older","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=18-104"},{"title":"Tax-General Article §10–718, long-term care insurance credit","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gtg&section=10-718"}]},{"lesson":"40","prompt":"A parent reviews a benefit schedule that has a separate lifetime maximum for braces. Which category should she look under?","options":["Endodontics","Periodontics","Orthodontics","Diagnostic radiography"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Orthodontics addresses tooth alignment. Some plans apply a separate lifetime limit to this category.","id":"HM-B-8-2","domain":7,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: dental coverage in the marketplace","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/dental-coverage/"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"A corporation is the owner and beneficiary of a disability buyout policy funding the purchase of an owner’s business interest. How are the premiums generally treated?","options":["Automatically deductible as wages to every shareholder","An itemized medical expense of each employee","Generally not deductible for the buyout coverage","A tax-free HSA contribution by the corporation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Disability buyout coverage finances an ownership transfer. Its premiums generally are not treated as deductible business overhead expense insurance premiums.","id":"HM-B-10-5","domain":9,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"A couple assumes their unmarried grandchild can qualify under Maryland’s dependent coverage statute only if they have a custody order. What does §15-418 actually say?","options":["A grandchild must always obtain a separate individual contract","Every grandchild qualifies solely on residence in Maryland","A grandchild must qualify only through a formal adoption decree","A grandchild is a separately listed category under the statute"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For plans subject to this Maryland rule, an unmarried grandchild under 25 is listed as a child dependent. The statute lists a grandchild separately from the other eligible-child category.","id":"HM-B-1-9","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"17","prompt":"An insured with a beneficiary designated as revocable wants a different beneficiary. Which general rule applies?","options":["A new policy and new underwriting are always necessary","The owner can normally change the designation in the manner required by the policy","The revocable beneficiary’s written consent is always necessary","Only the attending physician may make the change"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A revocable designation reserves the right to change the beneficiary. An irrevocable designation generally requires the beneficiary’s consent.","id":"HM-B-4-6","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, interest on late claim payments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"07","prompt":"A state insurance department is considering an NAIC model. When does that model ordinarily become enforceable as a state rule?","options":["When the NAIC posts the model on its website","When a producer passes a licensing exam","When two insurers agree to use its terminology","When the state adopts it through its lawmaking or regulatory process"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NAIC models help states develop consistent rules. The state’s own adoption process gives a model legal effect there.","id":"HM-B-2-7","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 USC 1681 et seq.","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act"},{"title":"18 USC 1033 and 1034","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1033"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: COBRA continuation coverage","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: ERISA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/erisa"}]},{"lesson":"01","prompt":"A client stores cleaning fluid in an unmarked drinking bottle. Which term describes the condition that increases the chance of injury?","options":["Policy exclusion","Speculative investment","Physical hazard","Loss frequency ratio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The unsafe physical condition increases the likelihood of injury. The actual poisoning or accident would be the loss-causing event.","id":"HM-B-2-1","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 2.1 and 3.1","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"A taxpayer itemizes, has adjusted gross income of $80,000, and has $8,000 of otherwise eligible unreimbursed medical expenses. Using a 7.5 percent floor, how much is above the floor?","options":["$8,000","$6,000","$2,000","$600"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The floor is $6,000, or 7.5 percent of $80,000. The $2,000 excess is the amount above the floor, assuming the expenses qualify.","id":"HM-B-10-1","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"25","prompt":"An employer describes a benefit funded only by its contributions and used to reimburse eligible medical expenses. Which arrangement is being described?","options":["A health reimbursement arrangement","An individual disability policy","An employee-owned HSA funded only by statutory employee contributions","A voluntary after-tax payroll savings account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An HRA is employer funded and reimburses expenses under its plan terms. It is distinct from an individually owned HSA.","id":"HM-B-6-2","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-19, 2026 HSA and HDHP amounts","url":"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-19.pdf"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"}]},{"lesson":"49","prompt":"Why does a Maryland agency designate a responsible licensed producer when it holds a business entity license?","options":["Allow all officers to negotiate without a line of authority","Give each employee the principal’s continuing education credits","Transfer the entity’s appointment to every unlicensed employee","Identify the person responsible for the entity’s compliance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Designating a responsible producer supports compliance and accountability. It does not waive the licensing rules for other people in the agency.","id":"HM-B-1-15","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–103, license required and exceptions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-103"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–104 and §10–109, qualifications and examination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-104"},{"title":"MIA Bulletin 24-19, repeal of prelicensing education","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Pages/Bulletins/24-19-Repealing-the-Prelicensing-Education-and-Experience-Requirements.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"51","prompt":"A Maryland carrier hires a new producer and appoints her. Who is responsible for maintaining the insurer’s required register of appointed producers?","options":["The insurer","The credit reporting agency","The policyholder","The applicant’s employer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The insurer must document the producers acting for it. A producer’s own sales journal does not replace the insurer’s register.","id":"HM-B-1-25","domain":0,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–118, appointments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-118"},{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101, definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"}]},{"lesson":"15","prompt":"A policy lapsed. The insurer demands an application for reinstatement and gives a conditional receipt. Which provision tells the owner when coverage resumes?","options":["Other insurance in this insurer","Examination and autopsy","Notice of claim","Reinstatement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The reinstatement clause sets out the insurer’s approval process and applicable default timing. A conditional receipt alone need not restore coverage immediately.","id":"HM-B-4-7","domain":3,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"25","prompt":"A person has a qualifying high-deductible plan but is also enrolled in Medicare. How does Medicare enrollment generally affect new HSA contributions?","options":["It makes the individual ineligible to make new HSA contributions for Medicare-covered months","It forfeits the existing HSA balance immediately","It converts all existing balances into an HRA","It allows unlimited additional contributions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Medicare enrollment ends HSA contribution eligibility for those months. Existing account funds can still be used for qualified expenses.","id":"HM-B-6-9","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-19, 2026 HSA and HDHP amounts","url":"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-19.pdf"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"}]},{"lesson":"11","prompt":"A conditional receipt accompanies an initial premium. The applicant assumes all coverage began immediately. Which document determines whether temporary coverage applies?","options":["The conditional receipt and its terms","The premium quote given at the first interview","The prior contract’s customary renewal date","The applicant’s request for an immediate effective date"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Payment under a conditional receipt is subject to the receipt’s terms, such as insurability and its effective-date rules.","id":"HM-B-3-4","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, producer conduct","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"At a disciplinary hearing, why should the notice identify the grounds for the proposed action?","options":["Turn an administrative matter into automatic criminal liability","Make the agency contract take precedence over the order","Allow the producer to substitute a privately chosen sanction","Give the licensee an opportunity to respond"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Notice and the opportunity for a hearing give the licensee a chance to address the basis of the proposed disciplinary action.","id":"HM-B-1-23","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"17","prompt":"An individual policy says the insurer may refuse renewal only if specified conditions in the contract occur. What form of renewability is described?","options":["Noncancelable","Conditionally renewable","Guaranteed issue","Optionally renewable without stated conditions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conditionally renewable coverage ties nonrenewal to stated conditions. Those conditions are different from a guarantee against rate increases.","id":"HM-B-4-3","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, interest on late claim payments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"21","prompt":"A patient assumes an HMO network rule means emergency care must always wait for a referral. Which situation should be distinguished from routine network care?","options":["A routine elective second opinion","An emergency needing immediate evaluation","A scheduled annual eye exam","A nonurgent follow-up already set for next month"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Emergency care has specific access protections. Routine referral and network rules should not be applied as a reason to delay emergency evaluation.","id":"HM-B-6-3","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Health-General Article Title 19, Subtitle 7, health maintenance organizations","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-701"},{"title":"COMAR 31.12.07, HMO regulations","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-12/chapter-31-12-07/"}]},{"lesson":"02","prompt":"People who expect expensive care are especially eager to enroll in a voluntary plan at a single price. What does the underwriter call this tendency?","options":["Diversification","Subrogation","Loss prevention","Adverse selection"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Differences in expected claims influence who wants coverage. The resulting mix can have higher losses than the pricing assumptions anticipated.","id":"HM-B-2-6","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Society of Actuaries — Risk and Insurance","url":"https://www.soa.org/globalassets/assets/files/edu/P-21-05.pdf"},{"title":"NAIC: A Regulator’s Introduction to the Insurance Industry","url":"https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/prod_serv_marketreg_rii_zb.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"The parents of a child who cannot support herself because of a qualifying disability ask about coverage past the ordinary age limit. Which fact is most relevant?","options":["Proof of continuing qualifying incapacity and dependency","A previously issued individual disability income policy","The existence of a medical condition regardless of self-support","A new parent employment contract listing the adult child"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maryland rules protect continued coverage for qualifying dependents who cannot support themselves. Insurers can require evidence of the qualifying circumstances.","id":"HM-B-1-18","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"33","prompt":"Two partners agree to buy a disabled partner’s interest after a prolonged disability. Which coverage can supply the agreed purchase funds?","options":["Personal short-term disability","Disability buy-sell insurance","Basic medical expense insurance","Office rent reimbursement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buy-sell coverage funds the ownership transfer described in the agreement. It does not merely pay the firm’s monthly bills.","id":"HM-B-5-4","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics: disability insurance in employee benefits","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ebs/"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: business disability coverage","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"A carrier receives a medical child support order outside annual enrollment. An employee says it can wait until next year. What should the carrier do?","options":["Ask the employer to substitute a life insurance policy","Require the child to buy an individual policy first","Return the order solely because the enrollment period is closed","Process enrollment under the qualifying order without waiting for open enrollment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A qualifying order can require dependent enrollment outside ordinary enrollment windows. The date of annual enrollment does not defeat the order.","id":"HM-B-1-24","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"09","prompt":"A disability contract excludes loss resulting from a named hazardous activity. What should a producer examine before claiming an accident is excluded?","options":["Whether the activity appeared on the application even if never excluded","Whether the producer regarded the activity as hazardous","Whether the insured participated in it at any time before issue","Whether the cause fits the actual exclusion"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An exclusion removes coverage for specified circumstances. Its wording and the cause of loss determine whether it applies.","id":"HM-B-3-3","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–814, reconstructive breast surgery","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-814"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: pre-existing conditions","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/pre-existing-conditions/"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"A producer intentionally reports a treatment that never occurred so the policyholder receives an insurance payment. Which element distinguishes this from a billing mistake?","options":["The client’s use of an individual plan","The timing of the first premium","The knowingly false representation made to obtain a benefit","The fact that a physician’s name appears on the bill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fraud involves intentional deception. An inadvertent error should be corrected but does not by itself establish that intent.","id":"HM-B-1-8","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"55","prompt":"An insurer refuses to change a disputed item after an applicant requests a correction. Which action helps later readers understand the dispute?","options":["Substitute an oral explanation to the producer for a record entry","Attach the applicant’s statement of dispute as required","Refer the dispute to another insurer without recording the applicant’s position","Delete the disputed entry automatically without considering the request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The applicant can challenge inaccurate information. A recorded statement of dispute preserves the applicant’s position when the insurer declines to make the change.","id":"HM-B-1-7","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"30","prompt":"A carpenter returns to work part time after a disabling injury. His policy measures residual disability through a qualifying reduction in earnings. What information is essential?","options":["The face amount of an accidental death rider","The deductible paid on the related medical bills","Earnings before and during disability","Number of medically restricted duties without earnings figures"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Residual disability benefits often use the relationship between prior and current earnings, subject to the contract’s threshold and other requirements.","id":"HM-B-5-2","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Social Security Administration: disability benefits","url":"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"45","prompt":"A customer receives a newly issued Maryland Medicare supplement and wants to review it at home. What return right should she look for?","options":["A return right beginning at the next birthday","A 30-day period after delivery to return the policy for a refund","A return right only after the first claim","A 48-hour period beginning at application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Medigap free-look period gives the buyer time to inspect the delivered contract. The birthday window addresses a different type of transaction.","id":"HM-B-1-10","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–906, Medicare supplement minimum standards","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-906"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–909, guaranteed issue and birthday window","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-909"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–910, 30-day right to return","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-910"}]},{"lesson":"15","prompt":"An agent promises verbally to remove a medical exclusion from an issued policy. Which requirement should the insured look for before treating the change as effective?","options":["A larger check for the unchanged premium","A letter from an unrelated insurer","An amendment approved and endorsed as the contract requires","A second copy of the original brochure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Policy changes must meet the entire-contract and amendment provisions. A sales conversation alone does not amend the issued contract.","id":"HM-B-4-1","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"42","prompt":"A beneficiary is formally admitted as an inpatient for a covered hospital stay under Original Medicare. Which part primarily addresses the facility’s inpatient charges?","options":["Part D","A Medigap application alone","Part A","Part B preventive services"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Part A is hospital insurance. The physician’s professional services may be billed under Part B even during an inpatient stay.","id":"HM-B-9-1","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare costs","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"},{"title":"Medicare & You handbook","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/medicare-and-you"}]},{"lesson":"50","prompt":"An agency principal completes continuing education for herself. What does that accomplish for the other resident producers in the office?","options":["Each producer must satisfy their own requirements","Only separately incorporated producers need individual credits","Every salaried producer receives the principal’s credits","The agency can allocate the hours across its licensed staff"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Continuing education applies to the individual producer in accordance with that producer’s obligations. One person’s credits do not transfer to colleagues.","id":"HM-B-1-6","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–116, continuing education","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-116"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–115, renewal and reinstatement","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-115"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, reporting of criminal and administrative actions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"}]},{"lesson":"48","prompt":"A long-term care applicant asks why she received an outline of coverage. What is its purpose?","options":["To determine eligibility for state Medicaid benefits","To replace the issued insurance contract","To guarantee that every future claim will be paid","To summarize benefits, limits and important terms for comparison"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The outline helps the applicant compare and understand coverage. Claim entitlement depends on the policy and facts of the claim.","id":"HM-B-1-11","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, Long-Term Care Insurance","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §18–104 and §18–105, applications and applicants 80 and older","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=18-104"},{"title":"Tax-General Article §10–718, long-term care insurance credit","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gtg&section=10-718"}]},{"lesson":"14","prompt":"Why might a switch to an apparently cheaper limited-benefit plan leave a family with more uncovered bills?","options":["The old policy pays all differences after cancellation","Every replacement must match every former benefit","A lower premium always extends the current plan’s benefits","Its benefit schedule or covered events may be narrower"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Comparing only premiums can hide substantial differences in benefits and terms. The new plan pays only what its contract provides.","id":"HM-B-3-7","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–909, replacement of Medicare supplement policies","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-909"},{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, long-term care insurance replacement","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"}]},{"lesson":"06","prompt":"A client asks which plan covers ongoing support with bathing or dressing after she meets its benefit triggers. Which policy fits that need?","options":["Accidental death insurance","A basic surgical schedule","Long-term care insurance","A business loan protection policy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Long-term care insurance can pay for qualifying personal care at home or in a facility, depending on the policy.","id":"HM-B-3-1","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 3.2 and 3.3","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"19","prompt":"A covered $2,400 in-network claim arrives after the deductible is satisfied. The member pays 25 percent coinsurance and has not reached any maximum. What does the insurer pay?","options":["$1,200","$1,800","$600","$2,400"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The member’s 25 percent share is $600. The insurer pays the remaining 75 percent, or $1,800.","id":"HM-B-6-1","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"51","prompt":"An insurer ends a producer’s appointment. The producer still holds an active Maryland license. What may the producer claim about that insurer now?","options":["The appointment remains active until the license expires","The producer can no longer act as its appointed agent","The individual license automatically renews the appointment","The agency’s letterhead alone continues authority"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A license and an appointment serve different purposes. An active producer license does not continue an appointment the insurer has terminated.","id":"HM-B-1-4","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–118, appointments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-118"},{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101, definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"}]},{"lesson":"10","prompt":"A mailer headlines “Every hospital bill paid” for a policy that pays a fixed $120 daily benefit. What must the producer change?","options":["The identity of the person who processes checks","Only the type size of the company address","The description must reflect the fixed amount and its limitations","Only the day on which the mailer is sent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fixed indemnity plan does not promise reimbursement of every bill. Advertising must not misstate the amount or nature of benefits.","id":"HM-B-1-19","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202, misrepresentation and false advertising","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://www.mdlifega.org/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 9, Subtitle 4, Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=9-403"}]},{"lesson":"12","prompt":"A producer suspects a hobby includes racing but has no details. Which action gives underwriting the most useful evidence?","options":["Document the activity, frequency and actual level of participation","Omit the hobby because the applicant earns no income from it","Rely only on the occupation class already shown on the application","Select the most hazardous classification without further information"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Accurate information about frequency, level and protective measures helps an underwriter evaluate the actual exposure.","id":"HM-B-3-5","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"MIB Group: consumer information","url":"https://www.mib.com/consumer_information.html"},{"title":"Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 USC 1681","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act"},{"title":"Health-General Article §18–336, HIV testing informed consent","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=18-336"}]},{"lesson":"29","prompt":"A $2,700 monthly disability policy has a maximum benefit period of 24 months. If 10 full monthly payments are due after the elimination period, what is the total before offsets?","options":["$64,800","$2,700","$27,000","$24,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ten payable months at $2,700 total $27,000. The maximum period is a limit, not a guarantee of 24 payments on every claim.","id":"HM-B-5-5","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"43","prompt":"A beneficiary with limited resources qualifies for Medicare Extra Help. What type of cost is this program designed to assist with?","options":["Medigap premiums for every policyholder","Part D prescription drug costs","All employer disability premiums","Long-term care room and board without limit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Extra Help is a subsidy for qualifying Medicare prescription drug costs. It is distinct from private supplemental insurance.","id":"HM-B-9-7","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare Advantage plans","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: drug coverage (Part D)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: Part D late enrollment penalty","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties"}]},{"lesson":"03","prompt":"A producer asks several unrelated insurers for proposals for the same client. Which distribution arrangement most naturally fits?","options":["An independent agency","A government benefit office","A direct-response sale without an intermediary","An exclusive agency limited to one carrier"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent agencies can represent multiple carriers. Their appointments and agreements determine which companies they may represent.","id":"HM-B-2-3","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101 definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"Health-General Article §19–705, HMO certificate of authority","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-705"}]},{"lesson":"22","prompt":"Which description best fits a point-of-service plan?","options":["Routine care exclusively through network referrals with no outside option","An unrestricted indemnity plan with no network incentives","A scheduled payment for each inpatient day regardless of network","Coordinated network care with an out-of-network option"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Point-of-service arrangements combine managed in-network care with an out-of-network option, usually at higher member cost.","id":"HM-B-6-5","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary: PPO and POS","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"TRICARE plans","url":"https://www.tricare.mil/Plans"}]},{"lesson":"24","prompt":"An eligible employee declines a group plan because of a spouse’s coverage, then loses that coverage. Which opportunity should the employee investigate promptly?","options":["Immediate access to every plan without an enrollment request","An automatic conversion into Medicare","HIPAA special enrollment in the employer plan","A permanent exemption from future premiums"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Loss of other coverage can create special enrollment rights if the conditions are met. The employee should request enrollment within the applicable window.","id":"HM-B-6-12","domain":5,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: HIPAA portability","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/portability"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule summary","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/laws-regulations/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"A client is considering leaving a policy. The producer falsely says the proposed replacement excludes emergency care to keep the old coverage in force. What is the practice?","options":["Subrogation against the new insurer","Lawful conservation of business regardless of the statement","Twisting to induce retention","A routine notice of nonrenewal"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Misrepresentation can induce someone to keep a contract as well as to replace one. The false comparison is the issue.","id":"HM-B-1-14","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"Employees pay group disability premiums through a pretax cafeteria plan. Why can the resulting wage-replacement benefits generally be taxable?","options":["Tax treatment depends only on the length of disability","All medical expense reimbursements are taxable","Payroll deduction always makes benefits tax free","The premiums were excluded from taxable pay rather than funded with after-tax employee dollars"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pretax salary reductions are generally treated like employer contributions for this purpose. Benefits attributable to them are generally taxable.","id":"HM-B-10-2","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"23","prompt":"A discharge planner evaluates an inpatient’s ongoing need for treatment while the patient is still hospitalized. Which review timing applies?","options":["Retrospective only","Concurrent","Prospective only","Postpayment recovery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Concurrent review assesses ongoing care during the admission and can inform discharge planning or continuing authorization.","id":"HM-B-6-8","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (model 120)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–10A, utilization review and grievances","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-10A-01"}]},{"lesson":"08","prompt":"A specified-disease policy lists cancer but does not list diabetes. A covered person is hospitalized solely for diabetes. What should the buyer expect from that policy?","options":["The hospitalization alone does not trigger the listed disease benefit","Coverage automatically converts to major medical","The insurer pays any medical bill over the deductible","Every inpatient admission triggers the cancer benefit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A specified-disease policy pays for covered conditions under its terms. It is narrower than comprehensive medical coverage.","id":"HM-B-3-2","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1301 et seq., short-term limited duration insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1301"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"42 U.S.C. §300gg–91(c), categories of excepted benefits","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t42/s300gg-91/"}]},{"lesson":"27","prompt":"A shopper qualifies for cost-sharing reductions on the individual Marketplace. In which metal tier must an eligible shopper generally enroll to receive those reductions?","options":["Bronze","Platinum","Gold","Silver"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cost-sharing reductions generally apply through eligible Silver Marketplace plans. Premium tax credits serve a different purpose.","id":"HM-B-6-7","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: plan categories","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/choose-a-plan/plans-categories/"},{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Maryland Insurance Administration: appeals and grievances","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/AppealsandGrievances.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"55","prompt":"An underwriting notice says a consumer reporting agency provided information that affected the decision. Who made the insurance decision?","options":["The insurer using the information","The applicant’s former employer","The Maryland guaranty corporation","The consumer reporting agency automatically"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The reporting agency furnishes information. The insurer decides whether to issue coverage and on what terms, subject to applicable notice rules.","id":"HM-B-1-17","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"36","prompt":"An employer pays the full premium for an eligible group benefit. Which term describes its funding structure for employees?","options":["Experience rated","Individually underwritten","Noncontributory","Contributory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Noncontributory means employees do not contribute to that premium. It describes who pays, rather than how the insurer sets rates.","id":"HM-B-7-2","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201 et seq., small employer market","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: group health plan basics","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans"}]},{"lesson":"44","prompt":"Two insurers sell the same standardized Medigap Plan G in Maryland. Which comparison still matters to a shopper?","options":["Premium, pricing practices and service","Whether one insurer may replace Medicare Part A","Whether the plan letter sets the same premium statewide","Whether one insurer can delete standardized basic Plan G benefits"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Benefits for a standardized plan letter are generally the same. Premiums and company practices can differ.","id":"HM-B-9-3","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how to compare Medigap policies","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits"},{"title":"Choosing a Medigap Policy (CMS guide)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/publications/02110-medigap-guide-health-insurance.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"38","prompt":"An employee asks how continuation differs from conversion. Which description is most accurate?","options":["Continuation automatically increases all benefit limits","Both necessarily keep the same group policy forever","Continuation keeps qualifying group coverage for a time, while conversion changes to an available individual contract","Conversion always requires the former employer to pay premiums"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Continuation and conversion provide different paths after group coverage ends. Eligibility, time limits and benefits depend on the relevant rule and contract.","id":"HM-B-7-4","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–409, continuation after loss of employment","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-409"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–407, continuation after death","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-407"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–408, continuation after divorce","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-408"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: COBRA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"An HSA owner turns 65, then withdraws money for a nonmedical purchase. What is the usual federal result for that withdrawal?","options":["Ordinary income tax, generally no additional 20 percent tax","The additional 20 percent tax but no ordinary income tax","No income tax once the owner becomes eligible for Medicare","Ordinary income tax plus the additional 20 percent tax"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Age 65 generally removes the additional tax on a nonqualified distribution. It does not turn a nonmedical purchase into a qualified expense.","id":"HM-B-10-4","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"13","prompt":"An applicant for an underwritten disability policy qualifies at the standard premium except for a particular excluded condition. How is the change commonly documented?","options":["A beneficiary assignment","A blanket change to all policies issued in the state","An exclusion rider identifying the condition","An increase in the policy loan rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A policy-specific rider can exclude a named condition while preserving the remaining coverage. Its terms should be explained at delivery.","id":"HM-B-3-6","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–504, discrimination against victims of abuse","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-504"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–909, genetic information","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-909"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–208, unfair discrimination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-208"}]},{"lesson":"41","prompt":"A consumer asks a marketplace navigator for a commission arrangement tied to the insurer selected. How does the navigator’s role differ from a producer’s sales role?","options":["Provide impartial enrollment assistance","Bind insurer coverage through the navigator’s own authority","Set individual premium rates based on the applicant’s health","Negotiate a separate fee if a particular carrier is selected"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The navigator helps consumers understand enrollment and available options. Compensation tied to carrier selection conflicts with impartial assistance.","id":"HM-B-1-12","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 31, Maryland Health Benefit Exchange","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"An order directs a producer to stop using a deceptive mailer immediately. Which remedy is being used?","options":["A binder","A premium audit","A cease-and-desist order","A certificate of renewal"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cease-and-desist order directs a person to stop a particular unlawful practice. It is distinct from paying a financial penalty.","id":"HM-B-1-16","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"35","prompt":"A worker receives a group certificate and wants to know its exclusions. Which source should she use first?","options":["The employer’s payroll enrollment deduction alone","The certificate and governing plan documents","The provider network directory without the benefit booklet","An explanation of benefits issued for a different service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The group plan and its coverage documents identify benefits, exclusions and member rights. The certificate supplies information about the worker’s coverage.","id":"HM-B-7-5","domain":6,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"43","prompt":"A person keeps Original Medicare and wants a standalone plan for outpatient prescription drugs. Which coverage should she examine?","options":["A Medigap plan sold today as new primary drug insurance","Part D prescription drug coverage","A Part A inpatient benefit period","A disability buy-sell plan"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A standalone Part D plan can complement Original Medicare. New Medigap policies are not a substitute for Part D drug coverage.","id":"HM-B-9-2","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare Advantage plans","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: drug coverage (Part D)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: Part D late enrollment penalty","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"An employee’s HRA reimburses an eligible $400 bill. Can she also treat the same $400 as an unreimbursed medical expense for an itemized deduction?","options":["Yes, if only the employee files the itemized deduction","Yes, because employer-funded HRAs are separate from itemized deductions","Yes, if payment and reimbursement occurred in the same calendar year","No, the HRA reimbursement reduces the unreimbursed amount"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The itemized medical expense deduction applies to eligible unreimbursed expenses. The same reimbursed cost cannot be claimed again as unreimbursed.","id":"HM-B-10-3","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"A court filing is being considered immediately after proof of loss is sent. Which provision may require the claimant to wait before suing on the policy?","options":["Renewal premium","Legal actions","Free look","Change of beneficiary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The legal-actions provision addresses when a lawsuit on the policy may begin and the time limit for bringing it.","id":"HM-B-4-5","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"A disability policy provides monthly installments for a continuing loss. What does the time-of-payment provision address?","options":["When benefits must be paid after proof of loss","How promptly the insured must first notify the carrier of a loss","How long the owner has to reinstate a lapsed policy","When the insurer may demand an examination during a claim"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Time of payment governs when the carrier pays an established claim, including periodic installments for a continuing covered loss.","id":"HM-B-4-2","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"An agency changes an applicant’s answers after signature and files them as originals. Which recordkeeping practice would have avoided the problem?","options":["Keep only the agent’s recollection of the original answers","Destroy the page before the application is submitted","Obtain and preserve the applicant’s authorization for a correction","Use another client’s signed correction form"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A change to material application information must be properly authorized and documented. A file should accurately reflect what the applicant approved.","id":"HM-B-1-13","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"31","prompt":"A claimant already receiving a disability benefit wants it to increase over a long claim as prices rise. Which feature can do that?","options":["A change-of-beneficiary clause","A shorter application period","A business buy-sell agreement","A cost-of-living adjustment rider"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A COLA rider can increase benefits during a covered disability using the contractual adjustment method. A future purchase option serves a different need.","id":"HM-B-5-3","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance riders","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"46","prompt":"A 59-year-old has Medicare because of disability and group coverage through current employment with a 150-employee employer. Under the usual large-group disability rules, who pays first?","options":["A Part D drug plan for every medical expense","Medicare in every case involving a person under 65","An individual Medigap policy before either plan","The large employer group plan"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For Medicare due to disability, a large group plan based on current employment generally pays first when the applicable 100-employee threshold is met.","id":"HM-B-9-4","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/who-pays-first"},{"title":"Medicare: skilled nursing facility care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care"},{"title":"Maryland Department of Health: Medicaid","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Pages/home.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"08","prompt":"A standalone vision plan covers routine exams and eyewear as scheduled. Which question best determines what it pays for new glasses?","options":["Whether the customer has ever held life insurance","What allowances, frequency limits and cost sharing the plan specifies","Whether the glasses were prescribed in the month of renewal","Whether the frames cost more than the entire annual premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Limited vision benefits follow their schedule and frequency restrictions. A benefit allowance can differ from the retail charge.","id":"HM-B-3-9","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1301 et seq., short-term limited duration insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1301"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"42 U.S.C. §300gg–91(c), categories of excepted benefits","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t42/s300gg-91/"}]},{"lesson":"05","prompt":"A buyer accepts an insurer’s prewritten policy without bargaining over its definitions. What explains why a genuinely ambiguous term may be construed against its drafter?","options":["The contract guarantees equal payments from both parties","The contract is one of adhesion","Every health contract is transferable without consent","Every application is a warranty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard-form insurance contracts are generally drafted by the insurer. Ambiguity can therefore be resolved in favor of the reasonable insured interpretation.","id":"HM-B-2-4","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Article §12–102, rescission for material misrepresentation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=12-102"}]},{"lesson":"39","prompt":"A Maryland small-group carrier wants to nonrenew a group just because several employees developed chronic illnesses. Which principle applies?","options":["Renewal may always be conditioned on new health statements","Claims alone are not a permissible nonrenewal reason","A high loss ratio permits nonrenewal of any single group","Only the employees who submitted claims must be excluded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guaranteed renewability limits the permissible reasons for ending the group’s coverage. A change in participants’ health is not by itself such a reason.","id":"HM-B-1-20","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §31–101, small employer definition","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201, small employer market definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating and availability","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"}]},{"lesson":"42","prompt":"A beneficiary spent three nights in a hospital entirely under outpatient observation. For the ordinary Original Medicare three-day inpatient requirement for skilled nursing facility coverage, how are those observation days treated?","options":["They do not count as inpatient days","They count only if the hospital is in Maryland","Two count if the patient saw a hospital physician","All three automatically count as an inpatient stay"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Observation is outpatient status. The ordinary skilled nursing facility rule refers to a qualifying inpatient stay, subject to applicable waivers.","id":"HM-B-9-6","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare costs","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"},{"title":"Medicare & You handbook","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/medicare-and-you"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"A producer tells a customer that she can change an insurer’s filed premium to cover her own operating costs. Which response is sound?","options":["Collect the filed premium and only lawfully permitted charges","Use a premium receipt to convert the surcharge into a filed rate","Add reasonable operating costs without disclosing a separate fee","Charge the surcharge if the prospect agreed to see multiple quotes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A producer cannot simply add agency expenses to a filed premium. Specific authorized fees have their own conditions and disclosures.","id":"HM-B-1-22","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]}]},{"id":"C","title":"Health Mock Exam C","minutes":105,"questions":[{"lesson":"55","prompt":"A producer poses as a patient to obtain confidential health information for an application. What kind of information-gathering practice does this describe?","options":["A signed authorization","A policyholder election","A pretext interview","A routine inspection report based on disclosed identity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Obtaining information under a false identity or false pretense raises the pretext-interview prohibition. Normal underwriting does not justify that deception.","id":"HM-C-1-21","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"A taxpayer who itemizes receives an insurance reimbursement for part of a qualifying dental bill in the same tax year. What amount is considered before applying the medical expense floor?","options":["Twice the amount paid by the taxpayer","Only the qualifying portion the taxpayer actually paid and was not reimbursed for","The original billed amount including the insurer’s payment","The maximum allowed by the dental plan regardless of payment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reimbursed expenses are excluded from the deductible medical expense calculation. The applicable adjusted-gross-income floor is applied afterward.","id":"HM-C-10-1","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"03","prompt":"A carrier pays a separate company to process claims but remains responsible for insured benefits. What is the separate company doing?","options":["Replacing the carrier as the policyholder","Third-party administration","Issuing a state certificate of authority to the carrier","Assuming the policyholder’s deductible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An administrator may process claims under contract. Providing administrative services alone does not transfer the underlying insurance obligation.","id":"HM-C-2-3","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101 definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"Health-General Article §19–705, HMO certificate of authority","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-705"}]},{"lesson":"08","prompt":"A hospital cash plan and a major medical plan cover the same person. Which statement accurately describes their different purposes?","options":["The two plans must use identical benefit schedules","Owning a cash plan removes all major medical deductibles","The cash plan pays its stated benefit and major medical pays covered expenses under its terms","The cash plan sets the major medical network rates"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fixed cash can supplement other resources during a covered event. Its payment schedule does not change the major medical contract.","id":"HM-C-3-10","domain":2,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1301 et seq., short-term limited duration insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1301"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"42 U.S.C. §300gg–91(c), categories of excepted benefits","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t42/s300gg-91/"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"For a plan subject to §15-418, a qualifying unmarried child dependent is offered lower benefits solely because she is the insured’s grandchild. What does the Maryland rule require?","options":["A separate individual contract at a special rate","A health exam before every renewal for grandchildren","The same applicable benefits and premium treatment as other covered dependents","Benefits limited to emergency services until age 18"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The statute defines eligible child dependents and provides parity of benefits and rates with other covered dependents under those plans.","id":"HM-C-1-24","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"19","prompt":"A deductible is met and a plan pays 70 percent of an allowed $900 service. What is the member’s share before considering any out-of-pocket cap?","options":["$270","$630","$90","$210"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The member pays the remaining 30 percent. Thirty percent of the $900 allowed service is $270.","id":"HM-C-6-1","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"01","prompt":"A warehouse worker has no interest in preventive safety because he assumes insurance will cover every injury. Which hazard is most directly associated with that attitude?","options":["Physical hazard","Collateral assignment","Catastrophic pooling","Morale hazard"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Indifference or carelessness arising from the presence of insurance is a morale hazard. It differs from deliberately fabricating a claim.","id":"HM-C-2-1","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 2.1 and 3.1","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"A claimant is physically unable to give notice within the usual period. What should be examined before concluding the claim is barred?","options":["Whether the insurer had approved the claimant for reinstatement","Whether the insured had already submitted a signed replacement application","Whether the premium grace period was still open on the notice date","Whether notice was given as soon as reasonably possible"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Notice provisions can allow for circumstances in which timely notice was not reasonably possible. The facts and contract language matter.","id":"HM-C-4-2","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"55","prompt":"An insurer proposes to share a customer’s nonpublic personal financial information with an unaffiliated marketer outside any exception. What must the customer have before covered sharing?","options":["A notice sent only after the first disclosure to the marketer","A claims authorization covering a different purpose","Notice and a reasonable opportunity to opt out","Permission from the producer in lieu of the customer’s rights"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Financial privacy rules generally require notice and an opportunity to opt out of covered nonaffiliated disclosures before sharing.","id":"HM-C-1-6","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"39","prompt":"A small employer sends a list of its employees’ diagnoses to negotiate a special surcharge for one worker. Which rating approach is appropriate under small-group medical rules?","options":["Exclude every worker with a chronic condition","Use permitted rating factors applicable to the group rather than a personal medical-history surcharge","Rate the individual solely on last year’s hospital charges","Make the group certificate depend on a negative medical test"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small-group medical rules restrict health-based underwriting. The group’s eligibility and pricing must follow the applicable lawful criteria.","id":"HM-C-7-5","domain":6,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §31–101, small employer definition","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201, small employer market definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating and availability","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"Under a Maryland policy subject to the newborn coverage rule, adding the baby does not increase the premium. What is wrong with denying coverage from birth because no extra premium arrived?","options":["No additional premium is due under that coverage","A new premium is always required for coverage to begin at birth","The insurer may delay newborn coverage until the first pediatric claim","Coverage must wait until the next premium billing date"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newborn coverage begins at birth. The notice-and-payment provision for an additional premium does not create a charge when no extra premium is required.","id":"HM-C-1-8","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"A licensed producer’s office assistant wants to earn a sales commission for explaining policy benefits to a prospect. What is the concern?","options":["The form of payment alone converts the work to clerical service","The assistant can use the employer’s line of authority","Compensation is allowed if the assistant works fewer than 20 hours","The proposed work and compensation involve licensed sales activity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Explaining coverage to induce a purchase is materially different from clerical support. Paying an unlicensed person a commission compounds that issue.","id":"HM-C-1-5","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"50","prompt":"A resident producer needs 24 hours of continuing education, including three hours in ethics. She has 24 hours of ordinary coursework and no ethics credit. What is missing?","options":["Nothing, because only the total matters","Another 24 hours of ordinary coursework","The required ethics coursework","A separate license for each course"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both the total and the required subject matter matter. Ordinary coursework alone cannot satisfy a specific ethics requirement.","id":"HM-C-1-2","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–116, continuing education","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-116"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–115, renewal and reinstatement","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-115"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, reporting of criminal and administrative actions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"}]},{"lesson":"49","prompt":"An insurance agency proposes paying an outside consultant to negotiate health policies for customers. What should it check first?","options":["Individual licensing for the work and line involved","The label used on the consultant’s invoices to the agency","Whether a salaried supervisor will review the closed sales","Whether permission to negotiate is written into the client agreement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calling someone a consultant does not change the substance of negotiating insurance. Licensing follows the activity performed.","id":"HM-C-1-14","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–103, license required and exceptions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-103"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–104 and §10–109, qualifications and examination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-104"},{"title":"MIA Bulletin 24-19, repeal of prelicensing education","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Pages/Bulletins/24-19-Repealing-the-Prelicensing-Education-and-Experience-Requirements.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"12","prompt":"An insurer receives a coded medical information alert suggesting a prior condition. What is the appropriate use of that alert?","options":["Verify the relevant facts with authorized sources","Adopt the alert as conclusive proof of a diagnosis","Assume the alert discloses the condition’s current severity","Rate the applicant solely on how many codes appear"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An alert is a lead for verification. The insurer must ground its decision in appropriate, accurate underwriting information.","id":"HM-C-3-5","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"MIB Group: consumer information","url":"https://www.mib.com/consumer_information.html"},{"title":"Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 USC 1681","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act"},{"title":"Health-General Article §18–336, HIV testing informed consent","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=18-336"}]},{"lesson":"13","prompt":"A disability applicant is offered a higher-than-standard premium because a lawful risk factor raises expected losses. Which underwriting action is illustrated?","options":["A guaranteed-issue standard offer","A substandard or rated offer","An absolute assignment","A full rescission"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rated offer accepts the applicant at adjusted terms. It differs from declining coverage altogether.","id":"HM-C-3-6","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–504, discrimination against victims of abuse","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-504"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–909, genetic information","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-909"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–208, unfair discrimination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-208"}]},{"lesson":"35","prompt":"A hospital network administers claims for an employer, but the employer pays the actual health claims from its assets. Who bears the ordinary claims risk?","options":["The participants’ Medicare supplement carrier","Every producer appointed by the administrator","The self-funded employer","The employee’s treating provider automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A self-funded employer retains the claims obligation. An administrator can process those claims without assuming that risk.","id":"HM-C-7-1","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"40","prompt":"Before an expensive covered bridge is placed, the dentist submits a treatment plan for an estimate of benefits. What is the practical purpose of predetermination?","options":["To remove annual benefit maximums","To transfer the dentist’s license to the insurer","To estimate coverage and expected patient cost under the plan","To guarantee eligibility even if coverage ends before the service"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Predetermination helps the patient understand expected benefits. Final payment remains subject to eligibility, the actual treatment and the contract.","id":"HM-C-8-2","domain":7,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: dental coverage in the marketplace","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/dental-coverage/"}]},{"lesson":"55","prompt":"A customer receives a privacy notice and asks why the insurer keeps records about her. Which answer best describes the purpose of the notice?","options":["How the insurer collects, uses and discloses information","A blanket authorization for future disclosures of medical records","A substitute for an adverse underwriting decision notice","The insurer’s guarantee that all outside sharing is forbidden"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An information-practices notice explains how customer data are handled and the relevant rights. It does not by itself authorize every disclosure.","id":"HM-C-1-17","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 4, Subtitle 4, insurance information and privacy protection","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=4-401"},{"title":"COMAR 31.16.08, privacy of consumer financial and health information","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-16/chapter-31-16-08/"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"25","prompt":"A worker is considering a nonqualified HSA distribution at age 40 and has no exception. Which consequence should be considered?","options":["A requirement to return the entire HSA balance to the employer","Automatic reimbursement from the HDHP","An unlimited tax-free distribution because the money belongs to the worker","Taxable income and generally an additional 20 percent tax"],"answer":3,"explanation":"HSA ownership does not make every withdrawal tax free. Qualified medical expenses receive different federal tax treatment.","id":"HM-C-6-6","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-19, 2026 HSA and HDHP amounts","url":"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-19.pdf"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"}]},{"lesson":"33","prompt":"A disabled shop owner submits business rent, employee wages and her own lost profit under an overhead expense policy. Which expense is normally outside its purpose?","options":["Covered utility charges","Her own lost profit","Covered shop rent","Covered nonowner employee wages"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Overhead coverage helps maintain eligible operating expenses. The owner’s lost earnings require separate income protection.","id":"HM-C-5-4","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics: disability insurance in employee benefits","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ebs/"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: business disability coverage","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"05","prompt":"After a policy is issued, only the insurer has made a legally enforceable promise to pay covered claims. What is this characteristic called?","options":["Indemnity","Contributory","Unilateral","Coordination"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a unilateral insurance contract the insurer promises performance if the contract’s conditions are met. The owner can generally stop paying and let coverage lapse.","id":"HM-C-2-4","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Article §12–102, rescission for material misrepresentation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=12-102"}]},{"lesson":"10","prompt":"A producer uses an insurer’s brochure but adds an unsupported claim that approval is automatic. Who should correct the altered sales presentation?","options":["The underwriting department after an application is submitted","Only the original author of the underlying insurer brochure","The applicant when the issued policy differs from the advertisement","The producer using the misleading presentation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A producer’s addition can make a presentation misleading. Approved source material does not validate an unsupported claim added later.","id":"HM-C-3-8","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202, misrepresentation and false advertising","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://www.mdlifega.org/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 9, Subtitle 4, Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=9-403"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"A taxpayer pays a personally owned disability income premium solely to replace wages if disabled. Is that premium generally an itemized medical expense?","options":["Yes, all premiums for disability are deductible medical premiums","Generally no, this premium insures against lost earnings","Yes, whenever the taxpayer itemizes other medical expenses","Yes, limited to the portion of premium for nonoccupational loss"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A personal income-replacement premium is distinguished from qualifying medical coverage. The nature of the protection determines its treatment.","id":"HM-C-10-3","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"26","prompt":"A participant’s plan wishes to set a lifetime dollar cap on essential health benefits. Which federal protection should be considered?","options":["The availability of a disability waiver rider","The tax exclusion for disability benefits","The birthday rule for Medicare supplements","The prohibition on lifetime dollar limits for essential health benefits in covered plans"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Federal law restricts lifetime dollar limits on essential health benefits. This rule addresses a benefit cap, distinct from a deductible or copayment.","id":"HM-C-6-7","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: essential health benefits","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/what-marketplace-plans-cover/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: grandfathered plans","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/health-care-law-protections/grandfathered-plans/"}]},{"lesson":"51","prompt":"A producer leaves one agency for another and changes the logo on her email. What should she check before acting for a carrier through the new agency?","options":["The necessary agency arrangement and carrier appointment","Whether the new agency has a contract with another carrier","Whether her former employer continues to list her on old policies","Whether her license renewal date is the same as her old appointment date"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A change of agencies does not automatically transfer authority to act for a carrier. The producer needs the applicable appointment and authority.","id":"HM-C-1-13","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–118, appointments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-118"},{"title":"Insurance Article §1–101, definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=1-101"}]},{"lesson":"45","prompt":"A Maryland Medigap owner in the birthday window wants to upgrade to a more generous plan without underwriting. What limit should the producer explain?","options":["The rule is limited to the current insurer’s highest-benefit plan","All upgrades must be offered without underwriting","The birthday protection applies to equal or lesser benefits","The birthday window applies only to Medicare Advantage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The state’s birthday opportunity does not guarantee a switch to richer benefits. The proposed replacement benefits must meet the statutory comparison.","id":"HM-C-1-9","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–906, Medicare supplement minimum standards","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-906"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–909, guaranteed issue and birthday window","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-909"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–910, 30-day right to return","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-910"}]},{"lesson":"48","prompt":"A buyer asks whether the long-term care insurer may use a prior hospital stay as the only way to qualify for covered care under Maryland rules. What should the producer explain?","options":["Every nursing facility benefit requires three inpatient nights","Eligibility cannot be conditioned on a prior hospitalization under that rule","Every claim requires a hospital discharge within the previous week","Only Medicare beneficiaries can use home care benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Private long-term care benefit triggers address functional or cognitive needs. The Medicare skilled facility stay rule is not a required private long-term care trigger.","id":"HM-C-1-25","domain":0,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, Long-Term Care Insurance","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §18–104 and §18–105, applications and applicants 80 and older","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=18-104"},{"title":"Tax-General Article §10–718, long-term care insurance credit","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gtg&section=10-718"}]},{"lesson":"06","prompt":"An injury creates treatment costs but the client continues working at full pay. What loss is clearly present?","options":["A total loss of earned income","A permanent dismemberment","Medical expense","A loss of business ownership"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The treatment bill is a medical expense. An injury does not automatically establish income loss or permanent loss of a body part.","id":"HM-C-3-1","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 3.2 and 3.3","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"A clinic is told its business will be boycotted unless it induces patients to buy a particular policy. Which market conduct issue is implicated?","options":["Coordination of benefits","A standard proof-of-loss requirement","Coercion and intimidation","Lawful risk classification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Threatening economic pressure to control insurance choices can constitute coercion. It differs from explaining contractual benefits or prices.","id":"HM-C-1-16","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"28","prompt":"A contract changes from an own-occupation definition for the first two years to an any-suitable-occupation definition afterward. What may change at that point?","options":["The date on which the disability began","The occupational test for continued benefits","The length of the original elimination period","The previously established monthly maximum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After the definition changes, the ability to perform other work consistent with the contractual standard can affect continued eligibility.","id":"HM-C-5-1","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"43","prompt":"A beneficiary qualifies for a Part D late penalty. What figure is used as the base for the percentage calculation?","options":["The deductible on the beneficiary’s Medigap plan","The national base beneficiary premium","The beneficiary’s total pharmacy bills for the year","The income of the beneficiary’s prescribing physician"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The penalty calculation uses the national base beneficiary premium and the number of full uncovered months. It is not based on a particular prescription bill.","id":"HM-C-9-8","domain":8,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare Advantage plans","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: drug coverage (Part D)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: Part D late enrollment penalty","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties"}]},{"lesson":"46","prompt":"A retiree has Original Medicare and ordinary retiree coverage, with no current employment coverage or other special coordination rule. Which is generally primary?","options":["The former employer’s retiree plan in every case","The former employer’s workers’ compensation regardless of injury","A Medicare supplement before Medicare","Medicare"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Retiree coverage generally pays after Medicare. Coverage based on current employment can have a different payment order.","id":"HM-C-9-4","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/who-pays-first"},{"title":"Medicare: skilled nursing facility care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care"},{"title":"Maryland Department of Health: Medicaid","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Pages/home.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"17","prompt":"A policyholder changes from office work to a more hazardous job. Which optional provision can adjust the benefit if the policy’s conditions are met?","options":["Time of payment of claims","Change of occupation","Entire contract","Claim forms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An occupation-change provision sets how benefits or premiums respond when the insured moves between occupational risk classes.","id":"HM-C-4-6","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, interest on late claim payments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"A disability plan’s premium was funded 60 percent by an employer with excluded contributions and 40 percent by the employee with after-tax money throughout the relevant period. What portion of a $2,000 benefit is generally taxable under those assumptions?","options":["$1,200","$0","$2,000","$800"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The taxable portion follows the employer-funded 60 percent in this example. Sixty percent of $2,000 is $1,200.","id":"HM-C-10-2","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"46","prompt":"An older applicant needs nursing facility care and asks how Maryland Medicaid can help. Which two categories of requirements are central?","options":["A private disability elimination period and a return-of-premium rider","Medicare Part D enrollment and a Medigap birthday window","An active producer license and a qualifying HSA","Financial and medical eligibility requirements"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Maryland Medicaid long-term care eligibility depends on applicable financial and medical criteria. Age or residence in a facility alone does not establish eligibility.","id":"HM-C-9-7","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how Medicare works with other insurance","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/who-pays-first"},{"title":"Medicare: skilled nursing facility care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/skilled-nursing-facility-care"},{"title":"Maryland Department of Health: Medicaid","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/Pages/home.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"04","prompt":"An agent privately tells a client he can erase a policy exclusion, but the insurer never granted or suggested such power. What should the client request before relying on the change?","options":["A change requested by the applicant but never accepted","A producer’s verbal assurance at the sales interview","The insurer’s receipt for the original, unmodified contract","An insurer-authorized endorsement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An agent cannot create authority merely by claiming it. Policy modifications must comply with the contract and the authority actually granted.","id":"HM-C-2-5","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–101 definitions of insurance producer","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-101"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"15","prompt":"In a policy with a time-limit-on-certain-defenses provision, why does the length of time since issue matter to a claim?","options":["The provision limits when the insurer can rely on certain defenses to coverage","It renews all lapsed policies without payment","It eliminates every exclusion when the first month ends","It changes the policy’s beneficiary every year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The time-limit provision restricts use of specified defenses after its stated period. It does not erase all exclusions or create new benefits.","id":"HM-C-4-1","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"Which example is a fair comparison when a producer discusses replacement?","options":["Promising the new insurer will always pay faster","Quoting a competitor’s expired brochure as its current policy","Using both contracts to compare covered services, exclusions and waiting periods","Comparing only the new premium while concealing a new exclusion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fair comparison uses accurate current terms and material differences. A favorable premium alone does not describe the coverage being exchanged.","id":"HM-C-1-3","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"37","prompt":"An employee’s spouse loses qualifying job-based coverage. Why might the employee be permitted to add the spouse between annual enrollment periods?","options":["The loss may create a special enrollment right if conditions and the request deadline are met","All group plan enrollment dates are optional","The employee can backdate any dependent change without a request","A spouse must enroll first in Medicare to get group coverage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Loss of other coverage can open a special enrollment period. The event and timely request matter.","id":"HM-C-7-3","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: special enrollment","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/portability"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependent coverage","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"}]},{"lesson":"40","prompt":"A plan covers preventive cleanings twice per year but major restorations at a different rate. Which service would normally be checked against the preventive frequency limit?","options":["A root canal","A routine cleaning","An orthodontic adjustment","A replacement bridge"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Routine prophylaxis is preventive. Bridges, root canals and orthodontic treatment fall into other categories with their own provisions.","id":"HM-C-8-1","domain":7,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: dental coverage in the marketplace","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/dental-coverage/"}]},{"lesson":"23","prompt":"An insurer examines an inpatient file after discharge to assess whether services met its medical necessity standards. What is this process?","options":["An eligibility waiting period","Retrospective utilization review","A concurrent stay review","A prospective authorization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Review after the care has been delivered is retrospective. A prospective decision is made before treatment.","id":"HM-C-6-12","domain":5,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (model 120)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–10A, utilization review and grievances","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-10A-01"}]},{"lesson":"43","prompt":"A Medicare Advantage enrollee chooses a routine nonemergency specialist outside her HMO network. What should she check before expecting payment?","options":["Only the Original Medicare Part A hospital deductible","Whether the physician sells Medigap","Whether the current calendar month has 31 days","The plan’s referral, network and out-of-network benefit rules"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Medicare Advantage plan design affects network and referral requirements. Covered emergencies are treated differently from routine elective care.","id":"HM-C-9-2","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare Advantage plans","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/health-plans"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: drug coverage (Part D)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: Part D late enrollment penalty","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties"}]},{"lesson":"38","prompt":"A former employee assumes COBRA continuation is free because the employer previously paid the full active-employee premium. What should the producer explain?","options":["The applicable continuation premium may be charged","The beneficiary pays nothing until the former employer’s next renewal","The former employer must cover the full cost during the election period","The old payroll deduction fixes the maximum charge during continuation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"COBRA protects access to continuation. It generally does not require the employer to keep subsidizing that coverage.","id":"HM-C-7-4","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–409, continuation after loss of employment","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-409"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–407, continuation after death","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-407"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–408, continuation after divorce","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-408"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: COBRA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"}]},{"lesson":"05","prompt":"An expense policy reimburses a covered $700 loss, subject to its terms. Which principle explains using the actual loss as the starting point?","options":["Speculative risk","Indemnity","Estoppel","Apparent authority"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Indemnity aims to restore a financial loss. A fixed-benefit policy uses a stated scheduled amount, which is a different benefit design.","id":"HM-C-2-8","domain":1,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Insurance Article §12–102, rescission for material misrepresentation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=12-102"}]},{"lesson":"21","prompt":"An HMO pays a physician a set monthly amount for each assigned member, independent of how many routine visits each member makes. What is this payment method?","options":["Fee for service","Per diem paid per hospital day","An indemnity benefit paid to the patient","Capitation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Capitation pays a set amount per member for an agreed period. Fee for service instead pays for individual services.","id":"HM-C-6-3","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Health-General Article Title 19, Subtitle 7, health maintenance organizations","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-701"},{"title":"COMAR 31.12.07, HMO regulations","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-12/chapter-31-12-07/"}]},{"lesson":"19","prompt":"After cost sharing on a covered claim, a member is $100 short of the in-network annual out-of-pocket maximum. The next covered in-network service would otherwise require $290 in cost sharing. What is the most she owes toward that service?","options":["$290","$190","$100","$390"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only $100 remains before the applicable maximum. Once reached, the plan pays covered in-network costs for the remainder of that plan year.","id":"HM-C-6-11","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: out-of-pocket maximum","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: deductible","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"A medical expense benefit is paid directly to a hospital under a valid assignment. Which issue is illustrated?","options":["When the insured first satisfied the deductible","Whether the service was medically necessary","How much of the claim is an eligible medical expense","Who is entitled to receive the payment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The payment-of-claims provision and any valid assignment determine who receives benefits. Payment to a provider does not increase the covered amount.","id":"HM-C-4-4","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"27","prompt":"A family compares two plans with the same metal level. Which feature still needs to be checked against its needs?","options":["Whether its doctors and prescriptions are covered on the desired terms","Whether every covered prescription has a zero copay","Whether the metal level guarantees identical networks","Whether each family pays the published actuarial percentage exactly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Metal level does not standardize every network, formulary or cost-sharing detail. Those differences can materially affect the family.","id":"HM-C-6-8","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov: plan categories","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/choose-a-plan/plans-categories/"},{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Maryland Insurance Administration: appeals and grievances","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/AppealsandGrievances.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"18","prompt":"A family compares a plan with a lower premium and a higher deductible to one with a higher premium and a lower deductible. What does the higher deductible change?","options":["The share of covered expense the family generally pays before the plan begins sharing those costs","The set of diagnoses a provider can record","The number of months in the policy year","The amount the insurer must pay when no services are used"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A deductible allocates an initial amount of covered expense to the member. Some services may be covered before the deductible under plan terms or law.","id":"HM-C-6-2","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"57","prompt":"A person pays an eligible provider bill using an HSA distribution and asks whether the same amount can also count toward an itemized federal medical deduction. Which answer is correct?","options":["The deduction always doubles because the expense was qualified","It can be deducted again only in a later month of the same year","The expense cannot produce both tax-free HSA reimbursement and a medical deduction for the same dollars","It can be deducted again if the HSA is held at another bank"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The tax rules prevent a double benefit for the same expense. A tax-free HSA reimbursement removes that amount from the unreimbursed expense calculation.","id":"HM-C-10-5","domain":9,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 502, medical and dental expenses","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"IRS Publication 969, HSAs and other tax-favored health plans","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"54","prompt":"At a sales meeting a producer knowingly makes up a dividend history to persuade shoppers to enroll. What conduct is at issue?","options":["A medical information request","Payment of earned commissions","Legitimate field underwriting","Misrepresentation of policy benefits or results"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Materially false information used to sell insurance is misrepresentation. Calling the numbers an illustration does not make the fabricated history true.","id":"HM-C-1-23","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202 through §27–213, unfair trade practices","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, rebates and inducements","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–213, twisting","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-213"}]},{"lesson":"01","prompt":"Which exposure is an example of pure risk for health insurance?","options":["The chance that a property sale earns a profit","The chance that a new business doubles in value","The possibility of profit or loss from trading stock","The possibility of an injury that creates medical expenses"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pure risk offers the possibility of loss or no loss. A speculative risk also includes a possible gain.","id":"HM-C-2-6","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 2.1 and 3.1","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"An insurer denies a claim and the consumer believes Maryland insurance law was violated. Which agency ordinarily handles insurance complaints in the state?","options":["Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation","National Association of Insurance Commissioners","Maryland Insurance Administration","Maryland Health Benefit Exchange"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Maryland Insurance Administration regulates insurers and handles insurance complaints. The marketplace assists with enrollment and has a different role.","id":"HM-C-1-20","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"39","prompt":"A business asks whether the diagnosis of its newest employee may determine that worker’s individual medical rate under small-group rating rules. What should the producer explain?","options":["The claim amount sets the worker’s premium dollar for dollar","The employee must obtain a disability policy before joining","Individual health status is not a permitted rating factor for that coverage","Employers set a medical surcharge for each worker’s condition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Small-group medical rating uses permitted factors. An employee’s diagnosis or individual claims history cannot simply be added as a personal risk surcharge.","id":"HM-C-1-11","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §31–101, small employer definition","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201, small employer market definitions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1205, small group rating and availability","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1205"}]},{"lesson":"11","prompt":"A producer notices that the birth date on an unsigned application conflicts with the client’s identification. What should happen before submission?","options":["Verify the correct date with the applicant","Use the age on the quote so the stated premium stays the same","Use the younger age until the insurer independently discovers the error","Transmit the application first and correct it only if a claim is filed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Field underwriting includes checking completeness and accuracy. A material discrepancy should be addressed before the applicant signs.","id":"HM-C-3-4","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–209, producer conduct","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-209"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"}]},{"lesson":"34","prompt":"A claimant has limited resources but lacks sufficient work credits for Social Security Disability Insurance. Which program uses a needs-based framework for eligible disabled people?","options":["A federal COBRA plan","Supplemental Security Income","Workers’ compensation for every illness","Medicare supplement Plan G"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SSI considers financial need and eligibility requirements. SSDI is based in part on the worker’s covered earnings and insured status.","id":"HM-C-5-5","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Social Security Administration: disability benefits","url":"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"},{"title":"Maryland Workers’ Compensation Commission","url":"https://www.wcc.state.md.us/"}]},{"lesson":"47","prompt":"A long-term care policy has a 30-day elimination period measured in covered service days. The insured receives services on 30 qualifying days over six weeks. Which measure controls the waiting period?","options":["The date the insured signed the original application","The 30 qualifying service days under the policy","The date the caregiver first requested reimbursement","Exactly one calendar month after issue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Some contracts count days of service, while others count calendar days. The stated service-day design governs this example.","id":"HM-C-9-5","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Long-Term Care Insurance Partnership Program","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/LongTermCarePartnership.aspx"},{"title":"IRS Publication 502, long-term care premiums and per diem limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"Maryland Medicaid: long-term services and supports","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/longtermcare/Pages/Home.aspx"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: long-term care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care"}]},{"lesson":"08","prompt":"A policy schedule pays $400 for a covered outpatient procedure. The provider bills $720. What determines the policy’s benefit?","options":["The $400 scheduled amount","The difference between the charge and the premium","The insured’s monthly earnings","The provider’s full $720 charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A scheduled or fixed benefit pays the specified amount for the covered event. The balance remains subject to any other coverage or arrangement.","id":"HM-C-3-2","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1301 et seq., short-term limited duration insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1301"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"42 U.S.C. §300gg–91(c), categories of excepted benefits","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t42/s300gg-91/"}]},{"lesson":"53","prompt":"An agency is behind on rent and wants to borrow client premiums for two days. Who bears the fiduciary duty while the agency has the money?","options":["The insurer only after it physically receives the premium","The bank solely because the deposit was made there","The client until the policy is delivered to the agency","The agency holding the premium funds"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A short repayment plan does not change ownership of fiduciary funds. The party holding those funds must preserve them for the people to whom they belong.","id":"HM-C-1-7","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §2–102 and §2–103, Administration and Commissioner","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-102"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–130, compensation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-130"},{"title":"COMAR 31.03.03, producer premium accounts","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-03/chapter-31-03-03/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–216, fees and charges","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-216"}]},{"lesson":"06","prompt":"A client wants coverage for a defined dollar amount upon accidental death and asks whether medical receipts determine the amount. What should the producer explain?","options":["It equals the medical deductible plus coinsurance","The accidental death benefit is generally the amount stated for the covered loss","It always equals the final emergency room bill","It depends on the family’s out-of-pocket maximum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accidental death is a stipulated benefit triggered under the policy’s definition. Medical expense reimbursement uses a different basis.","id":"HM-C-3-9","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"},{"title":"Prometric outline, sections 3.2 and 3.3","url":"https://www.prometric.com/sites/default/files/2021-09/2024_accident_and_health_or_sickness_producer_9.21.21.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"07","prompt":"An association develops educational standards for underwriters. Which role remains with a state regulator?","options":["Enforcing state insurance law","Setting the association’s meeting agenda","Editing all commercial training manuals","Electing the association’s private officers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Professional organizations support education and practice. A state regulator exercises the enforcement authority granted by state law.","id":"HM-C-2-7","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Fair Credit Reporting Act, 15 USC 1681 et seq.","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/fair-credit-reporting-act"},{"title":"18 USC 1033 and 1034","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1033"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: COBRA continuation coverage","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/cobra"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: ERISA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/erisa"}]},{"lesson":"20","prompt":"A basic surgical expense schedule lists $750 for a specific operation. A surgeon charges $1,050. What does the schedule establish before other provisions are applied?","options":["The surgeon’s right to collect only $300","An automatic increase to $1,800","A required payment of the full $1,050","A scheduled benefit of $750"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A schedule states the benefit for the procedure. The contract determines adjustments and the provider arrangement determines what may be billed.","id":"HM-C-6-10","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article Title 14, nonprofit health service plans","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=14-101"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary: fixed indemnity","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"}]},{"lesson":"24","prompt":"A group plan timely receives a qualifying special enrollment request after a child is born. Which event is the relevant trigger?","options":["An employee reaching a new salary band","Birth of a dependent","An announcement of next year’s deductible","A change in the provider used for routine visits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Birth can create group-plan special enrollment rights. The family must follow the plan’s applicable deadline and enrollment process.","id":"HM-C-6-9","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: HIPAA portability","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans/portability"},{"title":"HHS: HIPAA privacy rule summary","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/laws-regulations/index.html"}]},{"lesson":"29","prompt":"A claimant meets a policy’s elimination period but recovers four months later. Which provision limits how long the insurer pays even if disability continues longer?","options":["Free-look period","Grace period for the next premium","Benefit period","Application review period"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The benefit period is the maximum payable duration for a qualifying disability. Recovery may stop benefits earlier.","id":"HM-C-5-2","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"16","prompt":"Why should a claimant retain copies of a physician’s statement and itemized bills sent to an insurer?","options":["They document the facts and amount offered as proof of loss","They replace the policy’s coverage terms","They establish a new open enrollment period","They make every charge payable at its billed price"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Claim documents support evaluation of a loss and provide a record of what was submitted. Benefits still follow the contract.","id":"HM-C-4-7","domain":3,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, prompt payment of claims","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"02","prompt":"A family sets aside savings for the first $1,000 of covered medical bills and relies on its plan above that amount. Which part is the family retaining?","options":["Every loss of every plan member","The carrier’s administrative expenses","The amount it finances through the deductible","All risk above the annual maximum"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A deductible leaves an initial layer of expense with the insured. The contract determines how later expenses are shared.","id":"HM-C-2-2","domain":1,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Society of Actuaries — Risk and Insurance","url":"https://www.soa.org/globalassets/assets/files/edu/P-21-05.pdf"},{"title":"NAIC: A Regulator’s Introduction to the Insurance Industry","url":"https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/prod_serv_marketreg_rii_zb.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"47","prompt":"A policy offers inflation protection that increases the daily benefit each year. What risk is this option intended to address?","options":["The need to substitute Medicare for private insurance","Care costs rising during many years of ownership","The monthly fluctuation in the producer’s commission","The possibility the insured changes beneficiaries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inflation protection helps preserve the buying power of long-term care benefits. The actual increases follow the contract.","id":"HM-C-9-6","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Long-Term Care Insurance Partnership Program","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Consumer/Pages/LongTermCarePartnership.aspx"},{"title":"IRS Publication 502, long-term care premiums and per diem limits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"},{"title":"Maryland Medicaid: long-term services and supports","url":"https://health.maryland.gov/mmcp/longtermcare/Pages/Home.aspx"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: long-term care","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/long-term-care"}]},{"lesson":"30","prompt":"A proportional residual benefit equals the percentage of earnings lost times a $5,000 full benefit. An insured’s monthly earnings drop from $8,000 to $6,000. What is payable under that formula?","options":["$3,750","$1,250","$5,000","$2,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The earnings loss is $2,000 out of $8,000, or 25 percent. Applying that percentage to $5,000 gives $1,250.","id":"HM-C-5-3","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Social Security Administration: disability benefits","url":"https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"A Maryland producer deposits a client’s refund into his own account and refuses to return it. What is the clearest basis for discipline?","options":["Classifying the carrier as foreign","Charging a higher actuarial rate class","Misappropriating money belonging to another person","Delivering a policy by mail"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Money held for a client or insurer must be handled in a fiduciary capacity. Converting it to personal use can support disciplinary action.","id":"HM-C-1-4","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"17","prompt":"A policy lets the insurer terminate coverage during the policy term on the notice specified in the contract. How is this policy described?","options":["Cancelable","Noncancelable","Guaranteed renewable at an unchanged rate","Paid-up"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A cancelable policy allows termination on the stated terms during the policy period. Renewal categories generally address continuation at the next renewal.","id":"HM-C-4-3","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1005, interest on late claim payments","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1005"}]},{"lesson":"14","prompt":"A proposed replacement has an attractive premium but no confirmed effective date. What should the client do before cancelling current coverage?","options":["Assume the submitted replacement application covers all intervening losses","Confirm issuance, terms and effective date","Rely on the date the old insurer received a cancellation request","Treat the rate quotation as confirmation of coverage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An application or quote does not necessarily put coverage in force. Verifying the new policy helps prevent a gap.","id":"HM-C-3-7","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–909, replacement of Medicare supplement policies","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-909"},{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, long-term care insurance replacement","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"}]},{"lesson":"42","prompt":"A worker thinks a Medicare card makes every prescription obtained at a retail pharmacy a Part A service. Which coverage usually addresses outpatient retail prescriptions?","options":["Part B for every self-administered retail drug","Part D, subject to the plan’s coverage rules","Part A solely because the medication was prescribed","A Medigap deductible in all cases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Part D is the principal outpatient prescription benefit. Some specific drugs or settings are covered under other Medicare parts.","id":"HM-C-9-1","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: Medicare costs","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs"},{"title":"Medicare.gov: when to sign up","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start"},{"title":"Medicare & You handbook","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/medicare-and-you"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"After a producer’s license is revoked, the producer says the end of a calendar month automatically restores it. What is the relevant distinction?","options":["A revoked license requires the applicable restoration process","A new agency contract restores the revoked producer’s authority","Revocation suspends only appointments with the current carrier","Revocation automatically ends when the next renewal fee is paid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Revocation terminates authority. It differs from a temporary suspension with a defined end or conditions for return.","id":"HM-C-1-22","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"23","prompt":"Two employed spouses cover each other on their respective employer plans. For an ordinary claim for one spouse, which plan is generally primary for that individual?","options":["Whichever plan has the smaller deductible","Whichever spouse has the higher income","The plan covering that person as an employee","The plan covering that person only as a dependent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Under typical coordination rules, an employee’s own plan pays before a plan covering that person as a dependent. Additional rules cover other circumstances.","id":"HM-C-6-5","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (model 120)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–10A, utilization review and grievances","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-10A-01"}]},{"lesson":"56","prompt":"A parent plans to enroll an adopted child. For Maryland coverage tied to placement pending adoption, which event can trigger protection before a final decree?","options":["Assuming custody pending the adoption","The first annual policy renewal after the adoption","The mailing of the insurer’s annual report","The first birthday following the decree"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maryland recognizes qualifying placement or assumption of custody pending adoption. A family does not always have to wait for a final decree for coverage to begin.","id":"HM-C-1-18","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–401, newborn and adopted children","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-401"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–405, medical child support orders","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-405"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–418, child dependents to age 25","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-418"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–802, mental health and substance use parity","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–810, in vitro fertilization","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-810"},{"title":"Insurance Article §15–838, hearing aids for minors","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-838"}]},{"lesson":"33","prompt":"A group disability plan replaces 60 percent of monthly salary, capped at $3,500 before offsets. A worker earns $7,500 monthly. What is the maximum initial benefit?","options":["$3,000","$4,500","$7,500","$3,500"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sixty percent of $7,500 is $4,500. The plan’s stated monthly cap reduces that figure to $3,500.","id":"HM-C-5-8","domain":4,"scored":false,"sources":[{"title":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics: disability insurance in employee benefits","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ebs/"},{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: business disability coverage","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"}]},{"lesson":"41","prompt":"A parent assumes a Maryland Medicaid application must wait for the private marketplace’s annual open enrollment. What should the assister explain?","options":["Private open enrollment governs every public health program","Medicaid applications can be made year-round","A Medicaid applicant must first exhaust a COBRA period","Only an employer may submit a Medicaid application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Private marketplace enrollment windows and Medicaid eligibility operate under different rules. The family can apply for Medicaid outside annual open enrollment.","id":"HM-C-1-12","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Maryland Health Connection","url":"https://www.marylandhealthconnection.gov/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 31, Maryland Health Benefit Exchange","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=31-101"}]},{"lesson":"09","prompt":"A comprehensive medical applicant has asthma that began two years ago. Which statement about a pre-existing condition exclusion is appropriate for an ACA-compliant individual plan?","options":["It cannot exclude treatment merely because the asthma existed before enrollment","It can cover asthma only if the producer waives a rider","It must exclude asthma for the first six months","It can exclude asthma for life whenever the applicant disclosed it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ACA-compliant comprehensive coverage protects pre-existing conditions. Ordinary plan rules for covered services and cost sharing still apply.","id":"HM-C-3-3","domain":2,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–814, reconstructive breast surgery","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-814"},{"title":"HealthCare.gov: pre-existing conditions","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage/pre-existing-conditions/"}]},{"lesson":"49","prompt":"A Maryland applicant wants to sell accident and health insurance. Passing the required exam is complete. When may the applicant begin soliciting?","options":["As soon as the application fee is mailed","When the first prospect asks for a quote","Immediately upon leaving the test center","Once the appropriate producer license has been issued and other applicable requirements are met"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An exam result supports a license application. It does not by itself grant authority to transact insurance.","id":"HM-C-1-1","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–103, license required and exceptions","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-103"},{"title":"Insurance Article §10–104 and §10–109, qualifications and examination","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-104"},{"title":"MIA Bulletin 24-19, repeal of prelicensing education","url":"https://insurance.maryland.gov/Pages/Bulletins/24-19-Repealing-the-Prelicensing-Education-and-Experience-Requirements.aspx"}]},{"lesson":"10","prompt":"A producer tells a buyer that an insurer’s independent financial strength rating guarantees the State will pay every claim. What needs correction?","options":["The producer may make that guarantee if the rating is recent","Every rating is a legally binding claim guarantee","A rating is an opinion about financial strength and cannot promise State payment","Ratings automatically waive contract exclusions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A financial rating is not the policy and does not establish a State guarantee. Coverage depends on the insurance contract and applicable law.","id":"HM-C-1-19","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §27–202, misrepresentation and false advertising","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-202"},{"title":"Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://www.mdlifega.org/"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 9, Subtitle 4, Guaranty Corporation","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=9-403"}]},{"lesson":"31","prompt":"A disability policy waives premiums after the insured meets the waiver conditions. What financial obligation does the waiver address?","options":["Premiums required to maintain that policy during the qualifying period","All charges at out-of-network hospitals","All household debts during disability","The employer’s payroll taxes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Waiver of premium relieves the policy premium under its terms. It is separate from the monthly income benefit.","id":"HM-C-5-6","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance riders","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"15","prompt":"Under a standard reinstatement provision, a reinstated individual policy covers accidental injury immediately but sickness only after a stated waiting period. Which new loss can qualify right away?","options":["A covered accidental injury occurring after reinstatement","Every illness during the waiting period","A sickness that began before the policy was reinstated","A loss that occurred while the policy was lapsed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reinstatement can distinguish subsequent accidental injury from sickness. A lapse-period loss is not made retroactively payable by the standard provision.","id":"HM-C-4-5","domain":3,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"NAIC Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provision Law (model 180)","url":"https://content.naic.org/model-laws"},{"title":"Insurance Article Title 15, Subtitle 2, individual health insurance","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-201"}]},{"lesson":"44","prompt":"A client has Original Medicare and a supplement. Who generally pays first for a Medicare-covered medical claim?","options":["Whichever provider submitted a bill first","Original Medicare","The producer who sold the supplement","The Medigap carrier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Medigap supplements Original Medicare cost sharing according to the plan. It does not replace Medicare as the primary payer for a covered service.","id":"HM-C-9-3","domain":8,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Medicare.gov: how to compare Medigap policies","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap/basics/compare-plan-benefits"},{"title":"Choosing a Medigap Policy (CMS guide)","url":"https://www.medicare.gov/publications/02110-medigap-guide-health-insurance.pdf"}]},{"lesson":"58","prompt":"An employer provides qualifying group medical coverage and pays part of the premium. How is the ordinary employer contribution generally treated for the employee?","options":["Treated as a personal disability benefit payment","Counted as a taxable HSA withdrawal","Treated as a capital gain when the policy renews","Excluded from taxable income under the applicable employer health coverage rules"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Qualifying employer-paid group medical coverage is generally excluded from an employee’s taxable income, subject to applicable rules.","id":"HM-C-10-4","domain":9,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"IRS Publication 15-B, employer’s tax guide to fringe benefits","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15b"},{"title":"IRS Publication 535 and Schedule 1, self-employed health insurance deduction","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-1-form-1040"},{"title":"IRS Publication 525, taxable and nontaxable income","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"}]},{"lesson":"36","prompt":"Employees pay part of the premium and the employer pays the rest. How would the plan describe that arrangement?","options":["Fully experience rated by definition","Purely individual","Noncontributory","Contributory"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sharing premium cost between employer and employees makes the arrangement contributory. Funding shares do not themselves determine rate methodology.","id":"HM-C-7-2","domain":6,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §15–1201 et seq., small employer market","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=15-1201"},{"title":"U.S. Department of Labor: group health plan basics","url":"https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/health-plans"}]},{"lesson":"52","prompt":"An insurer discovers credible evidence that someone deliberately submitted altered bills for payment. Which response fits the purpose of Maryland’s fraud reporting rules?","options":["Send an anonymous warning to the billing provider instead","Wait for repayment before deciding whether a report is needed","Report through the required channel and preserve the evidence","Treat an internal sales note as a report to the authorities"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reporting through the proper channel allows investigators to assess the suspected fraud and protects the integrity of the evidence.","id":"HM-C-1-15","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Article §10–126, denial, suspension, revocation and penalties","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=10-126"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–210, hearings","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-210"},{"title":"Insurance Article §27–802, reporting insurance fraud","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=27-802"},{"title":"Insurance Article §2–401, Insurance Fraud Division","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=2-401"}]},{"lesson":"32","prompt":"A disability underwriter offers a longer elimination period while keeping the monthly benefit. Which part of claim cost shifts to the applicant?","options":["Every payment after age 65","The entire premium after recovery","The cost of all occupational licensing fees","The earliest days of an otherwise covered disability"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A longer elimination period shifts initial income-loss days to the insured. The benefit level still governs payable months.","id":"HM-C-5-7","domain":4,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"Insurance Information Institute: disability insurance basics","url":"https://www.iii.org/article/what-disability-insurance"},{"title":"NAIC consumer glossary","url":"https://content.naic.org/consumer_glossary"}]},{"lesson":"22","prompt":"A patient covered under an eligible military health program asks which program coordinates health benefits for eligible service members and their families. Which program is intended?","options":["Medigap","COBRA continuation","Medicaid spend-down","TRICARE"],"answer":3,"explanation":"TRICARE is the military health program. Eligibility and plan choices depend on the person’s military beneficiary status.","id":"HM-C-6-4","domain":5,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"HealthCare.gov glossary: PPO and POS","url":"https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/"},{"title":"TRICARE plans","url":"https://www.tricare.mil/Plans"}]},{"lesson":"48","prompt":"Which is a sound way to handle an applicant who declines an offered inflation protection option in a Maryland long-term care purchase?","options":["Document the applicant’s decision as required","Replace inflation protection with Medicare Part D","Omit all mention of inflation from the file","Add the option and conceal its premium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Offering inflation protection and documenting the choice helps establish that the applicant understood how future care costs can affect the benefit.","id":"HM-C-1-10","domain":0,"scored":true,"sources":[{"title":"COMAR 31.14.01, Long-Term Care Insurance","url":"https://regulations.justia.com/states/maryland/title-31/subtitle-14/chapter-31-14-01/"},{"title":"Insurance Article §18–104 and §18–105, applications and applicants 80 and older","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gin&section=18-104"},{"title":"Tax-General Article §10–718, long-term care insurance credit","url":"https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/laws/StatuteText?article=gtg&section=10-718"}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"01","number":1,"title":"Risk, perils and the health peril","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H01-01","lesson":"01","domain":1,"prompt":"Marcus does not know whether anyone in his family will be hospitalized this year. In insurance terms, that uncertainty is the:","options":["Hazard","Peril","Risk","Loss"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Risk is uncertainty about whether a loss will occur."},{"id":"H01-02","lesson":"01","domain":1,"prompt":"Icy steps outside a home make a fall more likely. The steps are best described as a:","options":["Physical hazard","Peril","Moral hazard","Speculative risk"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A physical condition that raises the likelihood of loss is a physical hazard."},{"id":"H01-03","lesson":"01","domain":1,"prompt":"An insured exaggerates the severity of an injury to increase a claim payment. This behavior illustrates:","options":["Morale hazard","Moral hazard","A peril","Adverse exposure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moral hazard is dishonesty or character flaws that increase losses to the insurer."},{"id":"H01-04","lesson":"01","domain":1,"prompt":"Which situation involves a speculative rather than a pure risk?","options":["A child develops an ear infection","A worker falls from a ladder","A family opens a food truck business","A commuter is injured in a car crash"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A new business could gain or lose money, which makes the risk speculative and not insurable in the ordinary sense."},{"id":"H01-05","lesson":"01","domain":2,"prompt":"Marcus’s daughter develops strep throat two weeks after his accident-only policy takes effect. How does the policy respond?","options":["It pays because the illness began after the effective date","It pays nothing because sickness is not a covered peril","It pays half the benefit during the probationary period","It pays only if a physician certifies the illness was unexpected"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An accident-only policy covers accidental injury, not sickness, regardless of when the illness begins."},{"id":"H01-06","lesson":"01","domain":2,"prompt":"A policy defines a covered accident using an accidental results definition. Marcus deliberately lifts a heavy box and unexpectedly tears a muscle. Which statement is correct?","options":["The claim is denied because the lifting was intentional","The claim is covered because the injury itself was unintended","The claim is covered only if a witness confirms the box slipped","The claim is denied unless the policy also covers sickness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under a results definition, an unexpected and unintended injury is covered even when the activity was deliberate."},{"id":"H01-07","lesson":"01","domain":2,"prompt":"Under a strict accidental means definition, which fact pattern is MOST likely to be denied?","options":["A pedestrian is struck by a car","A homeowner slips on an unseen patch of ice","A jogger intentionally jumps a fence and breaks an ankle on landing","A shopper is hit by a falling display"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cause, jumping, was intentional, so a strict means policy can deny even though the result was unintended."},{"id":"H01-08","lesson":"01","domain":2,"prompt":"A policy excludes sickness that begins during its first 15 days but covers accidents from the effective date. This difference exists mainly because:","options":["Accidents are always more expensive than illnesses","Illness symptoms may already exist when a person applies, while accidents cannot be anticipated","State law prohibits covering illness in the first month","Accident claims are paid by a different insurer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A probationary period guards against people buying coverage for an illness they already sense, which is not a concern with sudden accidents."}]},{"id":"02","number":2,"title":"Handling risk, pooling and insurability","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H02-01","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"Marcus decides not to buy a motorcycle after considering the injury risk. Which method of handling risk is this?","options":["Reduction","Avoidance","Transfer","Sharing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Not taking on the exposure at all is avoidance."},{"id":"H02-02","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"A family installs a handrail on steps they continue to use every day. The handrail is an example of:","options":["Risk avoidance","Risk retention","Risk reduction","Risk transfer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A safety measure that lowers the chance or severity of a loss is reduction."},{"id":"H02-03","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"Rosa chooses a $3,000 deductible on her medical plan. The deductible represents:","options":["Risk she has transferred to the insurer","Risk she has retained","A hazard the insurer accepts","Reinsurance purchased on her behalf"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insured keeps responsibility for the first $3,000 of covered loss, which is retention."},{"id":"H02-04","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer can predict with reasonable accuracy how many of 200,000 similar insureds will be hospitalized next year, even though it cannot predict any single case. This illustrates:","options":["Adverse selection","The law of large numbers","Reinsurance","Moral hazard"],"answer":1,"explanation":"As the number of similar exposures grows, actual results approach expected results."},{"id":"H02-05","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"People who anticipate needing medical care are more likely to buy coverage than people who feel healthy. Insurers call this tendency:","options":["The law of large numbers","Speculative risk","Adverse selection","Risk sharing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adverse selection is the tendency of likelier claimants to seek coverage."},{"id":"H02-06","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"Which characteristic makes a risk LESS suitable for private insurance?","options":["A large number of similar exposure units","A loss that is definite in time and amount","A single event likely to strike every insured at once","A chance of loss that can be calculated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A catastrophic loss hitting the whole pool at once defeats pooling and is avoided or limited by insurers."},{"id":"H02-07","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer cedes part of the risk on its medical plans to another company that agrees to reimburse a share of large claims. The company that issued the policies is the:","options":["Reinsurer","Ceding insurer","Policyholder","Third-party administrator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The original insurer that transfers part of its risk is the ceding insurer."},{"id":"H02-08","lesson":"02","domain":1,"prompt":"Marcus’s insurer has a reinsurance agreement. Where does Marcus file a covered claim?","options":["With the reinsurer directly","With the Maryland Insurance Administration","With his original insurer","Half with each company"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reinsurance does not change the policyholder’s contract; claims go to the issuing insurer."}]},{"id":"03","number":3,"title":"Insurers, nonprofit plans and HMOs","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H03-01","lesson":"03","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer owned by its policyholders, which may pay them dividends when results are favorable, is a:","options":["Stock insurer","Mutual insurer","Fraternal benefit society","Self-insurer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Policyholder ownership with possible dividends describes a mutual insurer."},{"id":"H03-02","lesson":"03","domain":1,"prompt":"A large employer pays employee medical claims from its own funds and hires an outside firm to process them. This arrangement is:","options":["A fraternal plan","Self-insurance","A mutual plan","Reinsurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An employer that retains and funds its own claims is self-insuring, often with an administrator."},{"id":"H03-03","lesson":"03","domain":1,"prompt":"A health insurer incorporated in Pennsylvania holds a Maryland certificate of authority and sells in Baltimore. In Maryland it is:","options":["Domestic and admitted","Foreign and admitted","Alien and nonadmitted","Foreign and nonadmitted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incorporated in another state makes it foreign; the certificate of authority makes it admitted."},{"id":"H03-04","lesson":"03","domain":1,"prompt":"Which program is an example of government rather than private insurance?","options":["A major medical policy from a stock insurer","A nonprofit health service plan contract","Medicaid","A fraternal society’s hospital indemnity plan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Medicaid is a tax-funded government program for people with limited income."},{"id":"H03-05","lesson":"03","domain":1,"prompt":"Theo tells Priya that an insurer’s A.M. Best rating guarantees her claims will always be paid. His statement is:","options":["Accurate, because ratings are backed by the state","Inaccurate, because ratings are independent opinions of financial strength, not guarantees","Accurate, because only rated insurers may sell in Maryland","Inaccurate, because ratings apply only to life insurers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ratings express an opinion about claims-paying ability; misstating them as guarantees is a misrepresentation."},{"id":"H03-06","lesson":"03","domain":1,"prompt":"A producer places business with several insurers and keeps ownership of her customer list when she leaves one of them. She works in a(n):","options":["Captive agency system","Direct response system","Independent agency system","Managing general agency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Independent producers represent several insurers and own their book of business."},{"id":"H03-07","lesson":"03","domain":0,"prompt":"Which Maryland organization combines the financing and the delivery of health care and must hold a certificate of authority from the Insurance Commissioner?","options":["A fraternal benefit society","A health maintenance organization","A nonadmitted insurer","A rating service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An HMO both finances and delivers care and is authorized under the Health-General Article with a certificate of authority."},{"id":"H03-08","lesson":"03","domain":0,"prompt":"An organization formed under Insurance Article Title 14 to provide health services to subscribers on a prepaid or indemnity basis, not for profit, is a:","options":["Stock insurer","Nonprofit health service plan","Self-insured trust","Reinsurer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Title 14 governs nonprofit health service plans serving subscribers."}]},{"id":"04","number":4,"title":"Agency and producer authority","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H04-01","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"Under the law of agency, when a producer sells an insurer’s health policy, the insurer is the:","options":["Third party","Principal","Fiduciary beneficiary","Broker"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insurer is the principal on whose behalf the producer acts."},{"id":"H04-02","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"A producer who represents only one insurer is described as:","options":["Independent","Captive","Nonadmitted","Apparent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A captive producer represents a single insurer."},{"id":"H04-03","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"Theo’s agency contract states he may solicit applications and collect initial premiums. This written grant is:","options":["Implied authority","Apparent authority","Express authority","Fiduciary authority"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Authority spelled out in the agency contract is express."},{"id":"H04-04","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"Theo’s contract allows him to collect premiums but says nothing about giving receipts. He gives Rosa a receipt. His authority to do so is best described as:","options":["Express","Implied","Apparent","Nonexistent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Issuing a receipt is a normal step in carrying out the express duty to collect premiums, which is implied authority."},{"id":"H04-05","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer revokes a producer’s contract but lets him keep using its forms and logo and never notifies the public. An applicant reasonably relies on him. Which doctrine may bind the insurer?","options":["Express authority","Implied authority","Apparent authority","Adverse selection"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The insurer’s conduct created a reasonable appearance of authority, which is apparent authority."},{"id":"H04-06","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"Rosa tells Theo about a prior back injury, but Theo omits it from the application. Which statement is correct?","options":["The insurer is treated as knowing about the injury because the producer knew","Rosa is solely responsible because she signed the application","The omission automatically voids the policy","The insurer may deny all claims because the producer acted outside express authority"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Knowledge gained by the agent within the scope of the agency is imputed to the insurer."},{"id":"H04-07","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"Which action breaches a producer’s fiduciary responsibility?","options":["Depositing a client’s premium into the insurer’s account the same day","Using a collected premium to pay the agency’s rent for a week","Explaining the policy’s exclusions before delivery","Submitting the application to the insurer promptly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Premiums belong to the insurer; using them for the producer’s own expenses is a fiduciary breach even if repaid later."},{"id":"H04-08","lesson":"04","domain":1,"prompt":"A former producer whose contract ended, and who returned all company materials, prints his own flyers claiming to represent the insurer. An applicant relies on the flyer. Why is apparent authority unlikely to bind the insurer?","options":["Because applicants can never rely on flyers","Because the appearance of authority came from the producer, not from the insurer’s conduct","Because apparent authority applies only to life insurance","Because the applicant did not pay a premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Apparent authority requires the insurer’s own conduct to create the appearance; a producer’s unilateral claims do not."}]},{"id":"05","number":5,"title":"Building and interpreting the contract","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H05-01","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"Rosa submits a completed application with her first premium. In contract terms, she has made the:","options":["Acceptance","Consideration","Offer","Warranty"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An application with premium is the applicant’s offer to the insurer."},{"id":"H05-02","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"The insurer’s promise to pay covered medical expenses in exchange for premium and truthful statements illustrates which contract element?","options":["Competent parties","Consideration","Legal purpose","Acceptance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Consideration is the value each party gives: premium and statements for the promise to pay."},{"id":"H05-03","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"A court finds a policy provision ambiguous and interprets it in favor of the insured because the insurer wrote the contract. This reflects the policy’s nature as a contract of:","options":["Indemnity","Adhesion","Utmost good faith","Reinsurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A contract drafted entirely by one party is one of adhesion, and ambiguities are read against the drafter."},{"id":"H05-04","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"Rosa pays $400 a month and later collects $30,000 for surgery. The unequal exchange is acceptable because an insurance contract is:","options":["Unilateral","Aleatory","Personal","Conditional"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An aleatory contract involves unequal values that depend on chance."},{"id":"H05-05","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"Why cannot an insurer sue Rosa for failing to pay her renewal premium?","options":["Because the contract is aleatory","Because the contract is unilateral and only the insurer made an enforceable promise","Because health policies are exempt from contract law","Because premiums are consideration only for the first year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a unilateral contract only the insurer’s promise is enforceable; the insured’s nonpayment simply ends the coverage."},{"id":"H05-06","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"Rosa understates her weight by five pounds on the application. The insurer later wants to rescind for misrepresentation. Which point decides the outcome?","options":["Whether the statement was material to the underwriting decision","Whether the statement was a warranty","Whether Rosa paid by check or cash","Whether the policy is participating"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rescission for misrepresentation requires materiality; a trivial error would not have changed the decision."},{"id":"H05-07","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"An applicant deliberately leaves out a recent cancer diagnosis that the application did not specifically ask about but that any insurer would consider material. This is best described as:","options":["Waiver","Concealment","Estoppel","Reasonable expectation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Intentionally withholding a material fact is concealment."},{"id":"H05-08","lesson":"05","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer repeatedly accepts late premium payments without objection, then denies a claim solely because the latest payment was late. Which doctrines work against the insurer?","options":["Adhesion and indemnity","Waiver and estoppel","Warranty and concealment","Aleatory and conditional"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accepting late payments waived prompt-payment rights, and the insured’s reliance estops the insurer from denying on that ground."}]},{"id":"06","number":6,"title":"Health losses, benefits and policy classes","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H06-01","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"Marcus is injured and cannot work for six months. Which type of coverage is designed to replace his lost earnings?","options":["Medical expense insurance","Disability income insurance","Dental insurance","Specified disease insurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disability income insurance replaces income lost when the insured cannot work."},{"id":"H06-02","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"A policy pays a lump sum only if the insured is diagnosed with cancer. It is best classified as a:","options":["Comprehensive medical plan","Specified disease policy","Long-term care policy","Disability income policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A policy limited to a named illness is a specified, or dread, disease policy."},{"id":"H06-03","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"An AD&D policy has a $100,000 principal sum. The insured loses one hand in an accident. The policy most commonly pays:","options":["Nothing, because the insured survived","The capital sum, often $50,000","The full $100,000 principal sum","Monthly income until recovery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Loss of one limb typically pays the capital sum, commonly half the principal sum."},{"id":"H06-04","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"An insured dies of a heart attack while holding an AD&D policy. What does the policy pay?","options":["The principal sum","The capital sum","Nothing, because death resulted from sickness","Half the principal sum"],"answer":2,"explanation":"AD&D responds only to accidents; death from sickness is not covered."},{"id":"H06-05","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"Marcus receives a certificate of coverage while his employer holds the master policy. Marcus’s coverage is:","options":["Individual","Group","Government","Limited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A master policy held by the employer with certificates for employees defines group coverage."},{"id":"H06-06","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"Which coverage is created by statute and funded through payroll taxes rather than by a private contract for premium?","options":["Rosa’s individual medical plan","A group dental plan","Medicare","A hospital indemnity policy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Medicare is a federal government program funded by payroll taxes and premiums."},{"id":"H06-07","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"A policy pays $200 per day of hospital confinement and nothing else. Maryland requires it to carry a notice because it is:","options":["A comprehensive plan","A limited policy","A government program","A group certificate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A policy paying a narrow fixed benefit is limited and must disclose that it is not comprehensive coverage."},{"id":"H06-08","lesson":"06","domain":2,"prompt":"Gloria needs a year of help with bathing and dressing at home after a fall. Which coverage is designed for that expense?","options":["Medical expense insurance","Accidental death and dismemberment","Long-term care insurance","Vision insurance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Long-term care insurance covers custodial and personal care such as help with daily activities."}]},{"id":"07","number":7,"title":"Federal rules and industry organizations","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H07-01","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer orders an investigative consumer report on an applicant. Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, the applicant must be notified of the request:","options":["Only if the report is unfavorable","Within three days of the request","After the policy is issued","Never, because the report is confidential"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The FCRA requires notice within three days that an investigative report may be made."},{"id":"H07-02","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"An insurer declines an applicant based partly on a consumer report. Federal law requires the insurer to:","options":["Refund all premiums paid in the prior year","Notify the applicant and identify the consumer reporting agency","Report the applicant to the Maryland Insurance Administration","Destroy the report within 30 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adverse action notices must tell the applicant and name the agency so the report can be obtained and disputed."},{"id":"H07-03","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"A person convicted of a felony involving breach of trust wants to work as an insurance producer. Under 18 USC 1033 she must first:","options":["Wait ten years","Obtain written consent from the state insurance regulator","Pay a federal registration fee","Obtain a bond from her employer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The statute prohibits participation without the regulator’s written consent."},{"id":"H07-04","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"Which employer-sponsored plan is generally exempt from ERISA?","options":["A manufacturer’s group medical plan","A retailer’s long-term disability plan","A county government’s employee health plan","A law firm’s dental plan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Governmental plans, like church plans, are exempt from ERISA."},{"id":"H07-05","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"Under ERISA, a new participant must generally receive the summary plan description within:","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","One year"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The SPD must be furnished within 90 days of becoming a participant."},{"id":"H07-06","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"An employee of a 60-person company divorces the covered worker. Under COBRA, how long may the former spouse continue the group coverage?","options":["18 months","29 months","36 months","60 days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Divorce is a qualifying event that gives dependents up to 36 months."},{"id":"H07-07","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"Under the Affordable Care Act, premiums for comprehensive individual coverage may vary by age no more than:","options":["2 to 1","3 to 1","5 to 1","10 to 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Age rating is limited to a 3 to 1 ratio between the oldest and youngest adults."},{"id":"H07-08","lesson":"07","domain":1,"prompt":"A producer tells a client that the NAIC has approved a policy for sale in Maryland. The statement is wrong because the NAIC:","options":["Approves only life insurance policies","Is an association of state regulators that drafts models but has no approval or enforcement power","Approves policies only after the federal government reviews them","Licenses producers but does not approve policies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The NAIC coordinates regulators and writes model laws; approval happens at the state level."}]},{"id":"08","number":8,"title":"Limited policies and their notices","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H08-01","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"A policy pays $200 for each day the insured is confined in a hospital, regardless of the actual bill. This describes:","options":["Major medical insurance","Hospital indemnity insurance","Credit disability insurance","Blanket insurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fixed daily payment unrelated to charges is hospital indemnity, or hospital income, coverage."},{"id":"H08-02","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"Which statement about a limited policy sold in Maryland is correct?","options":["It must include all essential health benefits","It must carry a prominent notice stating that it is a limited policy","It may not be sold alongside a comprehensive plan","It cannot be medically underwritten"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The limited nature must be disclosed prominently so buyers do not mistake it for comprehensive coverage."},{"id":"H08-03","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"Rosa’s cancer policy pays a $25,000 lump sum on diagnosis even though her medical plan pays the hospital. The cancer policy is a:","options":["Comprehensive medical plan","Specified disease policy","Coordination-of-benefits plan","Government program"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A policy paying only for a named illness, regardless of other coverage, is a specified disease policy."},{"id":"H08-04","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"Dennis finances a car and buys coverage that will make his monthly loan payments if he becomes disabled. The beneficiary of this coverage is:","options":["Dennis","The lender","The Maryland Insurance Administration","The car dealer’s service department"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Credit disability names the creditor as beneficiary and pays the loan installments."},{"id":"H08-05","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"A bus company insures all passengers against accidental injury without listing their names. This coverage is:","options":["Individual accident insurance","Blanket insurance","Credit insurance","Short-term medical insurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Covering an unnamed, changing class of people exposed to a common hazard is blanket insurance."},{"id":"H08-06","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"An independent critical illness policy pays a lump sum after a heart attack, without coordinating with other coverage. Which statement is accurate?","options":["The benefit must be used to pay hospital bills","It is an excepted benefit not subject to the ACA’s essential health benefit rules","It replaces the need for a comprehensive medical plan","It may be sold only to people over 65"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent coverage for a specified illness can qualify for the federal exception when it does not coordinate benefits with another plan."},{"id":"H08-07","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"Under Maryland law, a short-term limited duration medical policy may:","options":["Last up to 12 months and be renewed twice","Last less than three months and may not be renewed","Last up to 36 months if the insurer agrees","Be issued without any disclosure of its limits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland limits short-term policies to less than three months with no renewal or extension."},{"id":"H08-08","lesson":"08","domain":2,"prompt":"A client between jobs needs coverage for an existing diabetes condition. Why is a short-term medical plan a poor fit?","options":["Short-term plans are available only to people over 50","Short-term plans are underwritten and exclude pre-existing conditions","Short-term plans cannot be purchased in Maryland","Short-term plans require a 90-day waiting period for all claims"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Short-term coverage is medically underwritten and excludes pre-existing conditions, so the diabetes care would not be covered."}]},{"id":"09","number":9,"title":"Common exclusions","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H09-01","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"Marcus was treated for asthma last year and now enrolls in a comprehensive individual medical plan. How does the plan treat his asthma?","options":["It is excluded for 12 months as a pre-existing condition","It is covered from the effective date because pre-existing condition exclusions are prohibited in comprehensive medical coverage","It is covered only after a 6-month waiting period","It is excluded unless he passes a medical exam"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Federal law bars pre-existing condition exclusions and health underwriting in comprehensive medical coverage."},{"id":"H09-02","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"In which policy does a pre-existing condition exclusion still commonly appear?","options":["A Marketplace bronze plan","A small employer medical plan","An individual disability income policy","An employer’s non-grandfathered major medical plan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Disability income policies are excepted benefits and may use look-back and exclusion periods."},{"id":"H09-03","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"An insured is injured during a suicide attempt. Under a standard health policy, the resulting medical expenses are:","options":["Covered in full","Excluded as an intentionally self-inflicted injury","Covered at 50 percent","Covered only if the insured was under age 21"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Injuries the insured deliberately causes are excluded, although treatment of the underlying mental health condition is covered."},{"id":"H09-04","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"Which procedure is MOST likely to be covered despite the cosmetic surgery exclusion?","options":["A facelift to look younger","Reconstructive surgery after a mastectomy","A tummy tuck after voluntary weight loss","Teeth whitening"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reconstruction after disease or injury is covered, and Maryland specifically mandates post-mastectomy reconstruction."},{"id":"H09-05","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"Rosa is injured while wiring a customer’s building. Her individual medical policy excludes the claim because:","options":["Electrical work is an illegal occupation","The injury is covered by workers compensation","She was intoxicated","The policy excludes all accidents"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Most health policies are nonoccupational and exclude conditions covered by workers compensation, the primary payer for job injuries."},{"id":"H09-06","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"A veteran receives treatment at a Veterans Affairs hospital at no charge. His private health policy:","options":["Must reimburse the VA in full","Excludes care furnished by government plans for which he has no obligation to pay","Pays him the cash value of the treatment","Must pay double benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Care provided by government plans and care the insured need not pay for are excluded to avoid double payment."},{"id":"H09-07","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"An insured breaks an ankle while fleeing a store he was robbing. His disability income policy:","options":["Pays benefits because the injury was accidental","Excludes the loss because it occurred while committing a felony","Pays half benefits","Pays only medical expenses, not income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Losses sustained while committing a felony are excluded."},{"id":"H09-08","lesson":"09","domain":2,"prompt":"A customer is wounded by a robber during a bank holdup. Is the customer’s medical claim excluded under the felony provision?","options":["Yes, because a felony was in progress","No, because the exclusion applies only to a person participating in the felony","Yes, unless the robber is convicted","No, but benefits are reduced by half"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A victim or bystander did not participate in the crime, so the exclusion does not apply."}]},{"id":"10","number":10,"title":"Marketing, advertising and the guaranty corporation","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H10-01","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"A health insurance advertisement lists benefits in large type and places exclusions in tiny print on the back. Under Maryland advertising rules this is:","options":["Acceptable because the exclusions are disclosed","Not acceptable because limitations must be disclosed with prominence comparable to the benefits","Acceptable for limited policies only","Acceptable if the insurer approved it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Limitations and exclusions must be presented as prominently as the benefits."},{"id":"H10-02","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"Which advertising statement is prohibited?","options":["“Underwritten by Chesapeake Health Insurance Company”","“Approved by the Maryland Insurance Administration for all seniors”","“A 30-day right to return applies”","“Benefits are subject to the exclusions listed in the policy”"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Implying government approval or endorsement is prohibited."},{"id":"H10-03","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"Who is responsible under Maryland rules for a misleading advertisement created and distributed by a producer?","options":["Only the producer","Only the insurer","Both the insurer, which is accountable for ads used on its behalf, and the producer who made the misrepresentation","Neither, if the ad was never printed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Insurers are responsible for advertisements used on their behalf, and producers face penalties for misrepresentation."},{"id":"H10-04","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"A Maryland resident’s major medical insurer becomes insolvent with $420,000 of covered claims outstanding. The guaranty corporation protects claims under a health benefit plan up to:","options":["$100,000","$250,000","$300,000","$500,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Health benefit plans such as major medical are protected up to $500,000."},{"id":"H10-05","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"Theo tells a hesitant client not to worry about the insurer’s finances because the Maryland guaranty corporation will pay if the company fails. His statement is:","options":["Permitted because it is true","Prohibited because the guaranty corporation may not be used to induce a sale","Permitted if the client asks first","Required disclosure under Maryland law"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using the existence of the guaranty corporation in solicitation or advertising is prohibited."},{"id":"H10-06","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"Which plan is NOT protected by the Maryland Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Corporation?","options":["A disability income policy from an authorized Maryland insurer","A self-funded employer medical plan","A major medical policy from an admitted insurer","A long-term care policy from a member HMO affiliate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-funded employer plans are not insurance from a member insurer and are excluded."},{"id":"H10-07","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"During a sales presentation Theo describes the monthly benefit and premium but never mentions the 90-day elimination period. His presentation is deficient because:","options":["Elimination periods may not be discussed before application","A fair presentation must disclose limitations such as waiting periods, not only benefits and price","Only the insurer may explain elimination periods","Elimination periods apply only to group policies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Omitting a material limitation misleads the buyer; fair presentations disclose limits alongside benefits."},{"id":"H10-08","lesson":"10","domain":2,"prompt":"A producer compares two insurers’ policies using accurate, complete information and recommends the one that better fits the client. This is:","options":["Twisting","Defamation","A permitted fair comparison","Prohibited advertising"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Accurate and complete comparisons are a legitimate part of a fair presentation."}]},{"id":"11","number":11,"title":"Field underwriting, application and delivery","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H11-01","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"The producer’s screening of an applicant’s health, occupation and lifestyle while taking the application is called:","options":["Home office underwriting","Field underwriting","Adverse selection","Reinsurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The producer’s initial screening is field underwriting."},{"id":"H11-02","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"An applicant wants to change an answer after writing it on the application. The correct procedure is to:","options":["Have the producer erase it and write the new answer","Have the applicant initial the correction","Leave both answers and let the underwriter choose","Submit a new application without the applicant’s signature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Corrections must be initialed by the applicant so the record shows the applicant approved the change."},{"id":"H11-03","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"Rosa pays her first premium with her application and receives a conditional receipt. The insurer approves her as applied for three weeks later. Her coverage is effective:","options":["On the date the policy is delivered","On the date of the application or medical exam","Thirty days after approval","Only after she signs a statement of continued good health"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A conditional receipt makes coverage effective as of the application or exam date if the applicant is approved as applied for."},{"id":"H11-04","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"Theo delivers a policy to an applicant who did not pay the premium with the application. Before leaving the policy, he must:","options":["Collect the premium and obtain a statement of continued good health","Have the applicant take a medical exam","Notify the Maryland Insurance Administration","Wait 10 days for the free-look period to end"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without premium the policy is not in force, and the insurer needs confirmation that health has not changed."},{"id":"H11-05","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"The free-look period on an individual health policy begins:","options":["When the application is signed","When the insurer approves the application","When the policy is delivered to the insured","When the first claim is filed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The right to examine runs from delivery, which is why a delivery receipt is obtained."},{"id":"H11-06","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"A producer negligently fails to submit a client’s application, leaving the client uninsured when a loss occurs. The client sues. Which coverage responds?","options":["The producer’s errors and omissions insurance","The client’s own health policy","The guaranty corporation","The producer’s surety bond"],"answer":0,"explanation":"E&O coverage pays defense costs and damages for negligent professional errors."},{"id":"H11-07","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"Which loss is NOT covered by a producer’s errors and omissions policy?","options":["A misquoted benefit that caused the client to buy inadequate coverage","A fine imposed by the Insurance Commissioner for misrepresentation","A delayed application that left the client uninsured","An incorrect explanation of a policy’s renewability"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Regulatory fines and intentional misconduct are excluded from E&O coverage."},{"id":"H11-08","lesson":"11","domain":2,"prompt":"Dennis turns 65 in March. Why should his producer notify him about Medicare eligibility beforehand?","options":["Because his individual policy automatically converts to Medicare","Because enrollment windows open around his birthday and late enrollment can bring permanent penalties","Because Maryland requires producers to enroll clients in Medicare","Because Medicare eligibility cancels all private coverage immediately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The initial enrollment and Medigap open enrollment periods are time-limited, and missing Part B or Part D enrollment can cause lasting penalties."}]},{"id":"12","number":12,"title":"Insurer underwriting and sources of information","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H12-01","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"Which document gives the insurer the producer’s own observations about the applicant and is not shown to the applicant?","options":["The attending physician statement","The producer’s report","The inspection report","The MIB report"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The producer’s report, or agent’s statement, records the producer’s observations for the insurer."},{"id":"H12-02","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"Rosa’s application mentions a back injury. To learn the diagnosis and prognosis from the doctor who treated her, the underwriter requests a(n):","options":["Inspection report","Producer’s report","Attending physician statement","Consumer credit report"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The APS provides the treating physician’s account of diagnosis, treatment and prognosis."},{"id":"H12-03","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"An insurer finds an MIB code suggesting an undisclosed condition. Which action is permitted?","options":["Decline the application based on the code alone","Investigate the code and confirm the facts before deciding","Share the code with the applicant’s employer","Add the code to the policy as an exclusion rider without inquiry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MIB codes are leads that must be verified through the applicant or medical records."},{"id":"H12-04","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"The purpose of the Medical Information Bureau is to:","options":["Set premium rates for member insurers","Help member insurers detect omissions and misrepresentations across applications","Provide medical treatment records to employers","Replace the attending physician statement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The MIB exists so insurers can spot information omitted on one application but reported on another."},{"id":"H12-05","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"Before an insurer tests an applicant for HIV, the applicant must:","options":["Pay for the test","Give specific written informed consent","Obtain a physician’s referral","Sign a waiver of confidentiality"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HIV testing requires the applicant’s specific informed consent on a separate form."},{"id":"H12-06","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"An underwriting source that covers an applicant’s lifestyle, finances and reputation, obtained from an outside agency, is the:","options":["Producer’s report","Attending physician statement","Investigative consumer report","MIB code"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An investigative consumer, or inspection, report comes from an outside agency and covers lifestyle and finances."},{"id":"H12-07","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"Who pays for a paramedical examination required by the insurer during underwriting?","options":["The applicant","The producer","The insurer","The Medical Information Bureau"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The insurer orders and pays for examinations and tests it requires."},{"id":"H12-08","lesson":"12","domain":2,"prompt":"For a disability income application, which factor carries MORE weight than it would for a medical expense application?","options":["The applicant’s occupation and income","The applicant’s zip code","The number of dependents","The producer’s years of experience"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disability income benefits replace earnings, so occupation hazards and income level drive the decision."}]},{"id":"13","number":13,"title":"Risk classes and unlawful discrimination","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H13-01","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"An applicant with better-than-average health and no risky habits is placed in which class?","options":["Standard","Preferred","Substandard","Declined"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Preferred applicants present the best risks and receive the lowest rates."},{"id":"H13-02","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"The underwriter offers Rosa disability income coverage that excludes claims related to her prior back injury but covers all other conditions. This is a(n):","options":["Rated premium","Exclusion rider","Preferred classification","Declination"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carving a named condition out of coverage is an exclusion rider, a substandard tool."},{"id":"H13-03","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"Two applicants of the same age, occupation and health are charged different premiums because of their national origin. This is:","options":["Lawful risk classification","Unfair discrimination","A substandard rating","Adverse selection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Treating members of the same class differently on a prohibited basis is unfair discrimination."},{"id":"H13-04","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"An insurer refuses to renew a woman’s policy after learning she was injured by an abusive spouse. Under Maryland law this is:","options":["Permitted if the injuries were severe","Prohibited discrimination against a victim of domestic violence","Permitted if disclosed in the policy","Permitted for disability income policies only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland bars denying, canceling, nonrenewing, limiting or rating coverage because of domestic violence victim status."},{"id":"H13-05","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"Marcus has a genetic marker associated with a disease he has never developed. A health insurer wants to charge him more. Which statement is correct?","options":["It may do so if the marker is documented by a physician","It may not use genetic information or treat a predisposition as a pre-existing condition","It may do so only for family coverage","It may require Marcus to take a confirming genetic test first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Health insurers may not request genetic tests or use genetic information or family genetic history to deny or price coverage."},{"id":"H13-06","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"Which factor may an insurer lawfully use when classifying an individual disability income applicant?","options":["Religion","Occupation","National origin","Status as a victim of abuse"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Occupation reflects a real difference in disability risk and is lawful."},{"id":"H13-07","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"When an insurer rates an applicant based on information it collected during underwriting, Maryland’s information practices rules require the insurer to:","options":["Refund the first premium","Provide an adverse underwriting decision notice explaining how to learn the reasons","Report the applicant to the MIB within 24 hours","Obtain approval from the Insurance Commissioner"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adverse underwriting decisions require a notice telling the applicant how to obtain the specific reasons and information used."},{"id":"H13-08","lesson":"13","domain":2,"prompt":"Because federal law prohibits health-status underwriting for comprehensive individual medical plans, which type of coverage is MOST likely to use preferred, standard and substandard classes today?","options":["Individual major medical","Individual disability income","Small group medical","Marketplace silver plans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disability income remains fully underwritten with health-based classes."}]},{"id":"14","number":14,"title":"Replacing health insurance","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H14-01","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"Rosa’s two-year-old disability policy now covers her back condition. If she replaces it with a new policy, which risk arises?","options":["The new policy must cover the back condition immediately by law","The back condition may be treated as a pre-existing condition under the new policy’s look-back","Her premium is guaranteed to fall","The old insurer must refund all premiums"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A new disability policy brings a new look-back, so a condition already covered under the old policy can be excluded again."},{"id":"H14-02","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"Which comparison is essential before recommending replacement of a disability income policy?","options":["The color of the policy jackets","The definition of disability, elimination and benefit periods, riders and renewability","The producer’s commission on each policy","The insurer’s advertising budget"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Benefits, limitations, exclusions and renewability determine whether the new policy actually serves the client."},{"id":"H14-03","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"A client cancels her existing health policy, then applies for a replacement and is declined. What is the lesson?","options":["Replacement is never advisable","The existing policy should not be canceled until the new policy is issued and accepted","Insurers must accept applicants who held prior coverage","The producer should have backdated the new application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Underwriting may rate or decline the applicant, so existing coverage must remain in force until the new coverage is in hand."},{"id":"H14-04","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"When a Maryland Medicare supplement policy replaces another, the new insurer must:","options":["Impose a fresh six-month pre-existing condition exclusion","Waive pre-existing, waiting and probationary periods to the extent already satisfied for similar benefits","Charge the same premium as the old insurer","Require a physical examination"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires credit for time already served under the replaced policy."},{"id":"H14-05","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"A producer sells Gloria a second Medicare supplement policy while her first remains in force and no replacement occurs. This is:","options":["Good practice to maximize coverage","Prohibited duplication of Medicare supplement coverage","Required if she is under 70","Permitted if the premiums are paid monthly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selling a Medicare supplement policy to someone who already has one, without replacement, is a prohibited duplication."},{"id":"H14-06","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"Which situation MOST supports replacing an existing disability income policy?","options":["The new policy saves $10 a month but excludes a known back condition","The existing policy is conditionally renewable and the new one is noncancelable, and the client qualifies for standard rates","The producer earns a higher commission on the new policy","The client received a glossy mailer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixing a real gap in renewability while the client still qualifies, with no coverage lost, is a sound replacement."},{"id":"H14-07","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"An employer replaces a grandfathered medical plan with a new plan. Which statement is correct?","options":["The new plan keeps grandfathered status if premiums stay the same","Grandfathered status is lost and the new plan must meet all ACA requirements","Grandfathered status transfers to any plan from the same insurer","The employees must wait 12 months for coverage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A replacement plan is a new plan subject to the full ACA rules."},{"id":"H14-08","lesson":"14","domain":2,"prompt":"A producer tells a client, without basis, that her current insurer is about to become insolvent so she will buy a replacement policy. This conduct is:","options":["A fair comparison","Twisting, an unfair trade practice","Permitted puffery","Field underwriting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inducing replacement through misrepresentation is twisting."}]},{"id":"15","number":15,"title":"Required provisions: contract, defenses and premiums","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H15-01","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"Under the entire contract provision, which documents make up the complete agreement between Rosa and her insurer?","options":["The policy and the producer’s verbal promises","The policy, its endorsements and the attached application","The application and the sales brochure","The policy and the outline of coverage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The entire contract consists of the policy, endorsements and the application attached to it."},{"id":"H15-02","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"Who may validly change a provision of an individual health policy?","options":["The soliciting producer","An executive officer of the insurer, with the change endorsed on the policy","Any insurer employee by telephone","The insured, by writing on the policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Changes require executive officer approval and must be endorsed on or attached to the policy."},{"id":"H15-03","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"Three years after issue, an insurer discovers an innocent misstatement on Rosa’s application. Under the time limit on certain defenses, the insurer:","options":["May void the policy because any misstatement is material","May not use the innocent misstatement to void the policy or deny a claim","Must refund all premiums and cancel","May deny only claims over $5,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After two years only fraudulent misstatements may be used; innocent ones are barred."},{"id":"H15-04","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"Marcus pays his health premium monthly. The minimum grace period his policy must provide is:","options":["7 days","10 days","31 days","60 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Monthly premium policies must allow at least 10 days."},{"id":"H15-05","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"An insured is injured during the grace period before paying the overdue premium. The insurer:","options":["Denies the claim because the premium was late","Pays the claim, deducting the overdue premium","Pays only if the premium is paid within 24 hours of the injury","Cancels the policy retroactively"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Claims during the grace period are payable, and the insurer may deduct the unpaid premium."},{"id":"H15-06","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"A lapsed policyholder submits a reinstatement application and premium and receives a conditional receipt. The insurer never responds. The policy is reinstated:","options":["Never, because silence means denial","On the 45th day after the receipt date","On the 10th day after the receipt date","Only when the insured calls to confirm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without written disapproval, reinstatement occurs automatically on the 45th day after the conditional receipt."},{"id":"H15-07","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"Two days after a policy is reinstated, the insured develops pneumonia. How does the reinstated policy respond?","options":["It covers the illness in full","It does not cover the illness because sickness must begin more than 10 days after reinstatement","It covers the illness at 50 percent","It covers the illness only if the insured was hospitalized"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reinstated policies cover accidents immediately but sickness only if it begins more than 10 days later."},{"id":"H15-08","lesson":"15","domain":3,"prompt":"Which statement about the uniform required provisions is correct?","options":["Insurers may omit any required provision they dislike","Insurers may reword a required provision only if the new wording is at least as favorable to the insured","Required provisions apply only to group policies","Producers may waive required provisions at delivery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Substitutes are allowed only when not less favorable to the insured."}]},{"id":"16","number":16,"title":"Required provisions: the claim clocks","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H16-01","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"Under the uniform provisions, written notice of claim must generally be given within how many days after a loss?","options":["10 days","15 days","20 days","90 days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Notice of claim is due within 20 days or as soon as reasonably possible."},{"id":"H16-02","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"An insurer fails to send claim forms within 15 days after receiving notice of a claim. The claimant:","options":["Forfeits the claim","May submit proof of loss in any written form describing the occurrence and extent of the loss","Must wait until the forms arrive","Must file suit immediately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When forms are late, the claimant may use any written proof, and the insurer must accept it."},{"id":"H16-03","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"Written proof of loss for a single accident must ordinarily be furnished within:","options":["20 days","45 days","90 days","3 years"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Proof of loss is due within 90 days after the loss."},{"id":"H16-04","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"A claimant was in a coma and could not file proof of loss within 90 days. Under the proof of loss provision:","options":["The claim is denied as late","Proof is accepted when reasonably possible, and the one-year outside limit does not apply during legal incapacity","Proof must still be filed within 90 days by a relative","The insurer may reduce the benefit by half"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provision accepts late proof as soon as reasonably possible, and legal incapacity suspends the one-year outside limit."},{"id":"H16-05","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"Disability income benefits payable over many months must be paid to the insured at least:","options":["Weekly","Monthly","Quarterly","Annually"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Periodic benefits for a continuing loss must be paid at least monthly."},{"id":"H16-06","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"An insured dies from an accident covered by an AD&D policy with no named beneficiary. The death benefit is paid to:","options":["The attending hospital","The insured’s estate","The Maryland Insurance Administration","The insured’s employer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Death benefits go to the named beneficiary or, if none, to the insured’s estate."},{"id":"H16-07","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"Under the legal actions provision, an insured who has filed proof of loss may sue the insurer:","options":["Immediately","No sooner than 60 days and no later than 3 years after proof of loss","Only after 1 year","Only with the Commissioner’s permission"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Suit may not be filed before 60 days or after three years from when proof of loss was required."},{"id":"H16-08","lesson":"16","domain":3,"prompt":"Who pays for a physical examination the insurer requires while a disability claim is pending?","options":["The insured","The insurer","The attending physician","The state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insurer may examine the insured as often as reasonably necessary at its own expense."}]},{"id":"17","number":17,"title":"Optional provisions and renewability","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H17-01","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"Rosa changes from a desk job to a more hazardous occupation without telling her disability insurer. When she is injured, the change of occupation provision allows the insurer to:","options":["Void the policy","Reduce benefits to what her premium would have purchased at the more hazardous rate","Deny the claim entirely","Charge a fraud penalty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Benefits are scaled to what the premium would buy at the new occupation’s rate."},{"id":"H17-02","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"An insured understated her age on the application. Under the misstatement of age provision, the insurer will:","options":["Cancel the policy and refund premiums","Adjust benefits to the amount the premium would have purchased at her true age","Pay full benefits as written","Double the premium going forward"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Benefits are adjusted to what the premium paid would have bought at the correct age."},{"id":"H17-03","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"A disability income insured earns $4,000 a month but holds policies paying $5,500 a month combined. The relation of earnings to insurance provision:","options":["Pays $5,500 as contracted","Reduces total benefits to $4,000, the insured’s earnings, with a $200 minimum","Cancels the smaller policy","Pays nothing because of overinsurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provision limits benefits to earnings, or average earnings over two years, never below $200 a month."},{"id":"H17-04","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"The insurer discovers at claim time that the insured owes one month of premium. The unpaid premium provision permits the insurer to:","options":["Deny the claim","Deduct the overdue premium from the claim payment","Cancel retroactively to the policy date","Charge double the missed premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insurer may subtract premium due from the benefit paid."},{"id":"H17-05","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"A policy provision conflicts with a Maryland statute setting a longer grace period. Which provision resolves the conflict?","options":["Entire contract","Conformity with state statutes","Legal actions","Change of occupation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conformity with state statutes amends conflicting provisions to meet the state’s minimum requirements."},{"id":"H17-06","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"Which renewability clause prevents the insurer from both canceling the policy and raising the premium until a stated age?","options":["Guaranteed renewable","Conditionally renewable","Noncancelable","Optionally renewable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Noncancelable locks both the coverage and the premium schedule."},{"id":"H17-07","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"Marcus’s individual medical plan cannot be canceled because of his health, but the insurer raises premiums for everyone in his age band. His policy is:","options":["Noncancelable","Guaranteed renewable","Cancelable","Conditionally renewable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guaranteed renewable coverage cannot be canceled for health but may have class-wide premium increases."},{"id":"H17-08","lesson":"17","domain":3,"prompt":"Gloria receives a Medicare supplement policy on June 1. Under Maryland law, until what date may she return it for a full refund?","options":["June 11","June 21","July 1","December 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maryland requires a 30-day right to return on Medicare supplement policies, so June 1 plus 30 days is July 1."}]},{"id":"18","number":18,"title":"Medical plan concepts","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H18-01","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan reimburses a share of each provider’s charge after care is delivered. This payment method is:","options":["Prepaid","Fee-for-service","Capitation","Community rating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Payment made per service after care is fee-for-service, or indemnity."},{"id":"H18-02","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"An HMO collects a fixed monthly amount from Priya and provides care to her employees through its own physicians. The HMO operates on a:","options":["Fee-for-service basis","Prepaid basis","Benefit schedule basis","Specified coverage basis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fixed payment in advance for care is the prepaid basis."},{"id":"H18-03","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan pays $1,200 for an appendectomy regardless of the surgeon’s actual fee. This plan uses:","options":["Usual, customary and reasonable charges","A benefit schedule","Prepaid capitation","Coordination of benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A flat dollar amount per procedure is a benefit schedule."},{"id":"H18-04","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"Under a usual, customary and reasonable approach, what does the patient owe when an out-of-network surgeon charges more than the UCR allowance?","options":["Nothing; the plan pays the full charge","The amount above the allowance, subject to surprise-billing protections","Double the deductible","The entire bill"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Charges above UCR fall to the patient unless the provider accepted the allowance or federal surprise-billing rules apply."},{"id":"H18-05","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"A modern plan covers hospital, surgical, physician, diagnostic and preventive services under one deductible and coinsurance structure. It provides:","options":["Specified coverages","Comprehensive care","Limited benefits","Hospital indemnity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Covering the full range of services under one structure is comprehensive care."},{"id":"H18-06","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"Which plan feature is associated with lower premiums and negotiated provider prices?","options":["Any-provider freedom","A limited choice of network providers","UCR reimbursement everywhere","A benefit schedule with no network"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Networks of providers who accept discounted fees lower plan costs and premiums."},{"id":"H18-07","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"A person covered under a nonprofit health service plan or an HMO contract is usually called a(n):","options":["Insured","Subscriber or member","Beneficiary","Underwriter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Service plans and HMOs issue subscriber contracts to subscribers or members."},{"id":"H18-08","lesson":"18","domain":5,"prompt":"Why do prepaid plans have a financial incentive to cover preventive care generously?","options":["Because state law pays them a bonus for each physical","Because they keep the fixed payment regardless of utilization, so preventing illness lowers their costs","Because preventive care is excluded from UCR limits","Because fee-for-service rules require it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A prepaid plan profits when members need less care, which rewards prevention."}]},{"id":"19","number":19,"title":"Major medical: deductibles, coinsurance and stop-loss","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H19-01","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan has a $2,000 deductible and 80/20 coinsurance with a $7,000 out-of-pocket maximum. The insured incurs $12,000 in allowed charges. How much does the insured pay?","options":["$2,000","$4,000","$4,400","$7,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"$2,000 deductible plus 20% of the remaining $10,000, which is $2,000, totals $4,000, below the $7,000 cap."},{"id":"H19-02","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"Marcus’s plan has a $3,000 deductible, 80/20 coinsurance and a $6,000 out-of-pocket maximum. On $20,000 of allowed charges the insurer pays:","options":["$13,600","$14,000","$16,000","$17,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Marcus pays the $6,000 maximum, so the insurer pays the remaining $14,000."},{"id":"H19-03","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"A provision that applies a single deductible when several family members are injured in the same accident is the:","options":["Carryover provision","Common accident provision","Corridor deductible","Stop-loss provision"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The common accident provision requires only one deductible for family members hurt in one accident."},{"id":"H19-04","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"Expenses incurred in October through December that count toward the next year’s deductible illustrate a:","options":["Per-cause deductible","Carryover provision","Family deductible","Coinsurance corridor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The carryover provision credits last-quarter expenses to the following year’s deductible."},{"id":"H19-05","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"Which statement describes coinsurance correctly?","options":["A flat dollar amount paid at each office visit","A percentage of allowed charges shared between insured and insurer after the deductible","The amount paid before the plan pays anything","The premium shared with an employer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coinsurance is the percentage split applied to allowed charges after the deductible."},{"id":"H19-06","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"Once an insured reaches the plan’s out-of-pocket maximum for the year, the plan pays covered in-network charges at:","options":["80 percent","90 percent","100 percent","The UCR rate only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After the stop-loss limit, the plan pays 100 percent of covered charges for the rest of the year."},{"id":"H19-07","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"Which item does NOT count toward a comprehensive plan’s federal annual out-of-pocket maximum?","options":["The deductible","Coinsurance on covered services","Copayments for covered prescriptions","Monthly premiums"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Premiums are never counted toward the out-of-pocket maximum."},{"id":"H19-08","lesson":"19","domain":5,"prompt":"In a supplemental major medical arrangement, the amount the insured pays after the basic plan’s benefits are exhausted and before major medical begins is the:","options":["Stop-loss","Corridor deductible","Copayment","Lifetime maximum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The corridor deductible bridges the basic plan and the major medical layer."}]},{"id":"20","number":20,"title":"Health service plans and fixed indemnity","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H20-01","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"A policy pays $75 for each physician visit and $1,500 for each surgery regardless of the actual charges. This is a:","options":["Major medical plan","Fixed indemnity plan","Health maintenance organization","Preferred provider organization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Stated amounts per event unrelated to the bill define fixed indemnity coverage."},{"id":"H20-02","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"Why is a fixed indemnity plan classified as an excepted benefit?","options":["Because it is sold only to seniors","Because its benefits are fixed amounts not tied to actual medical expenses","Because it covers only accidents","Because it is issued by nonprofit plans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Benefits unrelated to actual expenses place it outside comprehensive-coverage rules."},{"id":"H20-03","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"In Maryland, a nonprofit corporation organized to provide or pay for health services to subscribers is licensed under:","options":["Health-General Article Title 19","Insurance Article Title 14","Insurance Article Title 9","Tax-General Article Title 10"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nonprofit health service plans are governed by Insurance Article Title 14."},{"id":"H20-04","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"A provider who has agreed to accept the service plan’s payment as payment in full for covered services is called a:","options":["Nonparticipating provider","Participating provider","Gatekeeper","Reinsurer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Participating, or qualified, providers accept plan payment in full, billing only cost sharing."},{"id":"H20-05","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"A subscriber uses a nonparticipating physician. Compared with a participating physician, the likely result is:","options":["Identical payment and no balance bill","A lower plan payment and a possible balance bill","No coverage of any kind","A higher plan payment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plans typically pay less for nonparticipating providers, who may bill the difference."},{"id":"H20-06","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"The document that describes a service plan’s benefits, exclusions and member cost sharing is the:","options":["Producer’s report","Evidence of coverage","Conditional receipt","Attending physician statement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Subscribers receive an evidence of coverage or subscriber contract describing the plan."},{"id":"H20-07","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"Which statement about Maryland mandated benefits is correct?","options":["They apply to insurers but not to nonprofit health service plans","They apply to nonprofit health service plan contracts as well as insurer policies","They apply only to HMOs","They apply only to group plans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland mandated benefits bind insurers, nonprofit health service plans and HMOs alike."},{"id":"H20-08","lesson":"20","domain":5,"prompt":"A subscriber chooses a physician listed as in-network in the plan’s directory, but the listing was out of date. Maryland’s disclosure rules mean the subscriber:","options":["Must pay the full out-of-network charge","Is protected because the plan must maintain an accurate directory and the subscriber relied on it","Loses coverage for the visit","Must switch plans at the next open enrollment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carriers must keep directories accurate, and reliance on a listing protects the subscriber from out-of-network treatment of the claim."}]},{"id":"21","number":21,"title":"Health maintenance organizations","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H21-01","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"Which feature distinguishes an HMO from a traditional insurer?","options":["It pays claims after services are rendered","It both finances and delivers health care on a prepaid basis","It covers any licensed provider at the same benefit level","It sells only to employers with more than 50 employees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Combining prepaid financing with the delivery of care through its providers defines an HMO."},{"id":"H21-02","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"An HMO member visits a dermatologist for a nonemergency rash without a referral from her primary care physician. The HMO will most likely:","options":["Pay the claim at 80 percent","Deny the claim because specialty care requires a referral","Pay the claim only if the dermatologist is out of network","Pay the claim and bill the PCP"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under the gatekeeper model, nonemergency specialty care without a referral is not covered."},{"id":"H21-03","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"An HMO pays each participating physician a fixed monthly amount for every member assigned, regardless of visits. This payment method is:","options":["Fee-for-service","Capitation","Coinsurance","Usual, customary and reasonable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fixed per-member payment to providers is capitation."},{"id":"H21-04","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"An HMO that employs its physicians directly and whose physicians serve only HMO members is a:","options":["Staff model, closed panel","IPA model, open panel","PPO","Point-of-service plan"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Employed physicians serving only plan members describe a staff model, which is a closed panel."},{"id":"H21-05","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"An HMO member suffers chest pain while traveling outside the service area and is treated in an emergency room. The HMO:","options":["Denies the claim because the care was out of area","Covers the emergency care under the prudent layperson standard","Pays only if the member called the PCP first","Pays 50 percent of the charges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Emergency services are covered without referral when a prudent layperson would believe an emergency existed."},{"id":"H21-06","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"Why do HMOs typically cover annual physicals and screenings with little or no copayment?","options":["Because federal law requires HMOs to pay physicians bonuses","Because preventing illness lowers the costs of a plan that already holds the prepaid premium","Because preventive care is excluded from capitation","Because indemnity insurers require it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A prepaid plan profits when members stay healthy, which rewards prevention."},{"id":"H21-07","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"In Maryland, an HMO must hold a certificate of authority from the:","options":["Maryland Department of Health","Insurance Commissioner","Secretary of Labor","Comptroller"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Insurance Commissioner issues the certificate of authority to an HMO."},{"id":"H21-08","lesson":"21","domain":5,"prompt":"A person covered by an HMO is most accurately called a(n):","options":["Policyholder","Member or subscriber","Beneficiary","Underwriter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HMO enrollees are members or subscribers holding an evidence of coverage."}]},{"id":"22","number":22,"title":"PPOs, point-of-service plans and TRICARE","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H22-01","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan pays 90 percent for care from contracted providers and 60 percent for care from others, and members need no referral to see a specialist. This plan is a(n):","options":["HMO","PPO","EPO","Staff model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two benefit levels with no gatekeeper define a preferred provider organization."},{"id":"H22-02","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan uses a network like a PPO but pays nothing for nonemergency care outside the network and requires no referrals. It is a(n):","options":["Point-of-service plan","Exclusive provider organization","Indemnity plan","TRICARE For Life"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An EPO restricts coverage to its network without a gatekeeper."},{"id":"H22-03","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"In a point-of-service plan, a member who goes outside the network for nonemergency care will typically:","options":["Receive no benefits","Pay a deductible and coinsurance and may need to file the claim","Pay only the in-network copayment","Lose HMO coverage for the rest of the year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Out-of-network POS benefits are indemnity style with deductible, coinsurance and member-filed claims."},{"id":"H22-04","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"Which plan design combines a primary care gatekeeper for in-network care with indemnity-style benefits for out-of-network care?","options":["PPO","HMO","Point-of-service plan","Fixed indemnity plan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The POS design is an HMO with an out-of-network indemnity option."},{"id":"H22-05","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"A physician contracts with several plans and treats patients from all of them. The networks that include this physician are:","options":["Closed panels","Open panels","Staff models","Gatekeeper models"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Open panels contract with independent providers who also treat other plans’ patients."},{"id":"H22-06","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"TRICARE Prime most closely resembles which civilian plan design?","options":["PPO","HMO","Fixed indemnity","Medicare supplement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prime uses a primary care manager and referrals, like an HMO."},{"id":"H22-07","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"A military retiree age 67 has Medicare Parts A and B. Which program pays secondary to Medicare for his care?","options":["TRICARE Prime","TRICARE Select","TRICARE For Life","Medicaid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TRICARE For Life wraps around Medicare for retirees 65 and older."},{"id":"H22-08","lesson":"22","domain":5,"prompt":"Marcus’s PPO negotiates a $8,000 fee for a surgery that the surgeon usually bills at $12,000. Because the surgeon is in network, Marcus’s coinsurance is calculated on:","options":["$12,000","$8,000","$4,000","The Medicare rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In-network providers accept the negotiated allowance, so coinsurance applies to $8,000 with no balance bill."}]},{"id":"23","number":23,"title":"Cost containment and coordination of benefits","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H23-01","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan requires approval before a scheduled hospital admission. This review is:","options":["Concurrent review","Prospective review","Retrospective review","Coordination of benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Review before care, such as precertification, is prospective review."},{"id":"H23-02","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"A nurse reviewer monitors a patient’s hospital stay and approves additional days as needed. This is:","options":["Prospective review","Concurrent review","Second surgical opinion","Case management of claims after discharge"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Review during the course of treatment is concurrent review."},{"id":"H23-03","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"Which service is an alternative to inpatient hospital care that plans encourage to contain costs?","options":["Longer hospital stays","Home health care","Emergency room visits for minor illness","Duplicate diagnostic tests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Home health care lets patients leave the hospital sooner at lower cost."},{"id":"H23-04","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"Marcus’s birthday is September 4 and Elena’s is March 12. Under the birthday rule, which group plan is primary for their child?","options":["Marcus’s plan, because he is older","Elena’s plan, because her birthday falls earlier in the calendar year","Whichever plan has the higher premium","Both plans pay equal shares"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The birthday rule looks at the month and day, not the year, so the earlier birthday is primary."},{"id":"H23-05","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"Elena is covered as an employee under her own group plan and as a dependent under Marcus’s. For Elena’s own claim, which plan is primary?","options":["Marcus’s plan","Elena’s own plan","The plan with the earlier effective date","The plan of the spouse with the earlier birthday"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A plan covering a person as an employee is primary over one covering the person as a dependent."},{"id":"H23-06","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"A child’s allowable expense is $4,000. The primary plan pays $2,800. The secondary plan would have paid $3,200 if it were the only coverage. The secondary plan pays:","options":["$3,200","$2,800","$1,200","$0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The secondary plan pays the $1,200 balance, bringing total payment to 100 percent of the allowable expense."},{"id":"H23-07","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"Divorced parents both cover their daughter under group plans. No court decree assigns responsibility for health coverage. Which plan is primary?","options":["The father’s plan always","The plan of the parent with custody","The plan of the parent with the earlier birthday","The plan with the lower deductible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Absent a court decree, the custodial parent’s plan is primary."},{"id":"H23-08","lesson":"23","domain":5,"prompt":"The purpose of a coordination of benefits provision is to:","options":["Allow the insured to collect from both plans in full","Prevent total benefits from exceeding 100 percent of the allowable expense","Transfer all liability to the secondary plan","Eliminate deductibles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"COB orders payment so the insured is made whole but not overpaid."}]},{"id":"24","number":24,"title":"HIPAA portability and renewability","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H24-01","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"Under HIPAA, a group health plan may NOT base an employee’s eligibility on:","options":["Hours worked per week","Job classification","Health status or claims experience","Length of service"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Health status, medical history and claims experience may not determine eligibility or contributions."},{"id":"H24-02","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"An employee declined group coverage because her spouse’s plan covered her. Her spouse loses his job and his coverage. Under HIPAA she may enroll in her own employer’s plan if she requests enrollment within:","options":["10 days","30 days","90 days","One year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Special enrollment after loss of other coverage must be requested within 30 days."},{"id":"H24-03","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"Which statement about guaranteed issue is accurate?","options":["HIPAA required insurers to accept small employers regardless of employee health, and the ACA extended guaranteed issue to all comprehensive individual coverage","Guaranteed issue applies only to disability income policies","Guaranteed issue allows insurers to charge sick applicants any premium","Guaranteed issue was repealed by the ACA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HIPAA covered small groups; the ACA extended the rule to individuals and all small groups."},{"id":"H24-04","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"Under the original HIPAA rules, a pre-existing condition exclusion in a group plan could last no longer than how many months for a timely enrollee?","options":["6 months","12 months","18 months","24 months"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Timely enrollees faced at most a 12-month exclusion, reduced by creditable coverage."},{"id":"H24-05","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"Under HIPAA, prior coverage stopped counting as creditable coverage after a break of:","options":["30 days or more","45 days or more","63 days or more","90 days or more"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A significant break of 63 days or more ended creditable coverage."},{"id":"H24-06","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"How did the Affordable Care Act change HIPAA’s pre-existing condition rules for comprehensive medical plans?","options":["It lengthened the exclusion to 24 months","It eliminated pre-existing condition exclusions entirely","It limited exclusions to children only","It made no change"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Comprehensive individual and group plans may no longer exclude pre-existing conditions at all."},{"id":"H24-07","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"An insurer wants to stop renewing Priya’s small group plan because the group’s claims were high. Under HIPAA’s renewability rules the insurer:","options":["May nonrenew for high claims","May not nonrenew for claims experience; it may raise class premiums within rating rules","May nonrenew with 30 days’ notice","Must refund the year’s premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guaranteed renewability bars nonrenewal for health or claims; premiums may still be adjusted by class."},{"id":"H24-08","lesson":"24","domain":5,"prompt":"Which is a permitted reason for an insurer to nonrenew group medical coverage under HIPAA?","options":["The group’s average age increased","An employee developed cancer","The employer failed to pay premiums","The producer changed agencies"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nonpayment of premium is one of the narrow permitted grounds."}]},{"id":"25","number":25,"title":"HSAs, HRAs and high-deductible plans","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H25-01","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"Which coverage must a person have to be eligible to contribute to a health savings account?","options":["Any comprehensive medical plan","A qualifying high-deductible health plan","Medicare Part A","A traditional PPO with a $500 deductible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HSA contributions require coverage under a qualifying HDHP and no disqualifying other coverage."},{"id":"H25-02","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"For 2026, the minimum annual deductible for a self-only high-deductible health plan is:","options":["$1,000","$1,700","$3,400","$4,400"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 2026 self-only HDHP minimum deductible is $1,700."},{"id":"H25-03","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"Rosa, age 57, has self-only HDHP coverage all of 2026. Her maximum HSA contribution for the year is:","options":["$4,400","$5,400","$8,750","$9,750"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The $4,400 self-only limit plus the $1,000 catch-up for age 55 and older equals $5,400."},{"id":"H25-04","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"Dennis enrolls in Medicare at 65 while keeping his HDHP through work. After enrolling in Medicare he may:","options":["Continue contributing to his HSA without limit","No longer contribute to the HSA but may spend the balance tax-free on qualified expenses","Contribute only the catch-up amount","Convert the HSA into an HRA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Enrollment in Medicare stops new HSA contributions, but existing funds remain available tax-free for qualified medical expenses."},{"id":"H25-05","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"A 45-year-old withdraws $2,000 from her HSA to pay for a vacation. The tax result is:","options":["Tax-free","Ordinary income tax only","Ordinary income tax plus a 20 percent penalty","A 10 percent penalty only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nonqualified withdrawals before age 65 are taxed as income plus a 20 percent penalty."},{"id":"H25-06","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"Which statement about a health reimbursement arrangement is correct?","options":["Employees may contribute pre-tax","Only the employer may fund it, and the employer owns it","It must be paired with a high-deductible health plan","Unused balances automatically belong to the employee when she leaves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HRAs are employer-funded, employer-owned arrangements."},{"id":"H25-07","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"Which feature belongs to an HSA but NOT to an HRA?","options":["Tax-free reimbursement of medical expenses","Portability when the employee changes jobs","Employer funding","Use for deductibles and coinsurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The HSA belongs to the individual and moves with her; an HRA stays with the employer."},{"id":"H25-08","lesson":"25","domain":5,"prompt":"Marcus has a family HDHP and also a general purpose health flexible spending account through his employer. His HSA eligibility is:","options":["Unaffected","Lost, because a general purpose health FSA is disqualifying coverage","Limited to the catch-up contribution","Doubled"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only a limited-purpose dental and vision FSA preserves HSA eligibility."}]},{"id":"26","number":26,"title":"The Affordable Care Act: coverage rules","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H26-01","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"Marcus’s 24-year-old daughter is married, employed and lives in another state. Under the Affordable Care Act, may she remain on his plan?","options":["No, because she is married","No, because she lives outside Maryland","Yes, until she turns 26, regardless of marital, student, residence or financial status","Yes, but only if she is a full-time student"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The ACA requires dependent coverage to age 26 without conditions on marriage, school, residence or dependency."},{"id":"H26-02","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"Elena receives an in-network screening mammogram recommended by the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force. Under a non-grandfathered plan she pays:","options":["Her deductible","A copayment","Nothing","20 percent coinsurance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Recommended preventive services must be covered without cost sharing when delivered in network."},{"id":"H26-03","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"An insurer discovers an innocent omission on Marcus’s application after he files a large claim. Under the ACA the insurer may:","options":["Rescind the policy retroactively","Not rescind, because rescission is limited to fraud or intentional misrepresentation","Rescind with 10 days’ notice","Rescind only the portion covering the omitted condition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rescission is allowed only for fraud or intentional misrepresentation of a material fact, with 30 days’ advance notice."},{"id":"H26-04","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"A comprehensive plan proposes a $2 million lifetime maximum on all benefits. Under the ACA this is:","options":["Permitted if disclosed","Prohibited for essential health benefits","Permitted for plans issued before 2020","Required by federal law"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lifetime and annual dollar limits on essential health benefits are banned."},{"id":"H26-05","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"Which action would cause a grandfathered plan to lose its status?","options":["Adding a new covered benefit","Significantly increasing the deductible or coinsurance","Renewing the plan with the same terms","Changing the plan’s third-party administrator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Significant cuts in benefits or increases in cost sharing or employee contributions end grandfathered status."},{"id":"H26-06","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"Which rule applies to grandfathered plans as well as non-grandfathered plans?","options":["Preventive care without cost sharing","Coverage of children to age 26","Essential health benefits in every category","The ACA appeals process"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dependent coverage to 26, the ban on lifetime limits and the rescission limits apply to all plans including grandfathered ones."},{"id":"H26-07","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the ten essential health benefit categories?","options":["Adult cosmetic surgery","Maternity and newborn care","Long-term custodial care","Adult dental care"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maternity and newborn care is one of the ten categories."},{"id":"H26-08","lesson":"26","domain":5,"prompt":"Rosa’s cancer policy carries a $50,000 lifetime maximum while her medical plan has none. Why is that permitted?","options":["Because cancer policies are issued by nonprofits","Because specified disease coverage is an excepted benefit outside the ACA’s dollar-limit rules","Because lifetime limits are allowed on any policy under $100,000","Because Rosa is self-employed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Specified disease and other excepted benefits are not subject to the ban on lifetime and annual limits."}]},{"id":"27","number":27,"title":"The Affordable Care Act: plans, appeals and subsidies","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H27-01","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"A marketplace plan is expected to pay about 70 percent of covered costs for a standard population. Its metal level is:","options":["Bronze","Silver","Gold","Platinum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Silver plans have an actuarial value of about 70 percent."},{"id":"H27-02","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"Who may enroll in a catastrophic marketplace plan?","options":["Anyone over 65","People under 30 or those with a hardship exemption","Only small employers","Only people receiving premium tax credits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Catastrophic plans are limited to people under 30 or with a hardship or affordability exemption."},{"id":"H27-03","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"Under SHOP in Maryland, premiums for a small employer’s plan are typically paid through:","options":["Separate bills from each employee to each carrier","A single consolidated bill paid by the employer","The employees’ individual tax returns","The Maryland Insurance Administration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SHOP consolidates the group’s premiums into one employer bill and distributes payment to carriers."},{"id":"H27-04","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"A member’s claim is denied. Under the ACA appeals rules she must request an internal appeal within:","options":["30 days","60 days","180 days","One year"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Members have 180 days from the adverse determination to request an internal appeal."},{"id":"H27-05","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"A Maryland carrier upholds its denial of a medically necessary procedure after an internal grievance. The member’s next step is:","options":["A lawsuit only","A complaint to the Maryland Insurance Administration for external review, whose decision binds the carrier","A petition to the NAIC","A second internal appeal to the same reviewer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The MIA conducts external review with independent medical input and its determination binds the carrier."},{"id":"H27-06","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"Cost-sharing reductions that lower deductibles and copayments for eligible enrollees apply only to:","options":["Bronze plans","Silver plans","Gold plans","Catastrophic plans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cost-sharing reductions are built into silver plan variants for enrollees between 100 and 250 percent of poverty."},{"id":"H27-07","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"Which statement about the individual mandate is accurate for a Maryland resident today?","options":["A federal penalty of $695 applies","The federal penalty is $0 and Maryland imposes no state penalty","Maryland charges a 2.5 percent income penalty","The penalty is paid to the insurer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The federal payment was reduced to zero in 2019 and Maryland has no individual penalty, using Easy Enrollment instead."},{"id":"H27-08","lesson":"27","domain":5,"prompt":"An employer with 120 full-time employees offers no health coverage. Under the ACA it may owe:","options":["Nothing, because the mandate applies only to individuals","The employer shared responsibility payment, because it is an applicable large employer","A fine to the guaranty corporation","Back premiums to its employees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Employers with 50 or more full-time equivalent employees must offer affordable minimum value coverage or pay the shared responsibility payment."}]},{"id":"28","number":28,"title":"Qualifying for disability benefits","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H28-01","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa can no longer work as an electrician but could manage a store. Under a true own-occupation definition she is:","options":["Not disabled, because she can do other work","Totally disabled, because she cannot perform the duties of her regular occupation","Partially disabled only","Disabled only if she refuses the store job"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Own occupation pays when the insured cannot perform the material duties of her own job."},{"id":"H28-02","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"Under an any-occupation definition, the same electrician who could manage a store is:","options":["Totally disabled","Not totally disabled, because she is suited by training and experience for other work","Presumptively disabled","Automatically entitled to residual benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because she can perform work for which she is reasonably suited, she fails the any-occupation test."},{"id":"H28-03","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"A group long-term disability plan pays if the insured cannot perform her own occupation for 24 months and thereafter only if she cannot perform any suitable occupation. This is a:","options":["Pure own-occupation definition","Split definition","Presumptive definition","Income replacement contract"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A definition that changes from own occupation to any occupation after a period is a split definition."},{"id":"H28-04","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"A policy pays benefits in proportion to the insured’s actual loss of earned income, with no requirement that she stop working. This describes:","options":["An any-occupation policy","A pure loss of income contract","A presumptive disability clause","A waiver of premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Income replacement contracts pay based on dollars of income lost."},{"id":"H28-05","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"Which loss would trigger a presumptive disability provision?","options":["A broken leg expected to heal in eight weeks","Total and permanent loss of sight in both eyes","Loss of one finger","Chronic back pain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Loss of sight in both eyes is a classic presumptive disability."},{"id":"H28-06","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"Under a presumptive disability provision, an insured who loses the use of both legs but later earns income from a desk job:","options":["Loses all benefits","Continues to receive total disability benefits for the benefit period","Receives only partial benefits","Must repay benefits received"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Listed losses are presumed total and permanent, so benefits continue regardless of work."},{"id":"H28-07","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"Why do disability policies require the insured to be under a physician’s regular care?","options":["To increase premiums","To verify that the disability continues and that it is being treated","To satisfy Medicare rules","To allow the insurer to choose the insured’s doctor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Regular care lets the insurer confirm ongoing disability through medical evidence and ensures treatment."},{"id":"H28-08","lesson":"28","domain":4,"prompt":"Which definition of disability most closely resembles the Social Security standard?","options":["True own occupation","Modified own occupation","Any occupation","Presumptive disability"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Social Security requires inability to engage in any substantial gainful activity, resembling any occupation."}]},{"id":"29","number":29,"title":"Individual disability income: the basic plan","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H29-01","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa earns $6,000 a month and her insurer limits disability benefits to 60 percent of income. The maximum monthly indemnity she can buy is:","options":["$3,000","$3,600","$4,200","$6,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sixty percent of $6,000 is $3,600."},{"id":"H29-02","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"The period after disability begins during which no benefits are payable is the:","options":["Probationary period","Elimination period","Benefit period","Grace period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The elimination period is the waiting period at the start of each claim."},{"id":"H29-03","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"Compared with a 30-day elimination period, a 180-day elimination period will:","options":["Increase the premium","Lower the premium","Shorten the benefit period","Eliminate the waiver of premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A longer elimination period means fewer and later claims, so the policy costs less."},{"id":"H29-04","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa becomes disabled on March 1 with a 90-day elimination period and benefits paid monthly in arrears. Her first check will arrive approximately:","options":["March 1","Late May","Late June","The following March"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The first month of benefits accrues through late June and is paid at the end of that month."},{"id":"H29-05","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"Under a waiver of premium provision, after the insured has been disabled for 90 days the insurer typically:","options":["Cancels the policy","Waives future premiums during disability and refunds premiums paid during the 90 days","Doubles the monthly benefit","Reduces the benefit period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Waiver of premium excuses premiums while disability continues and refunds those paid in the waiting period."},{"id":"H29-06","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"A self-employed electrician without workers compensation coverage needs a disability policy that covers injuries on and off the job. She should buy:","options":["Nonoccupational coverage","Occupational, or 24-hour, coverage","A hospital indemnity policy","Credit disability"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Occupational coverage pays regardless of where the disability arose."},{"id":"H29-07","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"Which disability is most likely EXCLUDED under a typical individual policy?","options":["A back injury from a weekend fall","Complications of pregnancy","An injury sustained while committing a felony","Cancer diagnosed two years after issue"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Losses while committing a felony are a standard exclusion."},{"id":"H29-08","lesson":"29","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa’s policy excludes sickness that begins in the first 15 days after issue but applies a 90-day wait to every claim. The 15-day provision is the:","options":["Elimination period","Probationary period","Benefit period","Recurrent disability period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A one-time exclusion of early sickness at policy start is the probationary period."}]},{"id":"30","number":30,"title":"Partial, residual and social insurance coordination","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H30-01","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"A policy pays 50 percent of the total disability benefit for up to six months when the insured returns to part-time work after a total disability. This is a:","options":["Residual disability benefit","Partial disability benefit","Presumptive disability benefit","Social insurance supplement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A flat percentage for a limited time after total disability is the traditional partial disability benefit."},{"id":"H30-02","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa’s total disability benefit is $3,600. Her income falls from $6,000 to $3,600 a month. Under a residual disability provision her monthly benefit is:","options":["$1,800","$1,440","$2,160","$3,600"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 40 percent income loss times the $3,600 benefit equals $1,440."},{"id":"H30-03","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"Which statement distinguishes residual from partial disability benefits?","options":["Residual benefits require a prior period of total disability; partial benefits do not","Residual benefits track actual income loss for the benefit period; partial benefits pay a flat percentage for a limited time","Partial benefits are always larger","Residual benefits are available only in group plans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Residual pays in proportion to lost income for as long as the loss lasts; partial pays a flat fraction briefly."},{"id":"H30-04","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"An insured’s income falls 15 percent after a disability. Under a typical residual provision requiring at least a 20 percent loss, the benefit is:","options":["15 percent of the total benefit","Nothing, because the loss is below the threshold","The full total disability benefit","50 percent of the total benefit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Most residual provisions pay nothing until income loss reaches 20 percent."},{"id":"H30-05","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"A rider pays an extra $1,200 a month during the first year of disability while the insured awaits a Social Security decision, then stops regardless of the outcome. This is a(n):","options":["Social insurance supplement","Additional monthly benefit","Cost-of-living adjustment","Future increase option"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An additional monthly benefit is a time-limited bridge paid regardless of Social Security’s decision."},{"id":"H30-06","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"Under a social insurance supplement rider, if Social Security approves the insured’s disability claim, the SIS benefit is:","options":["Paid in full in addition to Social Security","Reduced or eliminated by the Social Security benefit","Doubled","Converted to a lump sum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The supplement is offset dollar for dollar when Social Security pays."},{"id":"H30-07","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"A group long-term disability plan promises 60 percent of salary, reduced by Social Security disability benefits. An employee earning $5,000 receives $1,200 from Social Security. The plan pays:","options":["$3,000","$1,800","$1,200","$4,200"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sixty percent of $5,000 is $3,000; less the $1,200 Social Security benefit leaves $1,800."},{"id":"H30-08","lesson":"30","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa’s income loss reaches 80 percent and her residual provision treats a loss of 75 percent or more as total disability. Her benefit is:","options":["80 percent of $3,600","The full $3,600","50 percent of $3,600","Nothing until the loss reaches 100 percent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A loss at or above the policy’s threshold is treated as total disability, paying the full benefit."}]},{"id":"31","number":31,"title":"Disability riders, cash benefits and refunds","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H31-01","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"A rider increases the monthly disability benefit each year during a claim to reflect changes in the consumer price index. This is a:","options":["Future increase option","Cost-of-living adjustment rider","Waiver of premium","Social insurance supplement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A COLA rider raises benefits during a continuing disability to offset inflation."},{"id":"H31-02","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"A rider lets an insured purchase additional monthly benefit at specified dates without medical underwriting, subject to proof of income. This is the:","options":["Cost-of-living adjustment","Future increase option","Return of premium rider","Rehabilitation benefit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The future increase, or guaranteed insurability, option permits scheduled increases without evidence of insurability."},{"id":"H31-03","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"Which statement about a cost-of-living adjustment rider is correct?","options":["It raises the benefit every year the policy is in force, even before a claim","It raises the benefit only during a disability, typically after the first year","It is free with every policy","It lowers the premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The adjustment applies to benefits being paid during a continuing disability, usually starting after 12 months."},{"id":"H31-04","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"A disability rider adds extra monthly benefit at a premium that increases each year and may be converted later to level-premium coverage. This is a(n):","options":["Annual renewable term rider","Residual disability rider","Presumptive disability rider","Return of premium rider"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Term-style coverage with annually increasing premiums and conversion rights is the annual renewable term rider."},{"id":"H31-05","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"An insured suffers a minor injury that does not keep her from working but requires $800 of medical treatment. Which disability policy benefit could respond?","options":["Monthly indemnity","Medical reimbursement benefit for nondisabling injury","Waiver of premium","Cost-of-living adjustment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The medical reimbursement benefit pays medical expenses for an injury that does not disable, up to a stated amount."},{"id":"H31-06","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"A disability policy pays for a vocational retraining program to help the insured return to work, in addition to monthly benefits. This is the:","options":["Rehabilitation benefit","Capital sum","Residual benefit","Change of occupation provision"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rehabilitation benefits fund retraining that returns the insured to work."},{"id":"H31-07","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"A return of premium rider typically:","options":["Refunds all premiums with interest every year","Refunds a percentage of premiums paid, less claims, at set intervals if the policy stays in force, at a substantially higher premium","Eliminates the elimination period","Is required by Maryland law"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The rider returns premiums less benefits paid at intervals, and it raises the premium considerably."},{"id":"H31-08","lesson":"31","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa’s total disability benefit is fixed at $3,600. Which rider addresses the risk that her income grows and she becomes uninsurable?","options":["Cost-of-living adjustment","Future increase option","Medical reimbursement","Return of premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FIO guarantees the right to buy more benefit later regardless of health."}]},{"id":"32","number":32,"title":"Underwriting disability income","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H32-01","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"In disability income underwriting, the applicant’s occupation primarily determines:","options":["The free-look period","The occupation class, available definitions and maximum benefit period","The grace period","Whether the policy is participating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Occupation classes drive which definitions, benefit periods and limits are available."},{"id":"H32-02","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"Which applicant is MOST likely to qualify for a true own-occupation definition with the longest benefit period?","options":["A roofer","A commercial fisherman","An accountant","A stunt performer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Office and professional occupations receive the best class and the most favorable terms."},{"id":"H32-03","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"An insurer will not issue total disability coverage from all sources above 60 percent of an applicant’s earned income. This rule is a(n):","options":["Elimination period","Participation limit","Probationary period","Exclusion rider"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Participation limits cap total coverage from all sources relative to income."},{"id":"H32-04","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa earns $6,000 a month and already has a $1,000 group disability benefit. With a 60 percent participation limit, the maximum individual benefit the insurer will issue is:","options":["$3,600","$2,600","$4,600","$1,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sixty percent of $6,000 is $3,600; less the $1,000 group benefit leaves $2,600."},{"id":"H32-05","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"Why do insurers reduce the replacement ratio for high-income disability applicants?","options":["Because high earners are less likely to pay premiums","Because tax-free benefits near full income would remove the incentive to return to work and high earners have other resources","Because state law caps benefits at $5,000","Because high earners cannot be rated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lower ratios preserve the work incentive and reflect other resources available to high earners."},{"id":"H32-06","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"An applicant’s hobby is private piloting. The underwriter is MOST likely to:","options":["Decline all coverage automatically","Attach an aviation exclusion or apply a rating","Ignore the hobby","Shorten the free-look period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insurers commonly exclude or rate hazardous avocations such as aviation."},{"id":"H32-07","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"Instead of declining an applicant with a chronic knee condition, the insurer issues the policy with a longer elimination period and a shorter benefit period than requested. This is an example of:","options":["A rated premium","Modified benefits","An exclusion rider","Presumptive disability"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Changing the waiting period, benefit period or amount is a modified benefit offer."},{"id":"H32-08","lesson":"32","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa accepts a disability policy with an exclusion rider for her right shoulder. Two years later the shoulder causes a disability. The insurer:","options":["Pays full benefits","Pays nothing for that disability because the condition is excluded, unless the rider was removed","Pays half benefits","Cancels the policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An exclusion rider removes claims caused by the named condition; the insured may request review to remove it."}]},{"id":"33","number":33,"title":"Group and business disability coverage","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H33-01","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"Which feature is characteristic of group rather than individual disability income coverage?","options":["Benefits chosen as a fixed dollar amount by the insured","No individual medical underwriting and benefits set as a percentage of salary","Full portability when the insured changes jobs","Tax-free benefits regardless of who paid the premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Group plans cover eligible employees without individual underwriting and replace a percentage of salary."},{"id":"H33-02","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"A short-term disability plan most commonly pays benefits for:","options":["Up to 13 to 26 weeks","Two to five years","To age 65","For life"],"answer":0,"explanation":"STD benefit periods typically run 13 to 26 weeks, occasionally 52."},{"id":"H33-03","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"Priya’s STD plan pays for 13 weeks. To avoid a gap, her LTD plan’s elimination period should be:","options":["30 days","90 days","180 days","365 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Thirteen weeks is about 90 days, so a 90-day LTD elimination period hands off without a gap."},{"id":"H33-04","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"A group LTD plan replaces 60 percent of salary to a $6,000 monthly maximum, reduced by Social Security. An employee earning $15,000 a month receives $2,000 from Social Security. The plan pays:","options":["$9,000","$7,000","$6,000","$4,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sixty percent of $15,000 is $9,000, capped at $6,000, less the $2,000 offset leaves $4,000."},{"id":"H33-05","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"In a key employee disability policy, who receives the benefit?","options":["The key employee","The business","The key employee’s spouse","The Social Security Administration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The business owns the policy, pays the premium and receives benefits to offset the loss."},{"id":"H33-06","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"Which expense is NOT reimbursed by a business overhead expense policy?","options":["Office rent","Employee salaries","The disabled owner’s own salary","Utility bills"],"answer":2,"explanation":"BOE excludes the owner’s compensation and profit, which personal disability coverage addresses."},{"id":"H33-07","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"A disability buyout policy typically has a long elimination period of one to two years because:","options":["Premiums are lower for short elimination periods","The policy waits to confirm the disability is permanent before funding a purchase of the owner’s interest","State law requires it","Benefits are paid monthly for life"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A buyout is irreversible, so the policy waits until permanence is clear."},{"id":"H33-08","lesson":"33","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa, a sole proprietor, wants coverage that pays her shop’s rent and her employee’s wages if she is disabled. She needs:","options":["Key employee disability insurance","Business overhead expense insurance","A disability buyout policy","Group long-term disability"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BOE reimburses the fixed expenses of a business whose owner is disabled."}]},{"id":"34","number":34,"title":"Social Security disability and workers compensation","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H34-01","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"To receive Social Security disability insurance benefits, a 41-year-old worker must generally have earned:","options":["6 work credits","20 credits in the 10 years before disability","40 credits in the last 5 years","No credits; benefits are needs-based"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workers in their 30s and 40s generally need 20 credits earned in the ten years before disability, within the 40-credit rule."},{"id":"H34-02","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"Social Security defines disability as the inability to engage in any substantial gainful activity due to an impairment expected to:","options":["Last at least 6 months","Last at least 12 months or result in death","Last at least 24 months","Prevent the worker from performing her own occupation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The impairment must last or be expected to last at least 12 months or end in death."},{"id":"H34-03","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"A worker becomes disabled on January 10. Under Social Security’s five-month waiting period, benefits are first payable for:","options":["January","February","July","The following January"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Five full months, February through June, pass before benefits begin with July."},{"id":"H34-04","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"After receiving Social Security disability benefits for 24 months, a 50-year-old worker becomes eligible for:","options":["Medicaid automatically","Medicare","Full retirement benefits","Workers compensation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SSDI recipients qualify for Medicare after 24 months of benefits regardless of age."},{"id":"H34-05","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa, an electrician who could manage a store but not practice her trade, applies for Social Security disability. The likely result is:","options":["Approval, because she cannot perform her own occupation","Denial, because she can engage in substantial gainful activity in other work","Approval of partial benefits","Approval after a 90-day wait"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ability to perform any substantial gainful activity defeats a Social Security claim."},{"id":"H34-06","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"Which statement about Maryland workers compensation is correct?","options":["Employees must prove employer negligence to collect","It is a no-fault system and the exclusive remedy against the employer for most work injuries","Only employers with 50 or more employees must carry it","It covers injuries sustained anywhere, on or off the job"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workers compensation pays without regard to fault and generally bars negligence suits against the employer."},{"id":"H34-07","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"A Maryland worker is temporarily unable to work after a job injury. Workers compensation wage replacement is generally:","options":["100 percent of wages","Two-thirds of the average weekly wage, up to a state maximum","50 percent of wages for 13 weeks","A flat $500 a week"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Temporary total disability pays two-thirds of the average weekly wage subject to the state maximum."},{"id":"H34-08","lesson":"34","domain":4,"prompt":"Which benefit is provided by workers compensation but NOT by a typical individual disability income policy?","options":["Monthly income during disability","Unlimited medical expenses for the covered injury","Rehabilitation assistance","Waiver of premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workers compensation pays all reasonable medical costs of a work injury; disability income policies pay income, not medical bills, apart from small reimbursement benefits."}]},{"id":"35","number":35,"title":"Group contracts and eligible groups","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H35-01","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"In an employer group health plan, who is the policyholder of the master contract?","options":["Each covered employee","The employer","The insurer","The Maryland Insurance Administration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The employer holds the master policy as policyholder."},{"id":"H35-02","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"The document given to each covered employee that summarizes benefits, exclusions and claim procedures is the:","options":["Master policy","Certificate of coverage","Producer’s report","Conditional receipt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Employees receive certificates of coverage describing the plan."},{"id":"H35-03","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"A 2,000-employee hospital’s health premium for next year is based largely on the hospital’s own claims this year. This pricing method is:","options":["Community rating","Experience rating","Modified community rating","Participation rating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Experience rating uses the group’s claims history to set premiums."},{"id":"H35-04","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"Under Maryland’s small employer rating rules, a carrier may vary Priya’s group premium based on:","options":["The group’s claims experience","The employees’ health status","Age, tobacco use, geographic area and family composition","The bakery’s annual revenue"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Modified community rating permits only these factors."},{"id":"H35-05","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"Why do state laws require that a group be formed for a purpose other than obtaining insurance?","options":["To increase premium taxes","To limit adverse selection by ensuring the group’s health is roughly average","To protect producers’ commissions","To comply with the NAIC’s licensing rules"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A group assembled only to buy insurance would attract those most likely to claim."},{"id":"H35-06","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"A bank insures its borrowers so that loan payments are made if they become disabled. The bank is the policyholder and the borrowers are insured. This is a(n):","options":["Employer group","Creditor group","Association group","Labor union group"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A creditor group covers debtors with the lender as policyholder."},{"id":"H35-07","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"Forty small bakeries in Maryland pool their employees into one trust to buy health coverage. This arrangement is a:","options":["Credit union plan","Multiple employer trust, regulated as a multiple employer welfare arrangement","Blanket policy","Fraternal benefit society"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pooling unrelated small employers forms a multiple employer trust, which Maryland regulates as a MEWA."},{"id":"H35-08","lesson":"35","domain":6,"prompt":"Which organization is LEAST likely to qualify as an eligible group for group health insurance?","options":["A professional association formed 15 years ago for continuing education","A credit union covering its members","A labor union covering its members","A club formed last month solely to buy health insurance for its members"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A group formed solely to obtain insurance fails the purpose test."}]},{"id":"36","number":36,"title":"Group marketing and employer underwriting","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H36-01","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"An employer’s enrollment brochure describes a group plan as covering all prescriptions in full, although the plan has copayments. Who bears responsibility for the misleading material?","options":["No one, because brochures are not contracts","The insurer, which is responsible for advertising of its plan, and potentially the employer for the misstatement","Only the employees who relied on it","The Maryland Insurance Administration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insurers are accountable for advertising of their products, and misstatements can create liability for the described benefits."},{"id":"H36-02","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"A group master policy is delivered to an employer in Delaware; some certificate holders live in Maryland. As a general rule, the contract is governed by the law of:","options":["Maryland, because residents live there","Delaware, the state where the master policy was delivered","The state with the lowest premium tax","Whichever state the insurer chooses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The place of delivery of the master policy determines governing law."},{"id":"H36-03","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"Which factor may an insurer NOT use when underwriting a Maryland small employer group?","options":["The industry of the employer","The age distribution of employees","The health status of individual employees","The employer’s payroll administration capability"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Health status may not be used to deny or price small group coverage."},{"id":"H36-04","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"A group’s history of changing insurers frequently is a concern to underwriters because of:","options":["Persistency, since the insurer may not recover its setup costs","Community rating rules","The place-of-delivery rule","Coordination of benefits"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Poor persistency means the insurer may lose the group before recovering acquisition expenses."},{"id":"H36-05","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"In a noncontributory group health plan, the typical participation requirement is:","options":["50 percent","75 percent","100 percent","No minimum"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the employer pays the entire premium, all eligible employees must be covered."},{"id":"H36-06","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"Priya’s contributory plan requires 75 percent participation. She has 20 eligible employees, 4 of whom are covered under spouses’ plans. How many must enroll?","options":["15 of the 16 without other coverage, rounded to at least 12","At least 12 of the 16 employees without other coverage","All 20","At least 15 of the 20, including those with other coverage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Employees covered elsewhere are excluded from the count; 75 percent of the remaining 16 is 12."},{"id":"H36-07","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"Why do carriers require a minimum employer contribution toward group premiums?","options":["To increase producer commissions","To make enrollment attractive to healthy employees and keep the risk pool balanced","Because federal law sets a 90 percent contribution floor","To avoid community rating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An employer subsidy encourages healthy employees to enroll, limiting adverse selection."},{"id":"H36-08","lesson":"36","domain":6,"prompt":"A Maryland small employer meets the carrier’s participation and contribution requirements but has two employees with serious illnesses. The carrier:","options":["May decline the group because of the illnesses","Must issue coverage; health status cannot be used to deny a small group","May issue but exclude the two employees","May charge the group a health-based surcharge"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small employer coverage is guaranteed issue once participation and contribution rules are met."}]},{"id":"37","number":37,"title":"Group eligibility, enrollment and coordination","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H37-01","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"Under the Affordable Care Act, a group health plan’s waiting period for new employees may not exceed:","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","180 days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Federal law caps group plan waiting periods at 90 days."},{"id":"H37-02","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"After the waiting period ends, the window during which a new employee may enroll in the group plan without evidence of insurability is the:","options":["Grace period","Eligibility period","Elimination period","Probationary period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The eligibility period, often 31 days, follows the waiting period."},{"id":"H37-03","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"An employee waived group coverage at hire and has no qualifying event. When may she enroll?","options":["Immediately, upon request","At the next annual open enrollment","Never","Only with a physician’s statement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Late enrollees may join at the next open enrollment."},{"id":"H37-04","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"An employee’s spouse loses his own employer coverage on September 3. Under HIPAA special enrollment the employee may add him if she requests enrollment by:","options":["September 10","October 3","December 3","The next open enrollment only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Special enrollment after loss of coverage must be requested within 30 days."},{"id":"H37-05","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"A Maryland group plan must enroll a child named in a medical child support order:","options":["Only at the next open enrollment","Regardless of enrollment period restrictions","Only if the child lives with the employee","Only if the employee agrees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland law requires enrollment of a child under a medical child support order without regard to enrollment periods."},{"id":"H37-06","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"A child of married parents is covered under both parents’ group plans. The primary plan is determined by:","options":["The parent with the higher salary","The parent whose birthday falls earlier in the calendar year","The parent who enrolled first","The older parent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The birthday rule uses the earlier birthday in the year."},{"id":"H37-07","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"Which plan feature is designed primarily to prevent employees from joining only when they expect to need care?","options":["Coordination of benefits","Limited enrollment windows and waiting periods","The summary plan description","Experience rating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Enrollment windows and waiting periods stop people from timing their enrollment around illness."},{"id":"H37-08","lesson":"37","domain":6,"prompt":"A new full-time baker starts March 1 with a 60-day waiting period and a 31-day eligibility period. If she enrolls May 10, her coverage most likely takes effect:","options":["March 1","May 10","June 1","The following January 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Coverage typically becomes effective on the first day of the month after enrollment, June 1."}]},{"id":"38","number":38,"title":"Changing carriers, continuation and conversion","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H38-01","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"An employer changes insurers on July 1. An employee had already met $1,500 of her $2,000 deductible under the old plan. Under a deductible carryover provision the new plan:","options":["Requires a new $2,000 deductible","Credits the $1,500 toward the new plan’s deductible for the rest of the year","Waives all deductibles for the year","Refunds the $1,500"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carryover credits amounts already applied under the prior plan."},{"id":"H38-02","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"When an employer switches carriers, the new insurer must cover an employee who is currently disabled and receiving benefits as the old plan would have. This is the:","options":["Extension of benefits provision","No-loss no-gain rule","Conversion privilege","Birthday rule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No-loss no-gain ensures a carrier change neither reduces nor expands coverage for ongoing claims."},{"id":"H38-03","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"An employer terminates its group health policy entirely. An employee who was totally disabled at the time typically:","options":["Loses all coverage immediately","Continues to receive benefits for the disabling condition for a period under the extension of benefits provision","Must sue the insurer","Receives a refund of lifetime premiums"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Extension of benefits continues coverage for the existing disability for a stated period after the policy ends."},{"id":"H38-04","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"Priya’s bakery has 20 employees. A baker quits voluntarily. Under COBRA she may continue the group plan for up to:","options":["6 months","18 months","29 months","36 months"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Voluntary or involuntary termination, other than gross misconduct, allows 18 months."},{"id":"H38-05","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"A 12-employee Maryland business lays off a worker who had been covered for two years. Which continuation right applies?","options":["COBRA, because all employers are covered","Maryland continuation: up to 18 months, elected within 45 days","No continuation, because the employer is too small","Conversion only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland continuation applies to groups of any size after loss of employment not for cause, with a 45-day election and 18 months of coverage."},{"id":"H38-06","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"Under Maryland law, how long may a surviving spouse continue group coverage after the insured employee’s death?","options":["45 days","12 months","18 months","Indefinitely"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maryland allows 18 months for a qualified surviving spouse and dependent children."},{"id":"H38-07","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"A person whose group coverage ends wants an individual policy from the same insurer without a medical exam. The conversion privilege generally requires application within:","options":["10 days","31 days","60 days","180 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conversion must be requested within 31 days of termination."},{"id":"H38-08","lesson":"38","domain":6,"prompt":"An employee returns from two years of military service and rejoins the group plan. The plan must:","options":["Impose a new 90-day waiting period","Reinstate coverage without a new waiting period or exclusions, except for service-connected conditions","Charge 150 percent of the premium","Refuse enrollment until open enrollment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Federal law requires reinstatement without new waiting periods or exclusions other than service-connected conditions."}]},{"id":"39","number":39,"title":"Small employer plans in Maryland","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H39-01","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"Under Maryland law, a small employer is one that employed an average of how many employees during the preceding calendar year?","options":["Not more than 25","Not more than 50","Between 2 and 50","Not more than 100"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland defines a small employer as averaging not more than 50 employees."},{"id":"H39-02","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"An employer has 30 full-time employees and part-time employees who together work 2,400 hours a month. Its employee count for the small employer test is:","options":["30","40","50","54"],"answer":2,"explanation":"2,400 hours divided by 120 equals 20 full-time equivalents; 30 plus 20 is 50, which still qualifies."},{"id":"H39-03","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"A carrier may refuse to issue coverage to a Maryland small employer because:","options":["Two employees have chronic illnesses","The group fails the carrier’s uniformly applied participation requirement","The employees’ average age is high","The employer is in a hazardous industry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Participation and contribution requirements applied uniformly are lawful grounds."},{"id":"H39-04","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"Which factor may a Maryland carrier use to set a non-grandfathered small group’s premium?","options":["The group’s prior claims","Gender of employees","Tobacco use, up to a 1.5 to 1 ratio","The employer’s industry classification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tobacco use within 1.5 to 1 is a permitted factor, along with age, area and family composition."},{"id":"H39-05","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"Under Maryland’s small group rules, adult age rating may vary premiums by no more than:","options":["1.5 to 1","2 to 1","3 to 1","5 to 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Age rating is limited to a 3 to 1 ratio."},{"id":"H39-06","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"A small employer’s carrier announces midyear that premiums will rise because an employee was diagnosed with cancer. This is:","options":["Permitted with 30 days’ notice","Prohibited, because rates are set for the plan year and health status may not be used","Permitted for groups under 10 employees","Permitted if the employer agrees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small group rates are fixed for the plan year and may never reflect health status."},{"id":"H39-07","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"Which is a permitted ground for a carrier to nonrenew a Maryland small group plan?","options":["High claims in the prior year","The employer’s failure to pay premiums","An employee reaching age 60","The group adding dependents"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nonpayment of premium is a lawful ground for nonrenewal."},{"id":"H39-08","lesson":"39","domain":6,"prompt":"Before selling a small group plan, a Maryland carrier must disclose to the employer:","options":["Only the premium","Benefits, exclusions, rating practices, renewability terms and participation requirements","The names of its other small group clients","Its reinsurance arrangements"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires plain disclosure of these plan features so employers can compare offers."}]},{"id":"40","number":40,"title":"Dental insurance","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H40-01","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"Which dental service is classified as diagnostic and preventive?","options":["A crown","A root canal","A routine cleaning with x-rays","A bridge"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cleanings, exams and x-rays are diagnostic and preventive services."},{"id":"H40-02","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"A root canal is an example of which dental treatment category?","options":["Periodontics","Endodontics","Prosthodontics","Orthodontics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Endodontics treats the interior of the tooth, including root canals."},{"id":"H40-03","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"A dental plan pays a fixed $90 for any filling regardless of the dentist’s charge. This plan is:","options":["Nonscheduled","Scheduled","Integrated","Capitated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A flat dollar amount per procedure defines a scheduled plan."},{"id":"H40-04","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"Under a 100/80/50 nonscheduled plan with a $50 deductible that is waived for preventive care, Marcus has a $1,200 crown. Ignoring the annual maximum, the plan pays:","options":["$600","$575","$960","$1,150"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After the $50 deductible, 50 percent of the remaining $1,150 is $575."},{"id":"H40-05","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"A dental plan has a $1,500 annual maximum. A patient who has already received $1,300 of benefits this year needs a $1,000 procedure covered at 50 percent. The plan pays:","options":["$500","$200","$1,000","$0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The plan pays the lesser of its coinsurance share and the remaining annual maximum, which is $200."},{"id":"H40-06","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"Before beginning a $2,500 course of treatment, a dentist submits the plan to the insurer, which states in advance what it will pay. This is:","options":["Coordination of benefits","Predetermination of benefits","Concurrent review","A conditional receipt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Predetermination is a pretreatment estimate of benefits for costly dental work."},{"id":"H40-07","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"A group plan applies one combined deductible to both medical and dental expenses. This design is a(n):","options":["Stand-alone dental plan","Integrated deductible","Scheduled plan","Dental HMO"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An integrated deductible combines medical and dental expenses toward one amount."},{"id":"H40-08","lesson":"40","domain":7,"prompt":"Which plan feature is designed to discourage people from enrolling in voluntary dental coverage only when they need a crown?","options":["Free preventive cleanings","A 12-month waiting period for major services","UCR reimbursement","Choice of any dentist"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Waiting periods for basic and major services limit adverse selection."}]},{"id":"41","number":41,"title":"The Maryland Health Benefit Exchange","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H41-01","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"Premium tax credits and cost-sharing reductions are available only for coverage purchased through the:","options":["SHOP Exchange","Individual Exchange","Insurer directly","Maryland Insurance Administration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Subsidies for individuals and families flow only through the Individual Exchange."},{"id":"H41-02","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"Maryland’s annual open enrollment for individual coverage runs from:","options":["October 15 to December 7","November 1 to December 15","November 1 to January 15","January 1 to March 31"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maryland extends open enrollment through January 15."},{"id":"H41-03","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"A Maryland resident loses job-based coverage on June 30. How long does she have to enroll in an Individual Exchange plan under a special enrollment period?","options":["30 days","45 days","60 days","Until the next open enrollment only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exchange special enrollment periods generally last 60 days."},{"id":"H41-04","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"Which event does NOT open a special enrollment period on the Individual Exchange?","options":["Marriage","Birth of a child","A voluntary decision to drop coverage midyear","A permanent move to a new rating area"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Voluntarily dropping coverage is not a qualifying loss of coverage."},{"id":"H41-05","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"In SHOP, an employer selects a metal level and allows employees to choose any plan at that level from any participating carrier. This option is:","options":["Single plan","Employee choice","Community rating","Mandated referral"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Employee choice lets workers pick among plans at the employer’s chosen metal level."},{"id":"H41-06","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"An applicant to Maryland Health Connection is found eligible for Medicaid. The Exchange must:","options":["Deny the application","Refer and enroll the applicant in Medicaid","Sell the applicant a bronze plan","Charge a referral fee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The no-wrong-door rule requires referral and enrollment in the program the applicant qualifies for."},{"id":"H41-07","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"Which person may receive carrier commissions for enrolling consumers through Maryland Health Connection?","options":["A navigator","A certified application counselor","An authorized insurance producer","A Medicaid caseworker"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Licensed producers who complete Exchange authorization may be paid by carriers."},{"id":"H41-08","lesson":"41","domain":0,"prompt":"Maryland’s Easy Enrollment Health Insurance Program works by:","options":["Automatically enrolling every uninsured resident in Medicaid","Letting tax filers check a box so the Exchange can contact them and open a special enrollment window","Imposing a state tax penalty on the uninsured","Requiring employers to enroll all employees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The tax return checkbox shares information with the Exchange, which opens an enrollment opportunity."}]},{"id":"42","number":42,"title":"Medicare Parts A and B","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H42-01","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"Which person is eligible for Medicare before age 65?","options":["Anyone who has retired early","A worker who has received Social Security disability benefits for 24 months","A person with a private disability policy","A person enrolled in Medicaid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Twenty-four months of Social Security disability benefits, or ESRD or ALS, qualifies a person under 65."},{"id":"H42-02","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"Medicare Part A is financed primarily by:","options":["Monthly premiums from beneficiaries","The payroll tax paid by workers and employers","State general revenue","The Maryland guaranty corporation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Part A is funded through the Medicare payroll tax."},{"id":"H42-03","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"Dennis turns 65 on March 20. His initial enrollment period for Medicare runs from:","options":["March 1 to March 31","December 1 to June 30","January 1 to March 31","March 20 to September 20"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Three months before March, the month of March, and three months after gives December through June."},{"id":"H42-04","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"A person who delays Part B enrollment for three full years without employer coverage will pay a Part B premium penalty of:","options":["10 percent for three years","30 percent for as long as she has Part B","A one-time $500 fee","Nothing, because Part B penalties were repealed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ten percent for each full year of delay, 30 percent here, applies for life."},{"id":"H42-05","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"Under Medicare Part A, a benefit period ends when the patient has been out of the hospital or skilled nursing facility for:","options":["30 consecutive days","60 consecutive days","90 consecutive days","One calendar year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A benefit period ends after 60 consecutive days without inpatient hospital or skilled nursing care."},{"id":"H42-06","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"After the annual Part B deductible, Medicare generally pays what share of the approved amount for covered physician services?","options":["50 percent","80 percent","100 percent","20 percent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Part B pays 80 percent of the approved amount; the beneficiary pays 20 percent with no cap."},{"id":"H42-07","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"A physician who accepts Medicare assignment agrees to:","options":["Charge up to 15 percent above the approved amount","Accept the Medicare approved amount as payment in full","Bill the patient the full charge and let the patient file","Treat only Medicare patients"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Assignment means the approved amount is payment in full, with the patient owing only the deductible and coinsurance."},{"id":"H42-08","lesson":"42","domain":8,"prompt":"Which service is NOT covered by Original Medicare Parts A and B?","options":["Inpatient hospital care","Physician office visits","Routine dental care and hearing aids","Hospice care"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Original Medicare excludes routine dental, vision and hearing aids."}]},{"id":"43","number":43,"title":"Medicare Advantage and Part D","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H43-01","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"To enroll in a Medicare Advantage plan, a person must:","options":["Have Part A only","Have Parts A and B and live in the plan’s service area","Also hold a Medicare supplement policy","Be under age 65"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Advantage enrollment requires both parts and residence in the service area."},{"id":"H43-02","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"Which service remains covered by Original Medicare Part A even for a Medicare Advantage member?","options":["Routine dental care","Hospice care","Fitness programs","Hearing aids"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hospice is carved out and paid by Original Medicare."},{"id":"H43-03","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"A protection found in every Medicare Advantage plan but NOT in Original Medicare is:","options":["Coverage of inpatient hospital care","An annual out-of-pocket maximum for Part A and B services","Free Part B premiums","Nationwide access to any provider"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Advantage plans must cap out-of-pocket spending; Original Medicare has no cap."},{"id":"H43-04","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"The Medicare annual election period, when beneficiaries may join, switch or drop Advantage and Part D plans, runs from:","options":["January 1 to March 31","October 15 to December 7","November 1 to January 15","April 1 to June 30"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The annual election period is October 15 through December 7."},{"id":"H43-05","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"Part D drug plans list covered drugs by tier with different cost sharing. This list is the plan’s:","options":["Benefit schedule","Formulary","Summary plan description","Certificate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The formulary is the plan’s list of covered drugs and tiers."},{"id":"H43-06","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"Under the 2026 Part D standard benefit, after a member’s out-of-pocket drug spending reaches $2,100, covered drugs cost:","options":["25 percent coinsurance","5 percent coinsurance","Nothing for the rest of the year","The full price until the deductible resets"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once the annual cap is reached, covered drugs are free for the remainder of the year."},{"id":"H43-07","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"A beneficiary who does not qualify for Extra Help goes 20 full months without Part D or creditable drug coverage after her initial enrollment period. Her Part D late penalty is:","options":["A one-time $100 fee","20 percent of the national base beneficiary premium, generally for as long as she has Part D","1 percent for 20 months, then it ends","Waived automatically after age 70"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Twenty full months at one percent per month gives 20 percent of the national base beneficiary premium, generally for as long as she has Part D."},{"id":"H43-08","lesson":"43","domain":8,"prompt":"Dennis wants to see any provider who accepts Medicare nationwide with predictable costs and is willing to pay higher premiums. Which combination fits?","options":["A Medicare Advantage HMO","Original Medicare with a Medicare supplement and a stand-alone Part D plan","A Medicare Advantage special needs plan","Part A only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Original Medicare plus Medigap and Part D provides nationwide access and predictable costs at a higher premium."}]},{"id":"44","number":44,"title":"Medicare supplement insurance","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H44-01","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"Medicare supplement insurance is designed to:","options":["Replace Original Medicare with a private plan","Pay deductibles, coinsurance and other gaps left by Original Medicare","Provide prescription drug coverage","Cover long-term custodial care"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Medigap pays the gaps in Original Medicare."},{"id":"H44-02","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"Dennis enrolls in Medicare Part B at 65 in March. His Medigap open enrollment period, during which insurers may not use his health against him, lasts until:","options":["The end of March","The end of May","The end of August","The end of the calendar year"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Six months from the first month of Part B enrollment at 65 or older runs March through August."},{"id":"H44-03","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"A Medigap premium that increases each year as the insured grows older, in addition to inflation adjustments, uses:","options":["Community rating","Issue-age rating","Attained-age rating","Experience rating"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attained-age premiums climb with the insured’s current age."},{"id":"H44-04","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"Under which rating method do all policyholders pay the same premium regardless of age?","options":["Attained-age","Issue-age","Community","Scheduled"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Community rating charges everyone the same premium, rising only for inflation."},{"id":"H44-05","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"Which benefit is included in EVERY standardized Medigap plan?","options":["The Part B deductible","Part A hospital coinsurance plus 365 additional hospital days","Foreign travel emergency care","The skilled nursing facility coinsurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Part A coinsurance and 365 extra days are core benefits in every plan."},{"id":"H44-06","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"Dennis became eligible for Medicare in 2026. Which Medigap plan may he NOT buy?","options":["Plan A","Plan G","Plan N","Plan F"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Plans C and F, which cover the Part B deductible, are closed to people eligible on or after January 1, 2020."},{"id":"H44-07","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"A high-deductible Plan G differs from standard Plan G in that it:","options":["Covers more benefits","Pays nothing until an annual deductible is met, in exchange for a lower premium","Includes prescription drugs","Is available only to people under 65"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The high-deductible version trades a lower premium for an annual deductible."},{"id":"H44-08","lesson":"44","domain":8,"prompt":"Which statement about standardized Medigap plans is correct?","options":["Each insurer designs its own benefits for each letter","A Plan G from any insurer in Maryland provides the same benefits as a Plan G from any other","Standardization applies only to Plan A","Plan letters change every year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standardization means identical benefits for the same letter; only price and service differ."}]},{"id":"45","number":45,"title":"Maryland Medigap protections","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H45-01","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer sells a second Medicare supplement policy to a client who already holds one and does not replace it. Under Maryland rules this is:","options":["Permitted as extra protection","A prohibited sale of excessive or duplicate coverage","Permitted if the client signs a waiver","Required for clients over 80"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland prohibits selling duplicate or excessive Medicare supplement coverage."},{"id":"H45-02","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"Gloria’s Medicare supplement policy is delivered on April 10. Under Maryland law she may return it for a full refund until:","options":["April 20","April 30","May 10","October 10"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maryland requires a 30-day right to return, so April 10 plus 30 days is May 10."},{"id":"H45-03","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer replaces a client’s existing Maryland Medicare supplement with a new one from a different insurer. The new insurer is required to:","options":["Impose a new six-month pre-existing condition period","Credit the time served under the old policy and waive waiting periods for benefits it covered","Charge a replacement fee","Refuse coverage for any condition treated in the prior year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires credit for time served under the replaced policy."},{"id":"H45-04","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"Under Maryland’s minimum standards, when Medicare increases its Part A deductible in January, a Medicare supplement policy that covers the deductible must:","options":["Keep paying the old amount","Adjust its benefit automatically to the new amount","Cancel the benefit","Require a new application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Benefits tied to Medicare deductibles and coinsurance must change automatically when Medicare changes."},{"id":"H45-05","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"A Maryland Medicare supplement policy may exclude a pre-existing condition for no longer than:","options":["30 days","6 months","12 months","24 months"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Six months is the maximum exclusion, with a six-month look-back."},{"id":"H45-06","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"A 58-year-old Maryland resident qualifies for Medicare because of disability. Within six months of enrolling in Part B, Medigap insurers must offer her:","options":["Only Plan F","Plans A and D, with the Plan A premium capped at the average paid by those 65 and older","Any plan at any price","No plan until she turns 65"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires Plans A and D for disabled beneficiaries under 65 with the Plan A premium cap."},{"id":"H45-07","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"Under Maryland’s birthday rule, a Plan G policyholder may, for 30 days after her birthday:","options":["Buy a Plan F from any insurer","Switch to a policy with equal or lesser benefits from any insurer without underwriting","Cancel Medicare Part B","Enroll in Medicaid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The birthday window permits a switch to equal or lesser benefits without health questions."},{"id":"H45-08","lesson":"45","domain":0,"prompt":"A Medicare SELECT policy differs from a standard Medicare supplement because it:","options":["Covers prescription drugs","Requires use of network hospitals for full benefits, except in emergencies, in exchange for a lower premium","Is available only to people under 65","Has no standardized benefits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SELECT policies use networks to lower premiums while keeping standardized benefits."}]},{"id":"46","number":46,"title":"Medicare with employer plans and Medicaid","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H46-01","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"Dennis, 66, works full time for an employer with 60 employees and is enrolled in Medicare. For his medical claims:","options":["Medicare pays first and the group plan second","The group plan pays first and Medicare second","Only Medicare pays","Only the group plan pays"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For active employees 65 and older at employers with 20 or more employees, the group plan is primary."},{"id":"H46-02","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"A 67-year-old active employee works for a company with 12 employees and has Medicare. Which pays first?","options":["The group plan","Medicare","Medicaid","The employee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With fewer than 20 employees, Medicare is the primary payer."},{"id":"H46-03","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"A 55-year-old on Medicare because of disability is covered under her husband’s group plan at a 300-employee company. Which pays first?","options":["Medicare","The group plan, because the employer has 100 or more employees","Medicaid","Neither; she must choose one"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For disability-based Medicare, the group plan is primary if the employer has 100 or more employees."},{"id":"H46-04","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"An employee with end-stage renal disease has Medicare and a group plan from a 15-employee company. During the first 30 months of Medicare eligibility:","options":["Medicare pays first because the employer is small","The group plan pays first regardless of employer size","Medicaid pays first","The group plan pays nothing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For ESRD, the group plan is primary for 30 months regardless of size, then Medicare becomes primary."},{"id":"H46-05","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"Which statement about Medicaid is correct?","options":["It is funded entirely by the federal government","It is a joint federal and state program for people with limited income and resources","It is available only to people over 65","It pays before all other coverage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Medicaid is jointly funded and administered by the states under federal rules."},{"id":"H46-06","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"A person eligible for both Medicare and Medicaid is known as a:","options":["Qualified employer","Dual eligible","Late enrollee","Nonparticipating beneficiary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dual eligibles have both Medicare and Medicaid, and Medicaid may pay their Medicare costs."},{"id":"H46-07","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"Gloria needs a year of custodial nursing home care. Which program will pay for it?","options":["Medicare Part A, for the full year","Her Medicare supplement policy","Medicaid, if she meets Maryland’s financial and medical eligibility requirements","Part D"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maryland Medicaid can pay for this care when the applicant meets the financial and medical eligibility rules."},{"id":"H46-08","lesson":"46","domain":8,"prompt":"Under Medicare Part A, skilled nursing facility care is covered for up to how many days per benefit period after a qualifying hospital stay?","options":["20 days","60 days","100 days","365 days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Medicare covers skilled nursing care up to 100 days per benefit period, with coinsurance after day 20."}]},{"id":"47","number":47,"title":"Long-term care insurance","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H47-01","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"Under a tax-qualified long-term care policy, benefits are triggered when the insured cannot perform at least how many of the six activities of daily living?","options":["One","Two","Three","All six"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inability to perform two of six ADLs for an expected 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment, triggers benefits."},{"id":"H47-02","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six activities of daily living used in long-term care policies?","options":["Bathing","Dressing","Driving","Transferring"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Driving is not an ADL; the six are bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, transferring and continence."},{"id":"H47-03","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"Help with bathing, dressing and eating provided by aides rather than nurses is classified as:","options":["Skilled care","Intermediate care","Custodial care","Respite care"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Assistance with daily living by nonmedical aides is custodial care."},{"id":"H47-04","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"A policy benefit that pays for temporary care so a family caregiver can take a break is:","options":["Adult day care","Respite care","Home health care","Hospice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Respite care gives the primary caregiver temporary relief."},{"id":"H47-05","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"Gloria buys a $250 daily benefit with a three-year benefit period expressed as a pool of money. If she uses $150 a day, her benefits:","options":["Stop after exactly three years","Last longer than three years because the pool of $273,750 is drawn down more slowly","Are reduced to $150 permanently","Are forfeited for underuse"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using less than the maximum stretches the pool beyond three years."},{"id":"H47-06","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"Which optional long-term care benefit protects the daily benefit against rising care costs over time?","options":["Nonforfeiture benefit","Inflation protection","Return of premium","Waiver of premium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inflation protection increases the benefit, typically 5 percent compounded annually."},{"id":"H47-07","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"Which statement about tax-qualified long-term care policies is correct?","options":["Premiums are never deductible","Benefits are taxable as income","Premiums may be deductible as medical expenses within age-based limits and benefits are tax-free up to an indexed daily cap","They may use any benefit trigger the insurer chooses"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tax-qualified status provides these federal tax advantages."},{"id":"H47-08","lesson":"47","domain":8,"prompt":"A Maryland Partnership long-term care policy paid $200,000 in benefits before Gloria applied for Medicaid. The Partnership feature means:","options":["Medicaid is denied because she had insurance","Medicaid disregards $200,000 of her assets when determining eligibility","She must repay the $200,000 to the insurer","Her premiums are refunded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The asset disregard equals the benefits the policy paid."}]},{"id":"48","number":48,"title":"Maryland long-term care rules and the tax credit","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H48-01","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"Before or at the time of application for long-term care insurance, a Maryland producer must give the applicant:","options":["Only a premium quote","The long-term care consumer guide, an outline of coverage and the required disclosure form","A copy of the insurer’s financial statements","A Medicare supplement buyer’s guide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires the shopper’s guide, outline of coverage and the Things You Should Know disclosure."},{"id":"H48-02","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"The long-term care insurance personal worksheet is used to:","options":["Determine the applicant’s health classification","Assess suitability by reviewing income, assets and premium affordability","Calculate the insurer’s reserves","Select the applicant’s physician"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The worksheet supports the insurer’s suitability review of the purchase."},{"id":"H48-03","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"Which renewability clause is permitted in a Maryland individual long-term care policy?","options":["Optionally renewable","Conditionally renewable","Guaranteed renewable","Cancelable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Long-term care policies must be guaranteed renewable or noncancelable."},{"id":"H48-04","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"Three years after issue, an insurer discovers a misstatement on a long-term care application. It may rescind only if:","options":["The misstatement was material to any underwriting decision","The applicant knowingly and intentionally misrepresented relevant facts about her health","The policy has paid any claim","The applicant is over 80"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After two years, rescission requires a knowing and intentional misrepresentation of relevant health facts."},{"id":"H48-05","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"A Maryland long-term care policyholder with dementia forgets to pay her premium and the policy lapses. She may:","options":["Never reinstate","Reinstate within five months by showing the lapse resulted from cognitive or functional impairment","Reinstate only with a new medical exam","Reinstate only if under age 70"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland allows reinstatement within five months when lapse resulted from cognitive or functional impairment."},{"id":"H48-06","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"Under Maryland rules, an insurer may issue a long-term care policy without inflation protection only if:","options":["The applicant is under 50","The applicant rejects the offered inflation protection in writing","The premium is below $100 a month","The policy is a Partnership policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insurers must offer at least 5 percent compound inflation protection and may omit it only on written rejection."},{"id":"H48-07","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"A Maryland long-term care policy may NOT:","options":["Use a 90-day elimination period","Require a prior hospital stay before paying for nursing home care","Offer a shortened benefit period nonforfeiture option","Cover adult day care"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland forbids conditioning benefits on prior hospitalization."},{"id":"H48-08","lesson":"48","domain":0,"prompt":"Gloria pays $3,000 in premiums for a qualified long-term care policy in 2026. Her Maryland long-term care insurance credit for the year is:","options":["$3,000","$1,500","$500","$0, because the credit is one-time only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The credit is 100 percent of eligible premiums up to $500 per insured per year."}]},{"id":"49","number":49,"title":"Who needs a Maryland license","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H49-01","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"Which activity requires a Maryland producer license?","options":["Scheduling appointments for a licensed producer without discussing coverage","Urging a customer to apply for a particular health plan","Processing payroll deductions for an employer’s own benefit plan","Delivering mail at an insurance agency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Urging someone to apply is soliciting, which requires a license."},{"id":"H49-02","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"Which statement about Maryland producer licensing is correct as of 2026?","options":["A 40-hour prelicensing course is required before the exam","Prelicensing education was repealed effective October 1, 2024, but the examination remains","The exam is waived for applicants over 30","Applicants may retake a failed exam the same day"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland repealed mandatory prelicensing education in 2024 while keeping the exam and continuing education."},{"id":"H49-03","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"After failing the Maryland producer examination, an applicant must wait at least how long before retaking it?","options":["One day","Four days","30 days","90 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland law requires at least four days between examination attempts."},{"id":"H49-04","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"A person is paid a fee by consumers to analyze their health policies and recommend changes, and does not sell insurance. In Maryland she needs a(n):","options":["Producer license","Adviser license","Temporary license","No license"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compensated policy analysis and advice require an adviser license."},{"id":"H49-05","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"A Virginia producer with a resident license in good standing wants to sell health insurance in Maryland. She must:","options":["Take the Maryland exam and complete a Maryland course","Obtain a Maryland nonresident license through reciprocity with the required application and fee","Do nothing; her Virginia license is valid nationwide","Form a Maryland business entity first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nonresidents obtain Maryland authority through reciprocity based on their home-state license."},{"id":"H49-06","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"A business entity licensed as a producer in Maryland must:","options":["Designate a licensed producer as its principal contact with the Administration","License only its owners, not its salespeople","Use its entity license to cover all employees’ personal sales","Hold an adviser license as well"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Entities must designate a licensed producer responsible for compliance contact."},{"id":"H49-07","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"An agency receptionist who receives no commission begins recommending specific disability policies to callers. Under Maryland law she:","options":["Remains exempt because she is clerical staff","Has lost the exception because she is now soliciting and needs a producer license","Is exempt as long as a licensed producer signs the applications","Needs only an adviser license"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recommending policies is soliciting, which requires a producer license regardless of the job title."},{"id":"H49-08","lesson":"49","domain":0,"prompt":"A Maryland temporary producer license issued to the spouse of a deceased producer expires:","options":["After 90 days","After 15 months","After two years, like a regular license","Never, if the business continues"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Temporary licenses expire 15 months after their effective date."}]},{"id":"50","number":50,"title":"Keeping the license: renewal, education and reporting","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H50-01","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"A regular Maryland resident producer must complete how many hours of continuing education each renewal period, and how many of those in ethics?","options":["16 hours, 2 in ethics","24 hours, 3 in ethics","30 hours, 4 in ethics","40 hours, none in ethics"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires 24 hours per two-year period including at least three hours of ethics."},{"id":"H50-02","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"Under the current statute, a Maryland producer must complete continuing education no later than:","options":["The license expiration date","15 days before expiration","30 days after expiration","90 days before expiration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Section 10–116 requires completion at least 15 days before the license expires."},{"id":"H50-03","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"Theo’s license expired on June 30. He applies for reinstatement on August 10 with all fees and education. His reinstatement is:","options":["Retroactive to June 30","Prospective only, because more than 60 days have passed","Denied, because reinstatement is never allowed","Automatic without fees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After 60 days but within one year, reinstatement is prospective, leaving a gap in licensure."},{"id":"H50-04","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer may sell insurance while her license is expired if she:","options":["Intends to reinstate within 60 days","Has completed her continuing education","Works under a licensed agency","Never; an expired license does not permit selling until reinstatement is effective"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Maryland prohibits insurance business until reinstatement takes effect."},{"id":"H50-05","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"A Maryland producer moves to a new home. She must report the address change to the Administration within:","options":["10 days","30 days","60 days","One year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Changes of name, trade name, email and address must be reported within 30 days."},{"id":"H50-06","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer is charged with a felony and makes her initial court appearance on May 1. Under §10–126 she must report the prosecution to the Commissioner:","options":["Only if convicted","Within 30 days of the initial appearance, with the charging documents","Within one year","At her next license renewal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires a report within 30 days of the initial appearance, with the charging document and related orders."},{"id":"H50-07","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"Which criminal matter is excluded from the 30-day prosecution reporting requirement?","options":["A felony theft charge","A misdemeanor fraud charge","A vehicle-related misdemeanor","An embezzlement charge"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vehicle-related misdemeanors are excluded from the reporting duty."},{"id":"H50-08","lesson":"50","domain":0,"prompt":"A Virginia regulator suspends a producer’s Virginia license. The producer also holds a Maryland license. She must:","options":["Do nothing unless Maryland asks","Report the action to the Maryland Commissioner within 30 days, with the order","Surrender her Maryland license immediately","Report only if the suspension exceeds one year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Administrative actions by other jurisdictions must be reported within 30 days with the relevant order."}]},{"id":"51","number":51,"title":"Appointments and solicitation","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H51-01","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"Which document is the insurer’s registration of a producer as its agent under Maryland law?","options":["The producer license","The producer agreement","The appointment","The certificate of authority"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The appointment registers the producer as the insurer’s agent."},{"id":"H51-02","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"An insurer appoints AMG, a business entity. Which statement is correct about Theo, an AMG producer?","options":["He is automatically appointed because the agency is","He needs his own individual appointment recorded in the insurer’s register to act as its agent","He may act as the insurer’s agent only if he owns the agency","The agency appointment covers him for all lines"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Individuals acting for the insurer must be appointed in their own right."},{"id":"H51-03","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"A licensed producer solicits a health application for an admitted insurer that has not appointed him. The insurer accepts the application. The insurer must:","options":["Return the application because the producer was not appointed","Within 30 days either reject the application or appoint the producer and enter him in its register","Appoint the producer within one year","Report the producer to the Commissioner"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insurer has 30 days to appoint and register or to reject the application."},{"id":"H51-04","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"After appointing a producer, the insurer must provide written documentation of the appointment within:","options":["10 days","15 days","30 days","90 days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Register entry and written documentation are each due within 30 days."},{"id":"H51-05","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"How long must an insurer keep appointment records after an appointment terminates?","options":["One year","Three years","At least five years","Ten years"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Appointment records are kept while active and at least five years after termination."},{"id":"H51-06","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"An insurer terminates a producer’s appointment for a reason that could be grounds for discipline and notifies the Commissioner. It must send the producer a copy of that notice within:","options":["5 days","15 days","30 days","60 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insurer sends the producer a copy within 15 days after the required notice to the Commissioner."},{"id":"H51-07","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"When an insurer ends a producer’s appointment, the producer:","options":["May continue soliciting for that insurer until renewals are paid","Must stop soliciting for that insurer immediately","Loses her Maryland license automatically","Must return her license to the Commissioner"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cessation of solicitation is required once the appointment ends."},{"id":"H51-08","lesson":"51","domain":0,"prompt":"A licensed producer currently has no appointments. Which statement is correct?","options":["Her license is void","She may keep her license active and solicit applications that an insurer must then appoint or reject","She may bind coverage for any admitted insurer","She must obtain a temporary license"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A license without appointments stays in force; she may solicit, and insurers must appoint or reject within 30 days."}]},{"id":"52","number":52,"title":"Discipline, hearings and fraud","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H52-01","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"Which of the following is a ground for suspending or revoking a Maryland producer license under §10–126?","options":["Changing employers","Misappropriating premium funds belonging to an insurer","Selling only health insurance","Declining to write business for a particular insurer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Misappropriating, converting or unlawfully withholding money is a listed ground."},{"id":"H52-02","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"Instead of or in addition to suspending a license, the Commissioner may impose a penalty per violation of:","options":["$10 to $500","$100 to $5,000","$1,000 to $50,000","Up to $1 million"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Section 10–126 permits a penalty of $100 to $5,000 for each violation."},{"id":"H52-03","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"The Commissioner orders a producer to stop advertising a limited policy as comprehensive coverage, with further penalties if the conduct continues. This order is a:","options":["Subpoena","Cease-and-desist order","Restitution order","Conditional receipt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cease-and-desist order requires a person to stop a specific unlawful practice."},{"id":"H52-04","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer receives notice of a proposed license revocation and demands a hearing in writing. Under §2–210 the Commissioner must grant and hold the hearing within:","options":["10 days","30 days, unless postponed by agreement","90 days","One year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Commissioner must grant and hold the hearing within 30 consecutive days unless the parties agree to postpone, or refuse it."},{"id":"H52-05","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"After the Commissioner revokes a license, a petition for judicial review is generally due within:","options":["10 days","30 days of service of the order","6 months","There is no judicial review"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Section 2–215 generally allows 30 days after the order is served or a hearing is refused."},{"id":"H52-06","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer files a petition for judicial review of a revocation. While the petition is pending, the producer:","options":["May resume selling because the appeal stays the order","Remains revoked unless the court grants a stay","Is automatically reinstated","May sell only health insurance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Commissioner’s order stands unless the court issues a stay."},{"id":"H52-07","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer helps a client misstate his health history to obtain disability coverage. This conduct is best described as:","options":["Field underwriting","Insurance fraud","Twisting","A permitted exception"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Knowingly presenting false information to obtain coverage is insurance fraud."},{"id":"H52-08","lesson":"52","domain":0,"prompt":"An independent producer has good-faith cause to believe a client submitted a fraudulent claim. Under §27–802 she must:","options":["Keep quiet to protect the client relationship","Report the suspected fraud in writing to the Insurance Fraud Division","Report only if the claim exceeds $10,000","Confront the client and demand repayment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent producers with good-faith cause must report in writing to the Fraud Division, with protection for good-faith reports."}]},{"id":"53","number":53,"title":"MIA powers, records, fees and fiduciary duties","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H53-01","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"Who heads the Maryland Insurance Administration and enforces the Insurance Article?","options":["The Attorney General","The Insurance Commissioner, appointed by the Governor","The Comptroller","The NAIC president"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Commissioner leads the Administration and enforces the Insurance Article."},{"id":"H53-02","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer places a client’s health coverage with a company that holds no Maryland certificate of authority. Which consequence may follow?","options":["None, if the premium was competitive","Personal liability for the client’s unpaid claims plus license discipline","Only a warning letter","A requirement to obtain a surplus lines license"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Producers who act for unauthorized insurers may be held liable for claims and face discipline and penalties."},{"id":"H53-03","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"The Commissioner requests a producer’s electronic appointment records. They must be retrievable within:","options":["24 hours","10 working days","30 days","90 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Electronic records must be retrievable within ten working days of the request."},{"id":"H53-04","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"Which task may an unlicensed agency employee perform?","options":["Explaining a policy’s exclusions to an applicant","Recommending a deductible level","Scheduling an appointment and collecting basic contact information","Collecting a commission on a sale she arranged"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Clerical tasks without discussing coverage are permitted."},{"id":"H53-05","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer wants to pay a friend who is not licensed 20 percent of each commission for bringing in customers. Under Maryland law this is:","options":["Permitted as a referral fee","Prohibited, because sales compensation may be shared only with licensed producers","Permitted if the friend signs a contract","Permitted up to $500 per year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Section 10–130 limits commission sharing to licensed producers."},{"id":"H53-06","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"An insurer pays no commission on a particular policy with a $2,000 premium. The maximum placement service fee a producer may charge the client is:","options":["$100","$200","$300","Any amount the client agrees to"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The placement fee may not exceed 15 percent of the premium, which is $300."},{"id":"H53-07","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer deposits client premiums into the agency’s operating account and pays rent from it before remitting to the insurer. This is:","options":["Permitted if the insurer is paid within 30 days","Prohibited commingling and misappropriation of fiduciary funds","Permitted for premiums under $1,000","Permitted with the client’s consent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Premium funds must be kept in a separate premium account and may not be used for the producer’s own expenses."},{"id":"H53-08","lesson":"53","domain":0,"prompt":"Which withdrawal from a producer’s premium account is permitted under Maryland regulations?","options":["Payment of the agency’s payroll","Transfer of commissions actually earned on the premiums deposited","A loan to the agency owner","Any withdrawal that leaves at least $1,000 in the account"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Earned commissions may be transferred, along with payments to carriers and return premiums."}]},{"id":"54","number":54,"title":"Unfair trade practices","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H54-01","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer tells a prospect that a hospital indemnity policy is comprehensive health insurance. This conduct is:","options":["A permitted sales technique","Misrepresentation, an unfair trade practice","Rebating","Defamation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Giving a false or misleading account of a policy is misrepresentation."},{"id":"H54-02","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"To win a group account, a producer tells the employer, without basis, that the competing insurer is about to become insolvent. This is:","options":["Fair competition","Defamation","Rebating","Coercion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A false or maliciously critical statement about an insurer’s financial condition is defamation."},{"id":"H54-03","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer alters the date on a signed application so coverage appears to have started earlier. This conduct falls under:","options":["Field underwriting","False statements or entries, and potentially fraud","Twisting","A permitted correction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Altering records to deceive is a false entry and may be insurance fraud."},{"id":"H54-04","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer hands a $20 promotional notebook to every attendee at a seminar, whether or not they buy. Under Maryland’s rebating rules this is:","options":["A prohibited rebate","Permitted, because the item costs no more than $50 and is not conditioned on a purchase","Permitted only if the attendees sign applications","Prohibited because gifts of any value are banned"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both parts of the exception are met: value at or below $50 and no purchase condition."},{"id":"H54-05","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer offers to pay a client’s first month’s premium if she signs today. This is:","options":["A permitted promotional item","Rebating, prohibited under §27–209","Twisting","A placement fee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Giving value not specified in the policy to induce a purchase is rebating."},{"id":"H54-06","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer gives a client an incomplete comparison that hides a new waiting period in order to persuade her to replace her disability policy. This is:","options":["Twisting","Defamation","Rebating","A fair comparison"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inducing replacement through a misleading or incomplete comparison is twisting."},{"id":"H54-07","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer falsely tells a client that a competitor’s plan has no mental health coverage so the client will keep her current policy. Under §27–213 this is:","options":["Lawful, because no replacement occurred","Twisting, because the statute covers inducing retention as well as replacement","Permitted puffery","Rebating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland’s twisting statute includes misrepresentations made to induce a policyholder to retain a policy."},{"id":"H54-08","lesson":"54","domain":0,"prompt":"A producer pays an unlicensed friend $100 for every referred customer who buys a policy. This arrangement is:","options":["A lawful referral fee","Unlawful, because the payment is conditioned on a sale and amounts to sharing commission with an unlicensed person","Lawful if under $500 per year","Lawful if the friend never meets the customers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compensation that depends on the referred customer buying is sales compensation, which may go only to licensed producers."}]},{"id":"55","number":55,"title":"Adverse underwriting notices and privacy","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H55-01","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"Which of the following is an adverse underwriting decision under Maryland’s insurance information law?","options":["Issuing a policy exactly as applied for","Issuing a disability policy at a higher premium with an exclusion rider","Sending a renewal notice","Paying a claim"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rating, restricting, declining or terminating coverage are adverse underwriting decisions."},{"id":"H55-02","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"After an adverse underwriting decision, the insurer must give the applicant written notice that:","options":["Lists every competitor who might accept her","States the specific reasons or tells her she may request them, and explains her access and correction rights","Includes a refund of the application fee","Names the underwriter personally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The notice must give reasons or how to request them and inform the applicant of access and correction rights."},{"id":"H55-03","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"Rosa finds an incorrect MIB code in her underwriting file. Under Maryland’s information practices law she may:","options":["Do nothing; records cannot be changed","Request correction, and if the insurer refuses, file a statement of dispute that stays with the record","Sue the MIB only","Demand the insurer destroy the entire file"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Applicants may request correction, amendment or deletion and may dispute a refusal in writing."},{"id":"H55-04","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"Someone calls Rosa’s neighbor pretending to be a hospital employee to learn about Rosa’s health for underwriting purposes. This is a prohibited:","options":["Inspection report","Pretext interview","Special enrollment","Field underwriting technique"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Obtaining information by misrepresenting one’s identity or purpose is a pretext interview, which is prohibited."},{"id":"H55-05","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"Before sharing a customer’s nonpublic financial information with an unaffiliated company for marketing, a Maryland licensee must:","options":["Obtain the Commissioner’s approval","Provide an opt-out notice and a reasonable opportunity to opt out, and honor the customer’s choice","Pay the customer a fee","Wait one year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The privacy rules require notice and an opt-out opportunity before such disclosures."},{"id":"H55-06","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"Which disclosure of a client’s nonpublic health information may a producer make WITHOUT written authorization?","options":["Telling the client’s employer about her diagnosis","Sending the client’s medical records to the insurer’s underwriter","Mentioning her condition to a prospective customer","Selling a list of clients with chronic conditions to a pharmacy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Underwriting is an insurance function that permits disclosure without separate authorization."},{"id":"H55-07","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"A licensee shares customer information only within the permitted exceptions and its privacy practices have not changed since the last notice. Under Maryland’s rules it:","options":["Must still send a new annual notice","May rely on the exception from the annual notice requirement","Must obtain written consent from each customer","Must file a report with the Commissioner"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The annual notice exception applies when disclosures stay within permitted categories and practices are unchanged."},{"id":"H55-08","lesson":"55","domain":0,"prompt":"Which statement about the notice of information practices is correct?","options":["It is given only after a claim is denied","It tells applicants what information may be collected, from whom, how it is used and their access rights","It is required only for group policies","It replaces the need for an adverse underwriting notice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The notice describes information practices and the applicant’s rights."}]},{"id":"56","number":56,"title":"Maryland plan requirements and mandated benefits","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H56-01","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"Under Insurance Article §15–418, a Maryland health policy covering dependents must cover an unmarried child dependent until age:","options":["19","21","25","30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maryland requires coverage of unmarried child dependents under age 25; federal law extends to 26 for plans it governs."},{"id":"H56-02","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"Marcus’s family plan charges an additional premium per child. His daughter is born March 3. To keep her covered beyond the first 31 days, he must notify the insurer and pay the premium by:","options":["March 3","March 10","April 3","The next open enrollment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When a premium is charged, notice and payment are due within 31 days after birth."},{"id":"H56-03","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"Under §15–401, an adopted child is covered from the date of adoption, which is defined as:","options":["The date the adoption petition is filed","The earlier of the judicial decree of adoption or the assumption of custody pending adoption","The child’s next birthday","The first day of the next plan year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland defines the date of adoption as the earlier of the decree or assumption of custody pending adoption."},{"id":"H56-04","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"A court orders an employee to provide health coverage for his son, who lives with the mother and receives Medicaid. The carrier:","options":["May refuse because the child receives Medicaid","May refuse because the child does not live with the employee","Must enroll the child regardless of enrollment periods and may not refuse for those reasons","May enroll the child only at open enrollment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Section 15–405 requires enrollment without regard to enrollment periods and bars these refusal grounds."},{"id":"H56-05","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"A 27-year-old with an intellectual disability has depended on her parents since childhood and cannot support herself. Under Maryland law her parents’ health plan:","options":["Must terminate her coverage at the limiting age","Must continue her coverage past the limiting age while the incapacity and dependency continue, with proof","Must convert her to Medicare","May charge her the premium of an adult employee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coverage continues for dependents incapable of self-support because of intellectual disability or physical handicap, with proof of incapacity."},{"id":"H56-06","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a mandated offer from a mandated benefit?","options":["Both must be included in every policy","A mandated offer must be made available for the policyholder to accept or decline; a mandated benefit must be included","A mandated benefit applies only to HMOs","A mandated offer applies only to individual policies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Offers give the buyer the choice; benefits are built into the policy."},{"id":"H56-07","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"Under §15–838, a Maryland policy must cover hearing aids for a minor child up to:","options":["$500 per hearing aid every 12 months","$1,400 per hearing aid for each impaired ear every 36 months","$5,000 per year with no frequency limit","Unlimited cost once per lifetime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cap is $1,400 per hearing aid per impaired ear once every 36 months, with the insured able to pay the difference for a costlier device."},{"id":"H56-08","lesson":"56","domain":0,"prompt":"Under §15–810, an insurer may limit in vitro fertilization coverage to:","options":["One attempt per lifetime","Three attempts per live birth with a $100,000 lifetime maximum","Five attempts per year","No limits are permitted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland permits a limit of three attempts per live birth and a $100,000 lifetime benefit maximum."}]},{"id":"57","number":57,"title":"Taxing personal coverage and health accounts","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H57-01","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"Rosa pays the premiums on her individual disability income policy with after-tax dollars. When she becomes disabled, her monthly benefits are:","options":["Fully taxable as ordinary income","Taxable only above $2,000 a month","Tax-free","Taxable at capital gains rates"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Individually paid disability benefits are received free of federal income tax."},{"id":"H57-02","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"Which statement about individually paid disability income premiums is correct?","options":["They are deductible as a medical expense","They are not deductible, which is why the benefits are tax-free","They are deductible above 7.5 percent of adjusted gross income","They are deductible only for self-employed people"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The premium is nondeductible and the benefit is tax-free."},{"id":"H57-03","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"Marcus itemizes deductions. His individual medical plan premiums are deductible:","options":["In full, as an adjustment to income","Only to the extent his total medical expenses exceed 7.5 percent of adjusted gross income","Never","Only if the policy is a high-deductible plan"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Premiums are medical expenses subject to the 7.5 percent floor for itemizers."},{"id":"H57-04","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"An individual medical plan pays $30,000 to a hospital for Marcus’s surgery. For federal income tax purposes, the $30,000 is:","options":["Taxable income to Marcus","Taxable only if Marcus deducted the premium","Not taxable","Taxable at 20 percent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Payments for medical care are excluded from income."},{"id":"H57-05","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"Gloria pays $3,500 in premiums on a tax-qualified long-term care policy. For federal purposes, the deductible portion is:","options":["The full $3,500 as an adjustment to income","Limited to an age-based amount and treated as a medical expense subject to the 7.5 percent floor","Zero; long-term care premiums are never deductible","$500, matching the Maryland credit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Qualified long-term care premiums count as medical expenses up to an age-based limit, subject to the floor."},{"id":"H57-06","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"Which account is owned by the individual, portable between jobs and subject to a 20 percent penalty on nonmedical withdrawals before age 65?","options":["Health reimbursement arrangement","Flexible spending account","Health savings account","High-deductible health plan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The HSA is the individual’s portable account with the 20 percent penalty rule."},{"id":"H57-07","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"An employer funds a health reimbursement arrangement for its employees. The tax result is:","options":["Contributions are taxable wages to employees","Contributions are deductible to the employer and reimbursements are tax-free to employees","Employees may deduct the employer’s contributions","Reimbursements are taxed at 20 percent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Employer contributions are a deductible business expense and reimbursements for medical expenses are tax-free."},{"id":"H57-08","lesson":"57","domain":9,"prompt":"Marcus elects $3,000 of salary reduction into his health flexible spending account but incurs only $2,200 of eligible expenses by year end. Absent a carryover or grace period, the remaining $800 is:","options":["Refunded to him tax-free","Rolled into an HSA","Forfeited under the use-it-or-lose-it rule","Paid to him as taxable wages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unused FSA funds are forfeited unless the plan allows a limited carryover or grace period."}]},{"id":"58","number":58,"title":"Taxing employer, self-employed and business coverage","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H58-01","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Priya’s bakery pays 100 percent of the premium for its group long-term disability plan. When a baker collects LTD benefits, those benefits are:","options":["Tax-free","Fully taxable as income to the baker","Taxable only above $50,000","Taxable to the bakery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the employer paid and deducted the premium, the employee’s benefits are taxable income."},{"id":"H58-02","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"An employee pays her share of a group disability premium through a cafeteria plan with pre-tax dollars. Her disability benefits attributable to that share are:","options":["Tax-free, because she paid the premium","Taxable, because the premium was paid pre-tax","Taxable at 20 percent","Exempt up to $5,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-tax payment is treated like employer payment, so benefits are taxable."},{"id":"H58-03","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Employer-paid premiums for group medical and dental coverage are:","options":["Taxable wages to employees","Deductible to the employer and excluded from employees’ income, with tax-free benefits","Deductible only above 7.5 percent of payroll","Nondeductible to the employer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Group medical and dental premiums are deductible business expenses excluded from employee income, and benefits are tax-free."},{"id":"H58-04","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Which coverage may NOT be offered through an employer’s cafeteria plan or flexible spending account?","options":["Group medical","Group dental","Qualified long-term care insurance","Group vision"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Long-term care insurance is excluded from cafeteria plans and FSAs by federal law."},{"id":"H58-05","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Priya’s bakery pays for group AD&D coverage. Which statement is correct?","options":["Premiums above $50,000 of coverage are taxable to employees","Premiums are deductible to the employer, not taxable to employees, and benefits are tax-free","Death benefits are taxable to the beneficiary","Dismemberment benefits are taxable wages"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AD&D is treated as accident and health coverage with favorable treatment on premiums and benefits."},{"id":"H58-06","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Rosa, a sole proprietor with net self-employment earnings of $70,000, pays $9,000 in medical premiums for her family. On her federal return she may:","options":["Deduct nothing","Deduct $9,000 as an adjustment to income without itemizing","Deduct only the amount above 7.5 percent of her AGI","Deduct 50 percent of the premiums"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-employed individuals may deduct 100 percent of qualifying premiums above the line, up to net self-employment earnings."},{"id":"H58-07","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Rosa’s spouse becomes eligible for subsidized employer coverage in July. For the months she is eligible for that plan, Rosa’s self-employed health insurance deduction is:","options":["Unchanged","Not available for those months","Doubled","Available only for dental premiums"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The deduction is not allowed for any month the self-employed person is eligible for an employer-subsidized plan, her own or a spouse’s."},{"id":"H58-08","lesson":"58","domain":9,"prompt":"Priya’s bakery owns a business overhead expense policy and a key person disability policy. Which statement is correct?","options":["Both premiums are deductible and both benefits are tax-free","BOE premiums are deductible and BOE benefits are taxable; key person premiums are not deductible and key person benefits are tax-free","Both premiums are nondeductible and both benefits are taxable","Key person premiums are deductible and benefits are taxable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BOE follows the deducted-premium-taxable-benefit pattern; key person coverage follows the nondeductible-premium-tax-free-benefit pattern."}]},{"id":"59","number":59,"title":"Mixed cases: plans, claims and disability","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H59-01","lesson":"59","domain":5,"prompt":"A plan has a $1,500 deductible, 80 percent in-network coinsurance and a $5,000 out-of-pocket maximum. On $28,000 of in-network allowed charges the insured pays:","options":["$1,500","$5,000","$6,800","$7,100"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The $1,500 deductible plus $5,300 of coinsurance totals $6,800, but the $5,000 maximum caps the insured’s share."},{"id":"H59-02","lesson":"59","domain":3,"prompt":"A quarterly premium due April 1 goes unpaid. The insured is injured April 20. Under the uniform grace period provision the claim is:","options":["Denied because the premium was unpaid","Covered, because the 31-day grace period for quarterly premiums had not expired","Covered only if the premium is paid by April 21","Denied until reinstatement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Quarterly and other non-monthly premiums carry a 31-day grace period, so April 20 falls within it."},{"id":"H59-03","lesson":"59","domain":3,"prompt":"An insured gives notice of claim on day 10 and the insurer sends no claim forms by day 30. The insured may:","options":["Wait indefinitely for forms","Submit written proof of loss in any form describing the occurrence and extent of the loss","Sue immediately","Forfeit the claim"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When forms are not supplied within 15 days of notice, any written proof is acceptable."},{"id":"H59-04","lesson":"59","domain":4,"prompt":"A disability policy with a 90-day elimination period and benefits paid monthly in arrears covers an insured totally disabled on June 1. Her first benefit check arrives about:","options":["June 1","August 30","Late September","Late December"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The first month of benefits accrues through late September and is paid then."},{"id":"H59-05","lesson":"59","domain":4,"prompt":"A disabled insured with a $3,600 total benefit and a $1,200 social insurance supplement is approved for $1,500 a month from Social Security. Her private benefits become:","options":["$4,800","$3,600","$2,100","$3,300"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The supplement is offset by the $1,500 Social Security benefit, leaving the $3,600 base benefit, which individual policies do not reduce."},{"id":"H59-06","lesson":"59","domain":4,"prompt":"Rosa returns to work half time with a 40 percent income loss under a policy paying $3,600 for total disability with a residual benefit. Her monthly residual benefit is:","options":["$1,800","$1,440","$2,160","$3,600"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forty percent of $3,600 equals $1,440."},{"id":"H59-07","lesson":"59","domain":6,"prompt":"A 12-employee Maryland employer lays off a worker covered for two years. Which continuation right applies?","options":["COBRA for 18 months","Maryland continuation for up to 18 months, elected within 45 days","No continuation right","COBRA for 36 months"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland continuation applies to employers of any size after loss of employment not for cause."},{"id":"H59-08","lesson":"59","domain":6,"prompt":"A laid-off worker wants to buy an individual policy from her group insurer without a medical exam. She must apply within:","options":["10 days","31 days","45 days","90 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The conversion privilege generally requires application within 31 days of termination."},{"id":"H59-09","lesson":"59","domain":6,"prompt":"Elena’s plan, primary under the birthday rule, pays $4,200 of a $6,000 allowed claim. Marcus’s secondary plan would have paid $4,800 alone. The secondary plan pays:","options":["$4,800","$1,800","$600","$0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The $1,800 balance brings total payment to 100 percent of the allowed charge."},{"id":"H59-10","lesson":"59","domain":5,"prompt":"A client wants HMO-level premiums with the option to see out-of-network providers at a higher cost when she chooses. The design that fits is a(n):","options":["Exclusive provider organization","Point-of-service plan","Indemnity plan","Hospital indemnity policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A POS plan combines an HMO structure with out-of-network coverage at higher cost sharing."}]},{"id":"60","number":60,"title":"Mixed cases: Maryland rules in practice","version":1,"questions":[{"id":"H60-01","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"Theo’s license expires at the end of his birth month in August. Under Maryland’s continuing education rule, his 24 hours must be completed by:","options":["August 31","August 16, 15 days before expiration","September 15","July 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Education must be completed at least 15 days before the license expires."},{"id":"H60-02","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"In February an insurer accepts a health application Theo solicited before it appointed him. By law the insurer must, within 30 days:","options":["Cancel the policy","Either appoint Theo and enter him in its register or reject the application","Report Theo to the Commissioner","Pay Theo a placement fee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The insurer has 30 days to appoint or reject after accepting an application from an unappointed producer."},{"id":"H60-03","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"A client pays a $1,200 annual premium in cash to Theo. The correct handling is to:","options":["Deposit it in the agency’s operating account until the carrier bills","Deposit it in the separate premium account and remit it to the carrier","Keep it in the office safe as a courtesy","Apply it to Theo’s commission first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Premiums are fiduciary funds that belong in the premium account and go to the carrier."},{"id":"H60-04","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"An insurer pays no commission on a $1,200 policy. The maximum placement fee Theo may charge is:","options":["$60","$120","$180","$240"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fifteen percent of $1,200 is $180, the statutory ceiling."},{"id":"H60-05","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"During a comparison, a producer accurately describes both plans but omits his own plan’s referral requirement in order to win the sale. This is:","options":["A fair comparison","Twisting, because a material term was hidden to induce replacement","Defamation","Rebating"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inducing replacement through an incomplete comparison is twisting."},{"id":"H60-06","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"Priya’s bakery averaged 18 employees last year. Under Maryland law her group coverage is:","options":["Experience rated based on claims","Guaranteed available and community rated with age, tobacco, area and family factors only","Underwritten on each employee’s health","Available only through SHOP"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small employers receive guaranteed availability and modified community rating."},{"id":"H60-07","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"A covered employee’s child is born January 10 under a plan that charges a per-child premium. To keep the newborn covered beyond the first 31 days, the employee must give notice and pay by:","options":["January 10","January 31","February 10","The next open enrollment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Thirty-one days after birth is February 10."},{"id":"H60-08","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"Dennis receives his Medicare supplement policy on April 10. Under Maryland law he may return it for a full refund until:","options":["April 20","May 10","August 31","October 10"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires a 30-day right to return, so April 10 plus 30 days is May 10."},{"id":"H60-09","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"Before Gloria applies for long-term care insurance, Theo must present her with:","options":["Only a premium quote","The long-term care consumer guide, outline of coverage and personal worksheet","A Medicare supplement buyer’s guide","Her Medicaid application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Maryland requires the shopper’s guide, outline of coverage and personal worksheet at or before application."},{"id":"H60-10","lesson":"60","domain":0,"prompt":"Theo demands a hearing in writing after receiving notice of a proposed penalty. Unless the parties agree to postpone, the Commissioner must grant and hold the hearing within:","options":["10 days","30 days","90 days","One year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Section 2–210 requires the hearing within 30 consecutive days unless postponed by agreement, or a refusal."}]}]};
